window.INFO_TOPICS = [{"id":"45-overview","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">45 · Chapter 45</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Nutrition · elimination · pain</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Assessment of the GI system</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> The GI tract is one <b>25-foot tube</b> from mouth to anus, with the salivary glands, liver, gallbladder and pancreas emptying into it. Its jobs are <b>secretion, digestion, absorption, motility and elimination</b> — so every GI disease shows up as a problem with <b>nutrition, elimination or pain</b>. This chapter is the toolkit for chapters 46–49: what each part does, how to examine the abdomen in the <b>right order</b> (and when <b>not</b> to touch it), which labs point to which organ, and how to get a patient safely <b>through endoscopy and bowel prep</b>.</p><div id=\"ch45-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Food is chewed, swallowed and churned into <b>chyme</b>; acid, bile and enzymes break it down; the small intestine <b>absorbs</b>; the large intestine absorbs water and forms stool.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Structural or mechanical problems, impaired motility, infection, inflammation or autoimmune disease, and cancer anywhere along the tube or its accessory organs.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Appetite and weight change, dysphagia, heartburn, nausea and vomiting, pain (by <b>PQRST</b>), change in bowel pattern, blood or tarry stool, distention, jaundice, itching, bruising.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Inspection, auscultation, light palpation; CBC, liver enzymes, amylase and lipase, bilirubin; stool tests (FIT, gFOBT, stool DNA); x-ray, CT, MRI, ultrasound; EGD, ERCP, capsule, colonoscopy.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Treatment of the cause found; safe preparation and recovery for each test; screening from <b>age 45</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Bleeding, perforation, aspiration after sedation, pancreatitis after ERCP, dehydration and electrolyte loss from bowel prep.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A <b>pulsating abdominal mass</b> (possible aortic aneurysm — don't touch); <b>rigidity</b> (peritoneal inflammation); visible peristalsis (obstruction); severe pain, fever or a falling BP after endoscopy.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Endoscopy under moderate sedation<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>throat numbed — gag reflex gone<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>patient can't protect the airway<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>nothing by mouth until the gag reflex returns<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>then fluids, then home with a driver</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Nurse vs provider exam</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">The nurse <b>inspects, auscultates and palpates lightly</b>; the provider adds <b>deep palpation and percussion</b>. Auscultate <b>before</b> palpating so touching doesn't change bowel sounds.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">FIT vs gFOBT vs stool DNA</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>FIT yearly</b> — no diet or drug limits. <b>gFOBT yearly</b> — no NSAIDs for 7 days, no red meat or vitamin C &gt; 250 mg for 3 days. <b>Stool DNA (Cologuard) every 3 years.</b> Any positive → <b>colonoscopy</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">EGD vs colonoscopy recovery</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">EGD: <b>NPO until the gag reflex returns</b>. Colonoscopy: fluids <b>after flatus is passed</b>; lie on the <b>left side</b>; cramping and fullness for hours are expected.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Connections to other chapters</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Ch 46–47 use EGD; ch 48–49 use colonoscopy and stool tests; ch 56 diabetes patients need instructions while NPO for prep.</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Vital signs every 15–30 minutes after sedation, respiratory rate and SpO₂, gag reflex, pain, bleeding, passing flatus, intake and output during bowel prep.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Pulsating mass or aortic bruit → don't palpate, notify now. After endoscopy: severe pain, fever, bleeding or BP drop (perforation, hemorrhage). Respiratory rate &lt; 10 under sedation → stimulate (sternal rub).</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The wall</b> — four layers from the inside out: <b>mucosa</b> (thin smooth muscle and gland cells), <b>submucosa</b> (connective tissue), <b>muscularis</b> (circular and longitudinal smooth muscle that keeps contents moving), and <b>serosa</b> (connective tissue outside). The structure is the same the whole length of the tube.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Digestion and absorption</b> — food must become a liquid, <b>chyme</b>, before it can be absorbed. <b>Digestion</b> is mechanical and chemical breakdown: the stomach adds <b>hydrochloric acid</b>, the liver <b>bile</b>, the accessory organs <b>enzymes</b>. <b>Absorption</b> moves the products into the circulation.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Mouth and esophagus</b> — teeth cut, tear, crush and grind (adults have <b>32</b>); <b>saliva</b> softens food and starts digestion with <b>salivary amylase</b> (carbohydrates) and <b>lipase</b> (fats). The esophagus carries food through the diaphragm to the stomach; the <b>upper sphincter</b> stays closed to keep air out, the <b>lower esophageal sphincter (LES)</b> stays closed to keep stomach contents from refluxing — when it fails, <b>GERD</b> develops.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Stomach</b> — midline and <b>left upper quadrant</b>: cardia, fundus, body and <b>antrum (pylorus)</b>, guarded by the LES above and the <b>pyloric sphincter</b> below. <b>Parietal cells</b> make <b>hydrochloric acid</b> and <b>intrinsic factor</b> (needed to absorb <b>vitamin B₁₂</b> — without it, <b>pernicious anemia</b>); <b>chief cells</b> make <b>pepsinogen</b>. It stores, churns and mixes food into chyme. When chyme reaches the duodenum, <b>secretin and cholecystokinin</b> slow acid production and stomach motility.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Pancreas</b> — behind the stomach from the duodenal C-loop to the spleen (head, body, tail). <b>Exocrine</b>: enzymes for carbohydrate, fat and protein — <b>amylase, lipase, proteases</b>. <b>Endocrine</b>: islets — <b>alpha (glucagon), beta (insulin)</b>, delta and PP cells.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Liver and gallbladder</b> — the liver (mostly <b>RUQ</b>) gets about <b>1200 mL of blood a minute</b> from the hepatic artery and portal vein and does 400+ jobs: <b>makes bile</b>; <b>stores</b> iron, copper, vitamins A, D, E and B₁₂; <b>protects</b> (Kupffer cells engulf bacteria and old RBCs; <b>detoxifies drugs and alcohol</b> — so drug toxicity risk rises with aging); <b>metabolizes</b> — turns <b>ammonia into urea</b>, makes <b>albumin, prothrombin and fibrinogen</b>, stores and releases glycogen, handles fats. Bile flows through the common bile duct to the duodenum at the <b>sphincter of Oddi</b>; when that is closed it goes to the <b>gallbladder</b>, which <b>concentrates and stores</b> it and releases it when <b>fat</b> is eaten.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Small intestine</b> — <b>9–16 feet</b>: <b>duodenum</b> (first 8–10 inches; the bile and pancreatic ducts join at the <b>ampulla of Vater</b>), <b>jejunum</b> (~8 ft), <b>ileum</b> (~10 ft), ending at the <b>ileocecal valve</b>. <b>Villi</b> and circular folds make a huge surface — it is the <b>main organ of absorption</b>. Contents take <b>3–6 hours</b> to pass.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Large intestine</b> — <b>5–6 feet</b>: cecum (with the <b>appendix</b>, likely part of gut immunity), ascending, transverse, descending and sigmoid colon, rectum, and the last 1–1½ inches, the <b>anal canal</b> with internal and external sphincters. Slow segmental contractions allow <b>water and electrolyte absorption</b>, turning chyme into <b>feces</b>; distention triggers peristalsis toward the rectum.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Knowing which organ sits where turns vague abdominal pain into a short list. <b>RUQ</b>: liver, gallbladder, duodenum, head of pancreas. <b>LUQ</b>: stomach, spleen, body and tail of pancreas. <b>RLQ</b>: cecum and <b>appendix</b>. <b>LLQ</b>: sigmoid colon. <b>Midline</b>: the <b>aorta</b> — the one structure you must never press if it pulsates.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-gi-changes-with-aging","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"GI changes with aging","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">GI changes with aging</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Stomach</b> — <b>gastric mucosa atrophies</b> → less hydrochloric acid (<b>hypochlorhydria</b>) → <b>less iron and vitamin B₁₂ absorbed</b> and <b>bacterial overgrowth</b>, which leads to <b>atrophic gastritis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Bowel</b> — <b>peristalsis slows</b> and nerve signals dull → less urge to defecate → postponed bowel movements → <b>constipation and impaction</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Pancreas</b> — ducts dilate and vessels calcify; <b>less lipase</b> → poor fat digestion and absorption → <b>steatorrhea</b> (fatty stools).</div>\n<div><b>Liver</b> — fewer and smaller liver cells, more fibrous tissue → less protein synthesis, lower enzyme activity and cholesterol synthesis → <b>drugs metabolized slowly and can build up to toxic levels</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Gut microbiota</b> — abnormal microbial activity with age contributes to <b>anorexia and undernutrition</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Lab values in older adults (Table 45.2)</b> — <b>ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase and amylase</b> may run <b>slightly higher</b> than the adult range, and total <b>calcium slightly lower</b>. (ALT also runs slightly higher in men and African American people; AST slightly lower in females.)</div>\n<div><b>Who gets what</b> — older adults have more <b>stomach cancer</b>; younger adults more <b>inflammatory bowel disease</b>; <b>colon cancer is rising in adults under 50</b>. A recent trip abroad or <b>camping near lakes and streams</b> can explain diarrhea.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Aging moves the GI tract in one direction — <b>less</b>: less acid, less motility, less lipase, less liver enzyme. So the expected findings are slower drug clearance, constipation, fatty stools and poor B₁₂ and iron absorption — never more lipase, more frequent stools or better fat absorption. Lab ranges shift a little with age too, so a value just outside the adult range can be an older adult's normal — but a <b>critical</b> value never is.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"GI history and the abdominal exam","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">History</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Questions to ask (Box 45.1)</b> — daily food; drugs with dose and frequency; <b>vitamins, minerals, herbs</b>; appetite and <b>weight change (intended or not)</b>; special diet; <b>trouble chewing or swallowing</b>, denture fit; heartburn — how often, what triggers and relieves it; past GI disorders and surgery; <b>family history</b>; smoking or vaping, chewing tobacco; <b>alcohol</b>; pain, diarrhea, constipation or gas and the foods involved; <b>travel</b>; usual bowel pattern; <b>laxative use</b>; pain or <b>bleeding with bowel movements</b>; change in stool; past endoscopy or colonoscopy.</div>\n<div><b>Nutrition history</b> — many conditions start with intake or absorption. Ask about allergies, usual foods and meal times, <b>lactose intolerance</b> (about <b>50% of US adults</b> acquire it; more common in African American, Asian, Hispanic, Native American and Jewish people), <b>anorexia</b>, taste change (<b>COVID-19</b> can remove taste), <b>dysphagia</b>, pain with eating, nausea, vomiting, <b>dyspepsia</b> (heartburn), <b>unintentional weight loss</b> (some GI cancers), <b>alcohol and caffeine</b> (gastritis, ulcers).</div>\n<div><b>Money, culture and genes</b> — limited budgets and <b>food deserts</b> limit healthy food; people may substitute cheaper <b>OTC drugs or herbs</b> and delay care until disease is advanced. Ask about <b>spices and hot peppers</b> (indigestion) and <b>fasting</b> practices. Family history matters: <b>familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)</b> is autosomal dominant and predisposes to colon cancer.</div>\n<div><b>The current problem</b> — GI symptoms are vague, so build a <b>timeline</b> (a symptom diary helps). Bowel changes: pattern, <b>color and consistency</b>, diarrhea or constipation and what was tried, <b>frank blood or tarry stools</b>, distention, gas, unintended weight change. Weight: usual, change and when, appetite. Skin: rashes, <b>itching, jaundice, bruising or bleeding</b> (liver and biliary disease).</div>\n<div><b>Pain — PQRST</b> — <b>P</b>recipitating and palliative factors (what started it, what helps or worsens it); <b>Q</b>uality (sharp, dull, burning, crushing, twisting…); <b>R</b>egion and radiation (where, does it spread or move); <b>S</b>everity 0–10 and its effect on activity; <b>T</b>iming — onset, duration, frequency, sudden or gradual, night waking, relation to <b>meals</b>, seasons.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — elimination problems are <b>personal and embarrassing</b> — ask privately and matter-of-factly about how daily life is affected. <b>Stress</b> can trigger or worsen <b>IBS</b> and other GI disorders. A cancer diagnosis brings <b>grief</b> (denial, anger, depression) — refer for support when needed.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Examining the abdomen</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Set-up and order</b> — have the patient <b>empty the bladder</b>, lie <b>supine with knees bent</b> and arms at the sides (relaxes the abdominal muscles). Order: <b>inspect → auscultate → palpate</b> (and percuss) — so touching doesn't change bowel sounds. The generalist nurse does <b>inspection, auscultation and light palpation</b>; the provider does <b>deep palpation and percussion</b>. <b>No palpation if appendicitis or an aortic aneurysm is suspected.</b></div>\n<div><b>Inspect</b> — start at the <b>RUQ, then LUQ, LLQ, RLQ</b>; examine <b>painful areas last</b>. Note asymmetry, discoloration, scars, <b>distention</b>, bulging flanks, <b>taut glistening skin</b>, skin folds, fat, pressure injuries; contour (flat, rounded, concave, distended — look across at eye level); the umbilicus; movement with breathing. A pulsation over the aorta is occasionally visible; a <b>bulging</b> pulsating <b>mass</b> is the alarm (below).</div>\n<div><b>Organs by quadrant (Table 45.1)</b> — <b>RUQ</b>: most of the <b>liver</b>, <b>gallbladder</b>, <b>duodenum</b>, <b>head of the pancreas</b>, hepatic flexure, part of the ascending and transverse colon. <b>LUQ</b>: left lobe of the liver, <b>stomach</b>, <b>spleen</b>, <b>body and tail of the pancreas</b>, splenic flexure, part of the transverse and descending colon. <b>RLQ</b>: <b>cecum</b>, <b>appendix</b>, right ureter, right ovary and fallopian tube, right spermatic cord. <b>LLQ</b>: part of the descending colon, <b>sigmoid colon</b>, left ureter, left ovary and fallopian tube, left spermatic cord. <b>Midline</b>: <b>abdominal aorta</b>, <b>uterus</b> (if enlarged), <b>bladder</b> (if distended). Asymmetry or a finding in one area points to the structures beneath it.</div>\n<div><b>Two action alerts</b> — <b>visible peristaltic waves</b> are rare — note where they start and which way they go and <b>report them</b> (possible <b>intestinal obstruction</b>). A <b>bulging, pulsating mass</b>: <b>don't touch it</b> with hands or stethoscope — possible <b>abdominal aortic aneurysm</b> — notify the provider <b>immediately</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Auscultate</b> — with the <b>diaphragm</b> (bowel sounds are high pitched), lightly, <b>starting in the RLQ</b> (ileocecal valve), then the other quadrants. Normal: high-pitched irregular gurgles <b>5–30 per minute</b>. <b>Hypoactive or absent</b> after abdominal surgery, with <b>peritonitis</b> or <b>paralytic ileus</b>. <b>Hyperactive, loud gurgling (borborygmus)</b> with diarrhea, gastroenteritis, or <b>above a complete obstruction</b>. Listen for <b>bruits</b> over the aorta, renal and iliac arteries — an <b>aortic bruit suggests an aneurysm: don't percuss or palpate</b>, notify immediately.</div>\n<div><b>Is peristalsis back after surgery?</b> — the most reliable sign is not bowel sounds but asking: <b>flatus within the past 8 hours</b> or <b>a stool within 12–24 hours</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Light palpation</b> — four fingers together, pressing <b>½–1 inch (1.25–2.5 cm)</b> with a rotating motion, quadrant by quadrant, to find large masses and tenderness. Note <b>guarding</b> (voluntary) and <b>rigidity</b> — rigidity is involuntary and a sign of <b>peritoneal inflammation</b>. Tender areas are examined last and cautiously.</div>\n<div><b>Percussion (provider)</b> — <b>tympany</b> — high, loud, musical — over air-filled bowel; <b>dullness</b> — softer, thud-like — over solid organs such as the liver. Used to size the liver (<b>hepatomegaly</b>) and spleen (<b>splenomegaly</b>: dullness in the left anterior axillary line, before it can be felt) and to detect masses, fluid and air.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The exam order protects the findings and the patient. Listening first means the bowel sounds you hear are the patient's, not the ones your hands stirred up; examining painful areas last keeps the abdomen relaxed long enough to feel anything; and refusing to press a pulsating mass or an inflamed appendix avoids rupturing what you were trying to assess.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"GI labs and stool tests","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Blood and urine (Table 45.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>CBC and clotting</b> — a <b>CBC</b> finds anemia and infection — <b>GI bleeding is the most frequent cause of anemia in adults</b> (cancer, peptic ulcer, diverticulitis, IBD). <b>PT</b> depends on the <b>vitamin K–dependent clotting factors</b> the liver makes, so <b>liver disease prolongs PT</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Liver enzymes</b> — <b>ALT 4–36 units/L</b> and <b>AST 0–35 units/L</b> rise in most liver disorders, <b>highest with necrosis</b> such as <b>hepatitis and cirrhosis</b>. ALT: <b>significant</b> rise in hepatitis, <b>moderate</b> in cirrhosis, <b>mild</b> in pancreatitis; runs slightly <b>higher in older adults, men and African American people</b>. AST: also up with liver metastasis and <b>drug-induced liver injury</b>, down with acute kidney disease and chronic dialysis; slightly higher in older adults, lower in females. <b>Alkaline phosphatase 30–120 units/L</b> (slightly higher in older adults) rises with cirrhosis, <b>biliary obstruction</b> (inside or outside the liver) and liver tumor; it is low with hypophosphatemia and undernutrition. <b>Albumin 3.5–5.0 g/dL</b>: low in <b>liver disease and undernutrition</b>, high in dehydration.</div>\n<div><b>Pancreas</b> — <b>amylase</b> (60–120 Somogyi units/dL; 30–220 units/L) and <b>lipase (0–160 units/L)</b> rise in <b>acute pancreatitis</b> (and in chronic pancreatitis) — amylase within <b>24 hours</b>, staying up about <b>5 days</b>. They may be <b>normal when extensive pancreatic necrosis</b> leaves few cells to make enzymes. Amylase also rises with cholecystitis and perforated ulcer or perforated or necrotic bowel, and runs slightly higher in older adults; lipase rises with biliary disease, bowel obstruction and peptic ulcer. <b>Urine amylase</b> stays high <b>5–7 days</b> — useful when symptoms have lasted days.</div>\n<div><b>Bilirubin and ammonia</b> — <b>total bilirubin 0.3–1.0 mg/dL</b> (critical &gt; 12): <b>direct (conjugated) 0.1–0.3</b> rises with <b>biliary obstruction</b>, gallstones, liver metastasis; <b>indirect (unconjugated) 0.2–0.8</b> with <b>hemolysis</b>, hepatitis, cirrhosis. <b>GGT</b> also signals impaired excretion. <b>Urine urobilinogen</b> can show biliary or liver problems <b>before jaundice</b>. <b>Ammonia 10–80 mcg/dL</b> (6–47 μmol/L; the book's table prints mg/dL, a misprint) rises when the liver can't convert it to urea — <b>cirrhosis, hepatitis, hepatic encephalopathy and coma</b>, <b>portal hypertension</b>, and GI bleeding or obstruction with mild liver disease.</div>\n<div><b>Electrolytes and others</b> — <b>potassium 3.5–5.0 mEq/L</b> (critical <b>&lt; 3 or &gt; 6.1</b>) falls with <b>diarrhea, vomiting and gastric suction</b> and with ascites; sodium is lost with vomiting and diarrhea too, so both need replacing. Potassium rises with hemolysis and kidney failure. <b>Calcium 9.0–10.5 mg/dL</b> (slightly lower in older adults; critical <b>&lt; 6 or &gt; 13 mg/dL</b>) is absorbed in the gut, so it is measured to detect <b>malabsorption</b>; it falls with malabsorption, low albumin, hypoparathyroidism and pancreatitis, and rises with hyperparathyroidism and bone metastasis. Cholesterol &lt; 200 mg/dL (up with biliary obstruction and pancreatitis, down with liver damage). <b>D-xylose absorption</b> (plasma <b>20–57 mg/dL at 60 minutes</b>, <b>30–58 mg/dL at 120 minutes</b>) is low in malabsorption and short-bowel syndrome.</div>\n<div><b>Tumor markers</b> — <b>CEA (&lt; 5 ng/mL)</b> and <b>CA 19-9 (0–37 units/mL)</b> are used to <b>monitor treatment and detect recurrence</b> of GI cancers — <b>not to diagnose</b>, because benign conditions (inflammation, cirrhosis, Crohn's, pancreatitis, peptic ulcer, gallstones) also raise them. CA 19-9 rises with cancer of the pancreas, stomach, colon and gallbladder; CEA with GI, pancreatic, hepatobiliary, breast and lung cancer.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Stool tests</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Colorectal screening at home</b> — options (American Cancer Society): <b>high-sensitivity FIT every year</b>, <b>guaiac-based FOBT every year</b>, or <b>multitargeted stool DNA (mt-sDNA, e.g., Cologuard) every 3 years</b> — take-home kits, not a sample from a rectal exam. The DNA kit is <b>prescription only</b>: it is mailed to the patient after the provider orders it and mailed back in a prepaid container. It is <b>less specific than a colonoscopy</b> but appeals to people who <b>fear colonoscopy or worry about cost</b>. Covered by Medicare (no out-of-pocket cost when guidelines are met and the provider orders it) and most insurers; no bowel prep. Cost-effective, but <b>false negatives and false positives</b> occur. <b>Any positive result must be followed by a colonoscopy</b> — teach the patient to follow up promptly on results.</div>\n<div><b>gFOBT preparation</b> — guaiac reacts with more than blood, so it gives more <b>false positives</b> than FIT. <b>No NSAIDs for 7 days</b> before and during collection; <b>no red meat and no vitamin C over 250 mg/day for 3 days</b> before and during. <b>FIT has no food or drug limits</b>, so adherence is better.</div>\n<div><b>Who should get a colonoscopy instead</b> — a <b>personal or family history of colon cancer</b>, <b>inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn's or ulcerative colitis)</b>, or a <b>previous positive</b> screening test. <b>Black, American Indian and Alaska Native</b> people have higher colorectal cancer incidence — advocate screening for everyone.</div>\n<div><b>Other stool tests</b> — <b>ova and parasites</b> for parasitic infection; <b>fecal fat</b> when <b>steatorrhea</b> or malabsorption is suspected (fat should be absorbed with bile and pancreatic enzymes); infectious agents such as <b><i>Clostridium difficile</b></i> (now named <i>Clostridioides</i>) — a common cause of diarrhea in <b>older adults and after prolonged antibiotics</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A guaiac test detects the <b>peroxidase activity</b> of hemoglobin, but red meat contains animal hemoglobin and some foods and drugs interfere — so the diet and drug rules exist to stop the test from reacting to dinner instead of to a tumor. FIT uses antibodies to <b>human</b> hemoglobin, which is why it needs no preparation.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"Imaging and endoscopy — EGD, ERCP, capsule","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Imaging</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Plain film and acute abdomen series</b> — a <b>plain abdominal film</b> shows masses, strictures and <b>bowel gas patterns (obstruction)</b> — no prep except a gown and no metal. For severe pain or suspected <b>perforation</b>: an <b>acute abdomen series</b> — chest x-ray (may show a <b>hiatal hernia</b>), supine and <b>upright</b> abdominal films (<b>free air</b> under the diaphragm = perforation). Ultrasound and CT are used more often now.</div>\n<div><b>CT</b> — cross-sectional view of liver, pancreas, spleen, biliary tract. With <b>contrast</b>: ask about <b>past contrast reactions and severe drug allergies</b>, <b>NPO at least 4 hours</b>, IV access; warn of a <b>warm, flushed feeling or metallic taste</b>; a mild sedative for claustrophobia; lie still and hold breath on cue. About <b>10 minutes without contrast, 30 with</b>.</div>\n<div><b>MRI, ultrasound and others</b> — <b>MRI</b> (including <b>MRCP</b> of the bile and pancreatic ducts): screen for <b>implants</b> (magnets); longer than CT but <b>no radiation</b>. <b>Ultrasound</b>: painless, noninvasive, no radiation, little or no prep (may need fasting, depending on the organ), lie still, gel on the skin, 15–30 minutes, no aftercare. <b>Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS)</b>: through a scope — tumors of the wall, nodes, pancreas, stomach, rectum; <b>prep and aftercare as for endoscopy</b> (and ultrasound). <b>Liver-spleen scan</b>: IV radioactive tracer taken up by the liver and spleen (tumors, abscesses, size, blood flow); reassure that the dose is small and not dangerous; ask about <b>pregnancy and breastfeeding</b> (the tracer enters breast milk); about 15 minutes for uptake, then scans in several positions — lie still; eliminated in urine within <b>24 hours</b> — <b>wash hands well after toileting</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Endoscopy — general rules</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and why</b> — a flexible fiberoptic scope to see <b>bleeding, ulcers, inflammation, tumors</b>; biopsies and samples (e.g., for <b><i>Helicobacter pylori</b></i>) can be taken. Every endoscopic procedure needs <b>signed informed consent</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">EGD (esophagogastroduodenoscopy)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it can do</b> — views the <b>esophagus, stomach and duodenum</b> (called esophagoscopy or gastroscopy when it focuses on one organ — Fig. 45.5); can <b>stop bleeding</b> (clips, heat, injection, topical agents), <b>dilate a stricture</b>, view gastric lesions and support a <b>celiac</b> diagnosis.</div>\n<div><b>Before</b> — avoid <b>anticoagulants, aspirin and NSAIDs</b> for several days to a week (unless essential); usual morning meds unless told otherwise; <b>diabetes → get instructions</b> (NPO); <b>NPO 6–8 hours</b> (NPO means no food, no fluids — not even water — and <b>no smoking</b>); <b>remove dentures</b>.</div>\n<div><b>During</b> — <b>moderate sedation</b> (<b>midazolam, fentanyl or propofol</b>) — these <b>depress breathing</b>; <b>atropine</b> may dry secretions; a <b>local anesthetic spray numbs the gag reflex</b> (swallowing will be hard). Head of bed up, <b>bite block</b> in, 15–30 minutes. The nurse watches <b>respiratory rate and depth, SpO₂ and capnography</b>: <b>RR below 10 or exhaled CO₂ below 20% → stimulate with a sternal rub</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After — prevent aspiration</b> — vital signs <b>every 15–30 minutes</b> until sedation wears off; <b>side rails up</b>; <b>nothing by mouth until the gag reflex returns</b> (usually <b>30–60 minutes</b>) — the <b>priority is preventing aspiration</b>. Stop IV fluids once oral fluids stay down. Watch for <b>perforation: pain, bleeding, fever</b>. Needs <b>a driver</b>; <b>no driving for 12–18 hours</b>; a hoarse voice or sore throat can last days — <b>throat lozenges</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">ERCP (endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it does</b> — views the liver, gallbladder, <b>bile ducts and pancreatic duct</b> to find the cause of <b>obstruction</b> — now mostly <b>therapeutic</b>: a cannula in the common bile duct, <b>contrast dye</b> and x-rays; a <b>papillotomy</b> (cut in the sphincter at the ampulla of Vater) to remove <b>gallstones</b>; <b>stents</b> for strictures; biopsies.</div>\n<div><b>Before and during</b> — prepare as for EGD: <b>NPO 6–8 hours</b>, dentures out, IV for sedation; <b>medication reconciliation</b> (anticoagulants, NSAIDs, antiplatelets, antihyperglycemics — the provider decides what stops); ask about <b>contrast reactions and allergies</b> and <b>implanted devices</b> (an <b>ICD may need to be deactivated</b> for electrocautery). <b>Left lateral</b>, then <b>prone</b>; a tilt table spreads the contrast; <b>30 minutes to 2 hours</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After</b> — vital signs <b>every 15 minutes</b> until stable; <b>check the gag reflex before food or fluids</b>; stop IV fluids once oral fluids stay down without nausea or vomiting. Teach the complications that can appear <b>hours to 2 days later</b>: <b>pancreatitis, cholecystitis, infection, bleeding, perforation, biloma</b> — all bring <b>severe pain</b>; <b>fever means sepsis</b>. Colicky pain and flatulence from instilled air are expected. Report pain, fever, nausea or vomiting that doesn't resolve. Driver; no driving 12–18 hours.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Video capsule endoscopy (small bowel)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and how</b> — a swallowed camera capsule photographs the <b>whole small bowel</b> to find <b>bleeding</b> (battery ~10 hours, so not for the colon). <b>No food or drink, including water, for 12 hours before</b> and <b>for the first 2 hours</b> of the test; <b>clear liquids after 2 hours</b>, a <b>light lunch after 4 hours</b>. Sensors are placed on the abdomen and a <b>belt with a data recorder</b> is worn; swallow the capsule with water; normal activity but <b>no vigorous activity</b>; about <b>8 hours</b>, then return the equipment.</div>\n<div><b>Afterward</b> — the capsule is <b>single use</b> — it passes in the stool and is <b>discarded</b>. Report signs of <b>obstruction</b>, fever, chest pain or trouble breathing, or a capsule <b>not passed within 2 weeks</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The same three drugs used for sedation in every scope — midazolam, fentanyl, propofol — all <b>depress breathing</b>, and the throat spray removes the <b>gag reflex</b>. Put those together and the two dangers are hypoventilation during the procedure and aspiration after it, which is why the nurse watches the respiratory rate and capnography during, and withholds every sip until the gag reflex is back.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"Colonoscopy, sigmoidoscopy and colorectal screening","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Screening schedule (American Cancer Society)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>From age 45</b> — healthy adults choose one: <b>colonoscopy every 10 years</b>, <b>flexible sigmoidoscopy every 5 years</b>, <b>CT colonography (virtual colonoscopy) every 5 years</b>, or a stool test (FIT or gFOBT yearly, stool DNA every 3 years). <b>High risk</b> (family history, past polyps) → more often. <b>Young adults with obesity</b> are developing colon cancer (possibly from chronic low-grade inflammation).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Colonoscopy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it does</b> — views the <b>entire large bowel</b>; can take <b>biopsies and remove polyps</b>, find the cause of chronic diarrhea or <b>bleeding</b>, and treat bleeding.</div>\n<div><b>Preparation</b> — first-timers are often anxious — explain sedation and that pain will be controlled. <b>Stop aspirin, anticoagulants and antiplatelets</b> several days before; <b>diabetes → ask about drugs</b> for the NPO day. Bowel prep starts the <b>night before</b> (solutions taste better <b>chilled</b>); <b>clear liquids the day before</b>; <b>electrolyte or sports drinks</b> replace losses; <b>no red, orange or purple (grape) drinks or gelatin</b>; NPO for some hours before. Watery diarrhea starts about <b>an hour</b> after the prep; bisacodyl, suppositories or a small enema may be added. <b>Poor prep hides adenomas</b> and can make the test fail.</div>\n<div><b>During</b> — IV <b>moderate sedation</b> (midazolam, fentanyl, propofol); music may help but doesn't replace sedation. <b>Left side, knees drawn up</b>; air or <b>carbon dioxide</b> (less pain and distention) is instilled; <b>30–60 minutes</b>. <b>Atropine</b> is kept ready for <b>vasovagal bradycardia</b>. Watch RR, depth and SpO₂ — <b>RR &lt; 10 or exhaled CO₂ &lt; 20% → sternal rub</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After (Box 45.2)</b> — nothing by mouth until sedation wears off — <b>fluids once flatus is passed</b>; VS <b>every 15–30 minutes</b> until alert; <b>left lateral</b> position to pass gas; <b>side rails up</b>. Expected: <b>fullness, mild cramping and flatus</b> for hours; a <b>small amount of blood</b> in the first stool after biopsy or polypectomy. Watch for <b>perforation</b> (<b>severe abdominal pain and guarding</b>; fever may come later) and <b>hemorrhage or hypovolemic shock</b> (dizziness, light-headedness, <b>falling BP, tachycardia</b>, pallor or ash-gray skin, <b>altered mental status — may be the first sign in older adults</b>). Complications are uncommon, but <b>report excessive bleeding or severe pain</b> immediately. <b>Driver home</b>; <b>no driving, legal decisions or other work that needs focus</b> the rest of the day.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Virtual colonoscopy and sigmoidoscopy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Virtual colonoscopy (CT colonography)</b> — noninvasive 3-D images of the whole colon with the <b>same bowel prep and diet</b>. But a <b>polyp or bleeding found means a real colonoscopy</b> to treat it — the advantage of traditional colonoscopy is <b>diagnosis and treatment in one procedure</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Flexible sigmoidoscopy</b> — views the <b>rectum and sigmoid colon</b>: cancer screening (from 45, <b>every 5 years</b>), bleeding, inflammatory bowel disease. Prep: stop drugs the provider names (NSAIDs, anticoagulants); <b>clear liquids</b>; a laxative the night before; a <b>cleansing or Fleet enema the morning of</b>. <b>Left side, knee-chest</b>, <b>no sedation</b>, <b>5–15 minutes</b>; biopsies can't be felt. Afterward mild gas pain and flatulence; a little blood after biopsy; report heavy bleeding.</div>\n<div><b>Health literacy</b> — people vary widely in finding, understanding and using health information — and missed prep means missed screening. Have each patient <b>explain the test, prep and follow-up in their own words</b> (<b>teach-back</b>) and clarify gently until it's right.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Bowel prep works by flooding the colon with fluid the gut can't absorb, so the patient loses water and electrolytes along with stool — which is why the prep comes with electrolyte drinks, why red and purple liquids are banned (they look like blood on camera), and why diabetes medicines need a plan when the patient can't eat.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-adpie","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">For an assessment chapter the nursing process has two jobs: <b>collect a history and exam that point to the right organ</b>, and <b>get the patient safely through the test</b> that confirms it — the sedation, the bowel prep, the dye, and the hours afterward when complications show up.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>Box 45.1: diet and supplements, appetite and <b>weight change</b>, swallowing and heartburn, <b>bowel pattern and stool color</b>, <b>blood or tarry stools</b>, laxatives, <b>alcohol and tobacco</b>, travel and camping, family history (FAP, colon cancer), <b>lactose intolerance</b>, food access and culture, past scopes; pain by <b>PQRST</b>; jaundice, itching, bruising.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Exam</div><b>empty bladder, supine, knees bent</b>; <b>inspect → auscultate → light palpation</b>; RUQ → LUQ → LLQ → RLQ with painful areas last; bowel sounds starting <b>RLQ</b> (5–30/min); <b>bruits</b>; guarding vs <b>rigidity</b>; <b>never touch a pulsating mass</b>; skip palpation if appendicitis or aneurysm is suspected.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Before a test</div><b>allergies to contrast</b> and drugs; <b>anticoagulants, aspirin, NSAIDs, antiplatelets</b>; <b>diabetes drugs</b>; implanted devices (ICD, pacemaker, metal for MRI); <b>pregnancy or breastfeeding</b> (scans, contrast); dentures; NPO status; someone to <b>drive</b> them home; health literacy.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>potential for <b>aspiration</b> after sedation and a numbed throat; potential for <b>ineffective breathing</b> from sedation; potential for <b>bleeding or perforation</b> after biopsy, polypectomy, papillotomy or dilation; potential for <b>pancreatitis or sepsis</b> after ERCP; <b>fluid and electrolyte loss</b> from bowel prep; <b>anxiety</b> and embarrassment about elimination and procedures; <b>need for teaching</b> about prep and screening.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>the patient completes the prep correctly (clear returns), stays <b>free of aspiration</b>, keeps <b>RR ≥ 10 and SpO₂</b> in range during sedation, has <b>no perforation or hemorrhage</b>, goes home with a <b>driver</b>, can <b>teach back</b> the warning signs, and is screened on schedule from <b>age 45</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Before</div>informed consent; NPO as ordered (<b>6–8 h</b> EGD/ERCP, <b>4 h</b> for contrast CT, <b>12 h</b> capsule); stop anticoagulants, aspirin and NSAIDs as directed; diabetes instructions; dentures out; IV access; teach bowel prep (<b>clear liquids, no red/orange/purple</b>, electrolyte drinks, chilled solution, diarrhea starts in about an hour); explain sedation and expected sensations; ease anxiety.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">During</div>monitor <b>respiratory rate and depth, SpO₂ and capnography</b> — <b>RR &lt; 10 or exhaled CO₂ &lt; 20% → sternal rub</b>; <b>atropine</b> available for vasovagal bradycardia; bite block for EGD; left lateral positioning for colonoscopy.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">After</div>VS <b>every 15–30 minutes</b> until awake; <b>side rails up</b>; <b>EGD/ERCP: nothing by mouth until the gag reflex returns</b>; colonoscopy: <b>left side, fluids after flatus</b>; watch for <b>severe pain, guarding, fever, bleeding, falling BP and confusion</b>; stop IV fluids once oral fluids stay down; <b>driver</b>, no driving <b>12–18 hours</b>, no legal decisions that day; lozenges for sore throat; teach ERCP complications that can appear <b>up to 2 days</b> later.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>the gag reflex returned <b>before</b> any oral intake; vital signs returned to baseline; no signs of perforation, bleeding, pancreatitis or sepsis; fluids tolerated; the patient states the warning signs to report, who is driving, and when the next screening is due.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-overview","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">46 · Chapter 46</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Tissue integrity · nutrition · gas exchange · pain</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Oral cavity and esophageal conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> The mouth and esophagus start digestion and move food to the stomach, so their diseases threaten three things at once: <b>tissue integrity</b> (ulcers, lesions, burns), <b>nutrition</b> (it hurts or is hard to swallow) and the <b>airway</b> (swelling, secretions, aspiration). The exemplars are <b>stomatitis</b> for tissue integrity and <b>GERD</b> for nutrition. Around them sit oral cancer, salivary gland problems, hiatal hernia, esophageal cancer and esophageal trauma — and in every one of them the first question is <b>can this patient breathe and swallow safely?</b></p><div id=\"ch46-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Teeth and saliva begin digestion; the <b>lower esophageal sphincter (LES)</b> stays closed so stomach contents don't reflux; intact mucosa protects against infection.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Inflamed or ulcerated mucosa (stomatitis), precancerous or cancerous lesions, a weak or relaxed LES (GERD), the stomach slipping through the diaphragm (hiatal hernia), tumors, chemical burns or tears.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Mouth ulcers and white plaques; red or white patches that don't heal; heartburn and regurgitation after meals or lying down; dysphagia and odynophagia; night cough, hoarseness; weight loss.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Inspection and biopsy; EGD; <b>ambulatory pH monitoring</b> (most accurate for GERD); manometry; barium swallow (most specific for hiatal hernia); EUS, CT, MRI, PET for cancer staging.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Oral care and topical drugs; diet and lifestyle changes; antacids, H₂ blockers, PPIs; Stretta, Nissen fundoplication, LINX; surgery, chemo and radiation, dilation and stents for cancer.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Airway obstruction and aspiration pneumonia; malnutrition; Barrett's esophagus and stricture; esophageal cancer; anastomotic leak, mediastinitis and sepsis after surgery.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Stridor or choking; coughing or choking with swallowing; fever, fluid collection, tachycardia or tachypnea after esophageal surgery (leak); chest pain that may be cardiac.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">LES relaxes (fatty meal, caffeine, smoking, lying down)<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>acid refluxes into the esophagus<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>inflammation → reflux esophagitis<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>healing with Barrett's epithelium or scar<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>stricture, dysphagia, cancer risk</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Leukoplakia vs erythroplakia</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Leukoplakia</b>: thick <b>white</b> patches that <b>can't be scraped off</b>, mostly benign, linked to tobacco and chronic irritation. <b>Erythroplakia</b>: <b>red, velvety</b>, <b>precancerous</b>. Candida plaques, by contrast, <b>wipe off</b> and leave a red, sore base.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Sliding vs paraesophageal hiatal hernia</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Sliding (type I, most common)</b>: GE junction slides up — main problem is <b>reflux</b>, treated like GERD. <b>Paraesophageal (II–IV)</b>: stomach rolls up beside the esophagus — fullness, breathlessness after meals, <b>volvulus, obstruction, strangulation</b> risk → <b>surgery</b> when complications threaten (medical care is favored when possible).</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">GERD chest pain vs cardiac</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">GERD pain can radiate to the neck, jaw or back and mimic angina; it worsens bending or lying down and is relieved by upright posture, fluids or antacids. <b>Rule out the heart first</b> — stay with the frightened patient.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>PPIs</b> shut off the acid pump, so tissue heals fast — but acid helps absorb calcium, so long-term use raises <b>hip fracture</b> risk. <b>Viscous lidocaine</b> numbs the mouth — and the gag reflex — so watch for <b>burns and aspiration</b>. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Airway and breath sounds, swallowing (cough or choking with intake), oral mucosa, pain, weight and intake, NG tube drainage and placement after surgery, temperature and heart rate after esophageal surgery.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Stridor or airway swelling; choking; after esophagectomy: fever, fluid accumulation, tachycardia, tachypnea or confusion (anastomotic leak) → surgeon and Rapid Response Team.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The LES and the pressure gradient</b> — the <b>lower esophageal sphincter</b> keeps a higher pressure than the stomach so contents can't move up. Reflux happens when the LES <b>relaxes</b> (foods, drugs, smoking) or when <b>pressure in the abdomen or stomach rises</b> above it (obesity, pregnancy, tight belts, bending, large meals, ascites). An <b>NG tube holds the sphincter open</b>. Lying flat at night removes <b>gravity</b>, so acid sits on the esophagus longer.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>What lowers LES pressure (Box 46.5)</b> — <b>caffeine</b> (coffee, tea, cola), <b>chocolate</b>, <b>nitrates</b>, <b>citrus</b>, <b>tomatoes</b>, <b>alcohol</b>, <b>peppermint and spearmint</b>, <b>smoking</b>, <b>calcium channel blockers</b>, <b>anticholinergics</b>, high <b>estrogen and progesterone</b>, an <b>NG tube</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>How reflux injures</b> — acid and duodenal contents inflame the squamous lining (<b>reflux esophagitis</b>). Healing may replace it with <b>Barrett's epithelium</b> — columnar cells that resist acid but are <b>premalignant</b> — or with fibrous scar that narrows the lumen (<b>stricture</b>) → progressive dysphagia. Reflux reaching the throat and lungs causes <b>asthma, laryngitis, hoarseness, night cough, dental decay</b>, aspiration pneumonia; bleeding can occur.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why esophageal cancer spreads early</b> — the esophagus has <b>no serosa</b> to contain a tumor, and its mucosa is rich in <b>lymphatics</b> — so tumors grow quickly and reach nodes early; most are large at diagnosis and more than half metastasize. <b>Squamous cell</b> cancers arise in the upper two-thirds; <b>adenocarcinoma</b> — now the most common — at the distal end and upper stomach (where Barrett's develops).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Swallowing is a protected airway maneuver: the tongue, palate, epiglottis and a working gag reflex keep food out of the trachea. Anything in this chapter that hurts, numbs, swells or narrows the mouth and esophagus — ulcers, viscous lidocaine, a tumor, a stricture, a fresh fundoplication — weakens that protection, which is why <b>aspiration precautions</b> appear in almost every care plan here.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Stomatitis — the tissue integrity exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition</b> — inflammation in the mouth, commonly painful <b>aphthous ulcers (\"canker sores\")</b> that break the mucosa → <b>pain, bleeding and infection</b> risk. <b>Recurrent aphthous stomatitis</b> affects about <b>20%</b> of people, more often women.</div>\n<div><b>Primary vs secondary</b> — <b>Primary</b> (most common): aphthous (noninfectious), <b>herpes simplex</b>, traumatic ulcers. <b>Secondary</b>: opportunistic viruses, fungi or bacteria in people who are <b>immunocompromised</b> or on <b>chemotherapy, radiation or steroids</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Candidiasis</b> — <b><i>Candida albicans</b></i> overgrows when <b>long-term antibiotics</b> destroy normal flora: <b>white plaques</b> on the tongue, palate, pharynx and buccal mucosa that <b>wipe off to a red, sore base</b>. <b>Older adults</b> are at high risk (aging immunity, diabetes, malnutrition, stress, drugs that dry the mouth) — teach them <b>mouth care</b>, because preventing candidiasis is far easier than treating it.</div>\n<div><b>Other causes</b> — allergy; <b>deficiencies of B vitamins, folate, zinc or iron</b>; systemic disease; irritants such as <b>tobacco and alcohol</b>; foods that can trigger aphthous ulcers (coffee, potatoes, cheese, nuts, <b>citrus</b>, gluten); possibly a genetic tendency.</div>\n<div><b>Who is at risk for mouth disease</b> — tobacco or alcohol use, <b>high-sugar diets</b>, too little <b>fluoride</b>, existing oral disease, <b>poor access to dental care</b>, and systemic disease (atherosclerosis, <b>diabetes</b>, Alzheimer's).</div>\n<div><b>Prevention (Box 46.1)</b> — balanced diet and <b>water</b>; manage stress; <b>weekly self-exam</b> of the mouth; report pain, swelling or bite changes; <b>dentures that fit</b>; <b>brush and floss twice daily</b>; <b>no alcohol-containing mouthwash</b>; avoid drugs that inflame the mouth or reduce saliva; <b>regular dental visits</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Care</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Assess</b> — recent infections, nutrition, <b>oral hygiene</b>, trauma, stress; drugs, OTC and herbal products; past episodes; whether lesions affect <b>swallowing, eating or speaking</b>. Document each lesion's location, size, shape, odor, color and drainage. Lesions in the pharynx with <b>dysphagia or throat pain</b> may extend down the esophagus (swallowing studies). <b>Severe stomatitis and edema can obstruct the airway.</b> Assess coping with pain and body image. Watch <b>hydration</b>: fluid loss (for example, persistent vomiting with a climbing sodium and BUN) dries the mucosa — restore fluids (IV if needed) along with mouth care.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. <b>Impaired tissue integrity</b> from oral or esophageal lesions. 2. <b>Pain</b> from the lesions.</div>\n<div><b>Dysphagia action alert (PASS)</b> — dysphagia risks <b>airway obstruction, aspiration pneumonia and malnutrition</b>. Watch for coughing or choking when swallowing and food \"sticking\". <b>PASS</b>: is swallowing trouble <b>P</b>robable? <b>A</b>ccount for past problems; <b>S</b>creen for signs; get a <b>S</b>peech-language pathologist referral. Report and start <b>aspiration prevention</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Oral care (Box 46.2)</b> — remove dentures if the mouth is severely sore; oral hygiene <b>twice daily, after meals and as needed</b> (give the care if the patient can't) — <b>every 2 hours or more</b> if uncontrolled; <b>soft toothbrush or gauze</b>, toothpaste <b>without sodium lauryl sulfate</b>; <b>no commercial mouthwash or lemon-glycerin swabs</b>; rinse often with <b>warm saline and/or baking soda (sodium bicarbonate)</b> solution; <b>soft, bland, nonacidic</b> foods; topical analgesics or anesthetics as prescribed — <b>document their effect</b>. Oral care can be <b>delegated to AP</b> — then <b>inspect the mouth yourself</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — for infection: <b>clotrimazole troches</b>, <b>nystatin suspension (swish and spit)</b>, <b>chlorhexidine (swish and spit)</b>. For pain (\"swish and spit\"): <b>2% viscous lidocaine</b>, diphenhydramine liquid, dyclonine lozenges, aluminum-magnesium hydroxide with simethicone, attapulgite. Teach which are <b>swish and swallow, rinse only, or taken by mouth</b>; <b>finish antibiotics</b> even after feeling better. Severe pain may need <b>opioid analgesics</b> — for any prescribed pain drug, teach its side effects and <b>no driving or tasks that need concentration</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Viscous lidocaine alert</b> — it numbs the mouth and throat — the patient <b>may not feel burns from hot drinks</b>, and as sensation falls <b>aspiration risk rises</b>. Use with extreme caution.</div>\n<div><b>Comfort and recovery</b> — <b>cool or cold liquids</b> soothe; avoid hard, spicy, salty and acidic foods; <b>high-protein</b> foods for healing; vitamin C supplements (citrus may hurt). Most stomatitis is self-limiting; ongoing pain from chemo or radiation may need a support group. <b>Evaluate</b>: healthy mucosa without inflammation or infection; minimal or no pain.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Commercial mouthwash and lemon-glycerin swabs feel cleansing but work against healing: alcohol and acid <b>dry and sting</b> the mucosa, and dryness lets organisms thrive. Saline and baking-soda rinses clean without chemical irritation and help neutralize acid — which is why the evidence-based mouth-care box replaces the familiar products.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Oral tumors, oral cancer and salivary glands","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Premalignant lesions</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Erythroplakia</b> — <b>red, velvety</b> patches on the floor of the mouth, tongue, palate and mandibular mucosa — <b>precancerous</b>, and hard to tell from inflammation.</div>\n<div><b>Leukoplakia</b> — thick <b>white</b> patches firmly attached — <b>can't be scraped off</b>; slightly raised, sharply rounded. Mostly benign, but lesions on the <b>lips or tongue can become cancer</b>. Linked to <b>tobacco</b> and long-term irritation (<b>poorly fitting dentures, cheek chewing, broken teeth</b>). <b>Oral hairy leukoplakia</b> appears with immune compromise — an early sign of <b>HIV</b>, or with Epstein-Barr virus. Ask every patient about tobacco and offer counseling and treatment.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Oral cancer</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Prevention and risk</b> — <b>dentist at least twice a year</b> with an oral cancer screening (inspecting and palpating the mouth); <b>stop tobacco and alcohol</b>; protect lips from the <b>sun</b>; avoid <b>HPV</b> (sexually transmitted). <b>White men</b> have much higher HPV-related oropharyngeal cancer than other men — advocate <b>HPV vaccination and regular oral cancer screening for men</b>. Long industrial exposure to <b>polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAH)</b> — riskier still with HPV — and periodontal disease with jaw bone loss raise risk. <b>Genetics</b>: ask at-risk patients about <b>any history of cancer</b>; oral cancers often carry a <b>TP53</b> mutation (the gene for p53, a protein that regulates cell division and prevents tumors).</div>\n<div><b>Types</b> — most are <b>squamous cell carcinomas</b> of the lips, tongue, buccal mucosa and oropharynx after <b>40</b>: a <b>red, raised, eroded</b> lesion, or any lesion <b>not healed in 2 weeks</b>, or a lump in the cheek → assess further. <b>Basal cell</b> cancer on the <b>lips</b> from <b>sun</b>: a raised scab that becomes an ulcer with a <b>pearly border</b>; rarely metastasizes. <b>Kaposi sarcoma</b>: painless purple, red or brown nodule (palate, gums, tongue) — associated with <b>AIDS</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Key features (Box 46.3)</b> — mouth <b>bleeding</b>, poor appetite, <b>difficulty chewing or swallowing</b>, <b>unplanned weight loss</b>, <b>thick or absent saliva</b>, a <b>painless red, raised or eroded lesion</b>, a lump or thickening in the cheek.</div>\n<div><b>Speech, eating and self-image</b> — oral tumors and their treatment can impair <b>chewing, swallowing and speaking</b>, and pain limits daily activities and self-care. Removing the <b>tongue or part of the jaw</b>, or a <b>tracheostomy</b>, also changes appearance and speech → assess <b>self-image</b>, the <b>support system</b>, and educational, cultural and spiritual needs. The team includes a <b>speech therapist</b>, registered dietitian nutritionist, social worker and a spiritual leader if the patient wishes.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnose</b> — inspect every area with gloves, tongue blade and penlight; the provider palpates <b>cervical nodes</b>. <b>Needle or incisional biopsy</b> (excisional removes a small lesion completely); <b>CT or MRI</b> for spread and staging (MRI shows nerve involvement and tongue tumor thickness).</div>\n<div><b>Airway first</b> — <b>airway maintenance is the priority</b>. Assess dyspnea, rate and depth, <b>wheezes</b> (aspiration), <b>stridor</b> (partial obstruction). Promote <b>deep breathing</b> for an effective cough. <b>Semi- or high-Fowler</b>; fluids to thin secretions if the gag reflex is intact; chest physiotherapy; <b>oral suction with a Yankauer (tonsil tip)</b>, teaching family to suction. <b>Steroids</b> for edema, antibiotics for infection, <b>cool mist face tent</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Aspiration precautions</b> — check <b>LOC, gag reflex (before any fluids) and swallowing</b>; upright; suction at the bedside; AP feed <b>small amounts</b>; family and visitors <b>ask the nurse before giving food or drink</b>; <b>thickened liquids</b> and a swallow study with the <b>SLP</b> as needed.</div>\n<div><b>Oral care</b> — every <b>2 hours</b>; <b>soft-bristle or ultrasoft \"chemobrush\"</b> (especially with low platelets); <b>no foam swabs</b> (don't remove plaque, dry the mouth); <b>water-based lubricant</b> for lips and mucosa; <b>no alcohol mouthwash or lemon-glycerin</b>; rinse with <b>baking soda or warm saline</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — excision, radiation, cryosurgery, chemotherapy and targeted therapy, often combined (multimodal); <b>Mohs surgery</b> for basal cell cancer of the lip. Surgeries include dental extraction before radiation, <b>glossectomy</b> (tongue), <b>gastrostomy tube</b>, <b>laryngectomy</b>, mandible or maxilla resection, microsurgical or flap reconstruction, <b>neck dissection</b>, <b>tracheostomy</b>, tumor resection with margins. Good <b>oral care before</b> surgery lowers complications. After a small excision: <b>liquids for a day</b>, then advance; keep follow-up.</div>\n<div><b>At home (Box 46.4)</b> — <b>inspect the mouth daily</b>; meticulous oral hygiene with an <b>ultrasoft brush</b> cleaned after each use; follow-up visits; <b>thickened liquids</b> for dysphagia; soft foods for stomatitis; <b>saliva substitute</b> as prescribed.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Sialadenitis and xerostomia</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Sialadenitis</b> — inflammation of a <b>salivary gland</b> — usually the <b>parotid or submandibular</b> — from bacteria or viruses (often CMV), <b>immune compromise</b> (HIV, Sjögren syndrome), <b>less saliva</b> (radiation for head and neck or thyroid cancer), or drugs (<b>phenothiazines, tetracyclines</b>). Can become chronic; an untreated abscess can rupture into the <b>neck and mediastinum</b>. Best prevention: <b>routine oral hygiene</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Assess and treat</b> — dryness, pain, <b>facial swelling</b> over the gland, pus that can be massaged out; check <b>facial nerve (CN VII)</b> function; fever and malaise. Treat the cause and <b>increase saliva flow</b>: <b>hydration</b>, <b>moist heat</b>, <b>massage along the gland</b>, NSAIDs, antibiotics if infected, <b>sialagogues</b> (saliva stimulants).</div>\n<div><b>Xerostomia</b> — radiation (including <b>radioactive iodine</b>) dries the glands within <b>24 hours</b> → <b>severe dry mouth</b>, temporary or permanent depending on dose. Best help: <b>frequent sips of water</b> and <b>frequent mouth care, especially before meals</b>; <b>saliva substitutes</b> after radiation ends.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Saliva is not just moisture: it washes the teeth, buffers acid, carries antibacterial proteins and lets food form a bolus that can be swallowed. Lose it to radiation or drugs and the mouth becomes a place where bacteria climb into the salivary ducts, teeth decay, and food sticks — so the treatments all aim to <b>bring saliva back</b> or replace it.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"GERD — the nutrition exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition</b> — <b>gastroesophageal reflux (GER)</b> is backward flow of stomach contents (<b>regurgitation</b>); <b>GERD</b> is the chronic, more serious disease from <b>persistent</b> reflux. The <b>most common upper GI disorder</b> in the US, affecting <b>18–28%</b> of North American adults — most often middle-aged and older adults, but increasingly <b>younger</b> adults, so don't rule it out by age. It may cause <b>no symptoms at first</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — <b>overweight and obesity</b> (raised abdominal pressure) are the biggest; <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> (gastritis → slow emptying); <b>NG tubes</b>; pregnancy, tight belts or binders, bending over, ascites; <b>obstructive sleep apnea</b> (night reflux); strong genetic link plus lifestyle.</div>\n<div><b>Complications</b> — <b>reflux esophagitis</b>; <b>Barrett's esophagus</b> (premalignant); <b>stricture</b> with progressive dysphagia; <b>asthma, laryngitis, dental decay, cardiac disease</b>; <b>hemorrhage</b>; <b>aspiration pneumonia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — recognition and treatment lag for <b>Hispanic and Latino Americans</b>, especially when they aren't proficient in English and providers don't speak Spanish — advocate for <b>professional medical translators (interpreters)</b> for every English language learner.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Ask</b> — heartburn or <b>atypical chest pain</b>; new <b>asthma</b>, <b>morning hoarseness</b>, <b>coughing or wheezing at night</b> (reflux reaching the pharynx or lungs); <b>dysphagia</b> and <b>odynophagia</b> (painful swallowing).</div>\n<div><b>Key features (Box 46.6)</b> — the main symptoms are <b>dyspepsia</b> (indigestion) and <b>regurgitation</b> (can cause aspiration or bronchitis). Also <b>water brash</b> (sudden salivation — fluid in the throat <b>without</b> a sour taste), <b>pyrosis</b> (heartburn), epigastric or general abdominal pain, chest pain, <b>globus</b> (something in the throat), pharyngitis, <b>dental caries</b>, belching, flatulence, nausea, bloating. Severe GERD: discomfort after <b>every meal for 20 minutes to 2 hours</b>, <b>worse lying down, bending or straining</b>, <b>relieved by fluids, antacids or sitting up</b>. Severe pain may radiate to the <b>neck, jaw or back like cardiac pain</b>. Listen for <b>crackles</b> (aspiration).</div>\n<div><b>The frightened patient</b> — many come to the ED <b>fearing a heart attack</b> — stay with them until the diagnosis is clear and support coping.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — often <b>atypical</b>: <b>dysphagia, vomiting, anorexia, anemia, cough, respiratory problems</b> instead of heartburn; more complications (comorbidity, polypharmacy) → report so a GERD workup can be considered.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis</b> — usually from <b>symptoms and history</b>. <b>EGD</b> (with biopsy, moderate sedation, needs a driver) for atypical symptoms. <b>Ambulatory esophageal pH monitoring is the most accurate</b>: a nasal catheter or wireless capsule on the distal esophagus records pH for <b>24–48 hours</b> while the patient keeps a <b>diary</b> of activities and symptoms. <b>Manometry</b> (pressure of the LES and peristalsis) can't diagnose GERD alone — it checks function <b>before surgery</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. <b>Potential for compromised nutrition</b> from food choices. 2. <b>Acute pain</b> from reflux.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every GERD symptom follows gravity and pressure. Bending, straining, a big meal and lying flat all push stomach contents up against a weak sphincter; sitting up, a small meal and an antacid all reverse it. That is why the most effective treatments are not drugs at all but <b>positions, portions and timing</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Treating GERD — diet, lifestyle, drugs, procedures","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nutrition and lifestyle</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Food choices</b> — GERD is <b>chronic</b> — teaching is the nurse's most important role (treat it more aggressively in older adults). Work with the <b>RDN</b>. <b>Avoid foods that lower LES pressure or irritate</b>: <b>peppermint, chocolate, fatty (especially fried) foods, caffeine, carbonated drinks</b>; limit <b>spicy and acidic</b> foods (orange juice, tomatoes) until healed. Diet apps can help.</div>\n<div><b>Meals and sleep</b> — <b>4–6 small meals</b> instead of 3 large ones (large meals raise stomach pressure and slow emptying); <b>eat slowly and chew well</b> (less belching); <b>nothing within 3 hours of bedtime</b> (night reflux does the most damage, and regurgitation lying down risks <b>aspiration</b>); <b>sleep propped up</b> — <b>blocks under the head of the bed or a wedge pillow</b>, not just extra pillows.</div>\n<div><b>Habits</b> — <b>no alcohol or tobacco</b> (refer to cessation programs); <b>weight loss</b> if needed; <b>upright at least 1 hour after eating</b>; no <b>heavy lifting, straining or working bent over</b>; <b>loose, nonrestrictive clothing</b>. People with obesity often have sleep apnea too — <b>CPAP</b> reduces night reflux. These habits work and can bring prompt relief in uncomplicated cases. Teach the warning signs of complications such as <b>stricture</b> (worsening dysphagia) and <b>Barrett's esophagus</b>; an RDN or support group can help with meal planning.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Drug therapy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Drugs that cause reflux</b> — review with the provider: <b>oral contraceptives, anticholinergics, sedatives, NSAIDs, nitrates, calcium channel blockers</b> all lower LES pressure.</div>\n<div><b>The three classes</b> — <b>antacids</b>, <b>histamine (H₂) blockers</b> and <b>proton pump inhibitors (PPIs)</b> — the same drugs used for peptic ulcer disease (Table 47.1). They <b>inhibit acid secretion, speed stomach emptying, or protect the mucosa</b>. IV <b>esomeprazole or pantoprazole</b> prevent <b>stress ulcers</b> after surgery. PPIs heal tissue fast but symptoms <b>recur</b> when stopped; long-term use can <b>mask</b> reflux symptoms, and stopping the drug shows whether reflux has resolved.</div>\n<div><b>Long-term PPI cautions</b> — linked to kidney, liver and heart disease, dementia, GI tumors, <b>poor nutrient absorption</b>, and more respiratory and GI infections — monitor long-term users. In <b>older adults</b>, PPIs reduce <b>calcium absorption</b> (and protein digestion) → brittle bones → <b>hip fracture</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Procedures and surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Stretta procedure</b> — nonsurgical: <b>radiofrequency energy</b> through an endoscope near the GE junction <b>reduces vagus nerve activity</b>. Patients with obesity or severe symptoms may not be candidates; it can replace surgery when other measures fail. Afterward (Box 46.7): <b>clear liquids for 24 hours</b>, then <b>soft diet</b>; <b>no NSAIDs or aspirin for 10 days</b>; keep taking PPIs; <b>liquid medicines</b> when possible; <b>no NG tube for at least 1 month</b> (perforation); report abdominal or <b>chest pain, bleeding, dysphagia, nausea or vomiting, shortness of breath</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication (LNF)</b> — the minimally invasive <b>standard surgery</b> for severe GERD — only a small share of patients need it: reflux that fails medical treatment, high-volume reflux, drug intolerance or nonadherence, a <b>large hiatal hernia</b>, sometimes reflux-related respiratory symptoms. The <b>fundus is wrapped around the lower esophagus</b>. Keep following the antireflux diet and antacids — <b>recurrence is common</b>. (Care is in the hiatal hernia topic.)</div>\n<div><b>LINX</b> — a ring of <b>magnets</b> around the LES raises closing pressure but lets food through — for typical GERD with an abnormal pH study and partial PPI response, without hiatal hernia or severe esophagitis. <b>Action alert</b>: tell <b>every provider</b>; <b>older LINX devices must never go in an MRI</b>; newer \"MR Conditional\" ones only under set conditions.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>PPIs block the final step of acid making — the proton pump in every parietal cell — which is why they heal esophagitis faster than any other drug. But stomach acid also frees calcium, iron and B₁₂ from food and kills swallowed microbes; switch it off for years and the bill comes due as fractures, deficiencies and infections.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Hiatal hernia and fundoplication care","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hiatal (diaphragmatic) hernia</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Types</b> — the stomach pushes through the <b>esophageal hiatus</b> of the diaphragm into the chest. <b>Type I — sliding</b> (most common): the GE junction and part of the fundus slide up and down with position and pressure; main concern is <b>reflux</b> (volvulus and obstruction are rare). <b>Types II–IV — paraesophageal (rolling)</b>: II, the fundus rolls up <b>beside</b> the esophagus while the GE junction stays put; III, both herniate; IV, colon, spleen, pancreas or small bowel in the sac. High risk of <b>volvulus, obstruction and strangulation</b>; slow venous oozing causes <b>iron deficiency anemia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Key features (Box 46.8)</b> — <b>sliding</b>: heartburn, regurgitation, chest pain, dysphagia, belching. <b>Paraesophageal</b>: <b>fullness, breathlessness or a feeling of suffocation after eating</b>, chest pain that <b>mimics angina</b>, worse <b>lying down</b>. <b>Most</b> are asymptomatic; when symptoms occur they usually worsen <b>after meals or lying flat</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnose</b> — <b>barium swallow with fluoroscopy is the most specific</b> (sliding hernias show with position changes); <b>EGD</b> to view the esophagus and gastric lining; high-resolution manometry for larger sliding hernias.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — <b>sliding</b>: like GERD — <b>antacids or PPIs</b>, diet and lifestyle (teaching is the key nursing action). <b>Paraesophageal</b>: depends on type, symptoms and risk — medical management is favored when possible; <b>surgery</b> for volvulus, bleeding, obstruction, strangulation, perforation or respiratory compromise. Before elective surgery: <b>lose weight</b> if overweight, <b>quit smoking</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">After fundoplication</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The operation</b> — the fundus is <b>wrapped around the distal esophagus</b> and sutured to anchor it <b>below the diaphragm</b> — open (abdominal or thoracic) or <b>laparoscopic (LNF, fewer complications)</b>. A <b>thoracic approach</b> brings a <b>chest tube and NG tube</b> for several days.</div>\n<div><b>After LNF (Box 46.9)</b> — bleeding and infection are possible but uncommon — watch for them, and teach: <b>soft diet about a week</b> (no carbonated drinks, tough foods, raw vegetables); <b>antireflux drugs at least a month</b>; <b>no driving for a week</b> or on opioids; <b>walk daily, no heavy lifting</b>; remove small dressings and <b>shower at 2 days</b>, leave closure strips until <b>day 10</b>; wash incisions with soap and water, report redness or drainage; report <b>fever &gt; 101°F (38.3°C)</b> — <b>&gt; 100°F (37.8°C) if older than 65</b> — nausea, vomiting, uncontrollable bloating or pain; follow-up in <b>3–4 weeks</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After open repair — lungs first</b> — the priority is <b>preventing respiratory complications</b> (large hernias are highest risk): <b>HOB at least 30°</b>, early <b>ambulation</b>, <b>splint the incision</b> to cough, incentive spirometry and deep breathing, VTE prevention.</div>\n<div><b>The NG tube</b> — a <b>large-bore NG tube</b> keeps the wrap from becoming too tight. Drainage is <b>dark brown (old blood) at first</b>, then <b>yellowish green within 8 hours</b>. Check placement every <b>4–8 hours</b> and <b>anchor it well</b> — <b>reinserting it could perforate the fundoplication</b>. Keep it <b>patent</b> so the stomach stays decompressed (vomiting or retching can rupture the sutures). Frequent <b>oral care</b>; accurate I&amp;O.</div>\n<div><b>Complications (Box 46.10)</b> — <b>temporary dysphagia</b> when eating starts (<b>persistent</b> dysphagia = wrap too tight → may need dilation); <b>gas bloat syndrome</b> (can't belch to relieve distention); <b>atelectasis or pneumonia</b> (dyspnea, chest pain, fever); <b>obstructed NG tube</b> (nausea, vomiting, distention, no drainage).</div>\n<div><b>Eating again and gas</b> — clear fluids, then a near-normal diet over <b>4–6 weeks</b>; <b>smaller, more frequent meals</b> (smaller stomach reservoir); limit <b>caffeine, carbonated drinks, alcohol</b>; <b>supervise the first oral feedings</b>. For <b>aerophagia</b> and <b>gas bloat</b>: relax before meals, eat and drink slowly, chew well; <b>no carbonated drinks, gas-producing or high-fat foods, gum or straws</b>; move and walk often; <b>simethicone</b> if needed.</div>\n<div><b>Going home</b> — activity limits <b>3–6 weeks</b> (open) or about <b>1 week</b> (laparoscopic); diet changes; <b>stool softeners or bulk laxatives</b> (no straining); <b>inspect the incision daily</b>; report swelling, redness, tenderness, discharge or fever; <b>avoid people with respiratory infections</b> — coughing can make the incision or wrap <b>dehisce</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A fundoplication is a sutured cuff around the esophagus, and everything after surgery protects it: the large NG tube sizes the wrap and keeps the stomach empty so retching can't tear it, the tube is never blindly reinserted because the tip could pierce it, and coughing, straining and vomiting are prevented because each is a sudden pressure spike against fresh sutures.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Esophageal cancer and esophagectomy","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The disease</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — <b>alcohol</b>, <b>smoking</b>, diets low in <b>fresh fruits and vegetables</b>, foods high in <b>nitrates and nitrosamines</b> (pickled, fermented), <b>malnutrition</b>, <b>obesity</b>, <b>untreated GERD</b>. <b>Men</b> have higher incidence and mortality regardless of race.</div>\n<div><b>Key features (Box 46.11)</b> — silent early. The most common feature is <b>persistent, progressive dysphagia</b> — often not until the lumen narrows — with <b>weight loss, often more than 20 lb</b> over months. Also food <b>sticking</b>, <b>odynophagia</b>, <b>halitosis</b>, chronic <b>hiccups</b>, chronic <b>cough</b> with more secretions, <b>hoarseness</b>, severe persistent chest or abdominal pain, anorexia, regurgitation, nausea and vomiting, bowel changes.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnose and support</b> — <b>EGD with biopsy</b>; staging, often by <b>endoscopic ultrasound</b>; <b>PET</b> finds metastases more accurately than CT and tracks chemotherapy response. The diagnosis brings <b>anxiety</b>, fear of <b>choking at meals</b>, lost pleasure in eating — assess coping and support; refer to counseling, pastoral care, social work; plan end-of-life care when needed.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Without surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Nutrition and swallowing</b> — RDN sets calorie needs; <b>daily weight before breakfast, same scale</b>; <b>upright for several hours after meals, never flat</b>; <b>HOB at least 30°</b>; <b>semisoft foods and thickened liquids</b>; record intake; liquid supplements between meals; <b>enteral feeding</b> if dysphagia is severe; <b>gastrostomy or jejunostomy</b> for complete obstruction or fistula. <b>SLP</b> swallowing therapy; <b>OT</b> feeding techniques. <b>Critical rescue</b>: watch every meal for <b>aspiration</b> (airway obstruction, pneumonia) and teach caregivers to feed safely and respond to choking.</div>\n<div><b>Cancer treatments</b> — chemotherapy (often before surgery), radiation, or <b>chemoradiation</b>; <b>photodynamic therapy</b> with <b>porfimer sodium</b> (light-sensitive drug) as palliation; <b>esophageal dilation</b> for quick but temporary relief (<b>perforation</b> risk); <b>stents</b> (metal, plastic or biodegradable) to keep it open longer — palliation for patients who can't have surgery; a covered stent can seal a perforation (bacteremia → <b>antibiotics</b> to prevent sepsis and endocarditis; pain, <b>stent migration</b>, recurrent dysphagia); <b>trastuzumab</b> (HER2 target) IV every 3 weeks.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Esophagectomy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Before</b> — removal of all or part of the esophagus — <b>minimally invasive (MIE)</b> for early cancer, but most need open surgery. <b>Stop smoking 2–4 weeks before</b>; <b>respiratory rehab</b> and <b>nutrition support</b> (oral, tube or parenteral) reduce lung complications — teach the patient and caregiver to track <b>weight and I&amp;O</b>; treat dental disease; <b>oral care 4 times a day</b>; teaching and emotional support; <b>prophylactic antibiotics</b> and oxygen on the day.</div>\n<div><b>Lungs first</b> — <b>respiratory care is the highest priority</b>. After open surgery: <b>mechanical ventilation for at least 16–24 hours</b>. Then <b>turn, cough and deep breathe every 1–2 hours</b>, check breath sounds and dyspnea every 1–2 hours, <b>splint the incision</b>, give adequate analgesia. Pre-op radiation raises the risk of <b>atelectasis and pneumonia</b>. Keep the patient <b>semi- or high-Fowler</b>; keep the <b>chest tube</b> patent and watch its drainage.</div>\n<div><b>Heart and fluids</b> — <b>hypotension</b> from pressure on the back of the heart; <b>fluid overload</b> (older adults, node dissection) — edema, <b>crackles</b>, raised JVP; ICU hemodynamic monitoring every 2 hours; <b>atrial fibrillation</b> from vagus nerve irritation.</div>\n<div><b>Anastomotic leak</b> — wound infection can appear at <b>4–5 days</b>; a <b>leak at the anastomosis</b> — the dreaded complication — at <b>2–10 days</b> → <b>stop all oral intake</b> until healed; <b>mediastinitis</b> from a leak can cause <b>fatal sepsis</b>. <b>Critical rescue</b>: <b>fever, fluid accumulation, signs of inflammation, early shock (tachycardia, tachypnea, altered mental status)</b> → report <b>immediately to the surgeon and Rapid Response Team</b>.</div>\n<div><b>NG tube after esophageal surgery (Box 46.12)</b> — decompresses the stomach so the suture line isn't strained. Check placement <b>every 4–8 hours</b>; drainage <b>bloody → yellowish green by the end of day 1</b>; <b>secure it well</b>; <b>never irrigate or reposition without an order</b>; <b>oral and nasal care every 2–4 hours</b>; <b>HOB at least 30°</b>; first sips of water <b>upright</b> — watch for dysphagia; watch for leak signs.</div>\n<div><b>Feeding after surgery</b> — <b>jejunostomy (J-tube) feedings start on day 2</b> — <b>don't aspirate for residual</b> (tears the mucosa) — increased slowly through day 5. <b>Barium swallow on day 7</b>: no leak → NG tube out. <b>Minimal liquid diet for 2 weeks</b>, then as the surgeon orders.</div>\n<div><b>At home</b> — keep up <b>walking and the incentive spirometer</b>; avoid bed rest. <b>Small, frequent, high-calorie, high-protein, soft</b> meals and <b>supplement shakes</b>; <b>sit upright to eat and stay upright</b> after. Patients can lose up to <b>10% of body weight</b> — <b>report a loss of 5 lb or more in a month</b>; home <b>tube feeding or parenteral nutrition</b> if oral intake falls short. <b>Handwashing</b>, avoid sick people and crowds, report respiratory infection. Report returning <b>dysphagia or odynophagia</b> (stricture, reflux, recurrence). Focus on <b>quality of life</b>; plan hospice when needed. A rehab program of <b>brisk walking and diet education</b> improved reflux, sleep, mood and quality of life.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>After an esophagectomy the stomach is pulled up into the chest and joined to what remains of the esophagus, so the suture line sits next to the lungs, heart and mediastinum. A leak spills swallowed saliva and gastric juice into the chest — that is why nothing goes by mouth until a barium swallow proves the join is sealed, and why fever with tachycardia after day 2 is treated as a leak until proven otherwise.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-esophageal-trauma","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Esophageal trauma","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Esophageal trauma</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Causes</b> — blunt injury, <b>chemical burns</b>, surgery or endoscopy (rare), or <b>continuous severe vomiting</b>. Perforation: <b>straining, seizures, trauma, foreign objects, instruments or tubes</b>, complications of esophageal surgery, ulcers, chemicals. A tear lets <b>caustic acid into the mediastinum</b> → high mortality from <b>shock, respiratory failure or sepsis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Acid vs alkali</b> — caustic ingestion (accidental or intentional) damages the mouth and esophagus fast. <b>Acids burn the superficial lining</b>; <b>alkalis penetrate deeper</b> — strong alkalis can <b>perforate the esophagus within 1 minute</b>. Later: aspiration pneumonia, hemorrhage, and <b>strictures</b> as scar forms.</div>\n<div><b>Assess</b> — in the ED: <b>airway, breathing</b>, chest pain, <b>dysphagia</b>, vomiting, bleeding; the circumstances. Endoscopy if it won't extend the damage; <b>chest CT for mediastinal air</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Treat</b> — <b>NPO</b> to stop further leakage; esophageal and gastric <b>suction</b>; <b>esophageal rest for more than a week</b>; <b>TPN</b> for calories and protein; <b>broad-spectrum antibiotics</b> (sepsis); <b>high-dose corticosteroids</b> (inflammation, prevent <b>strictures</b>); opioid and nonopioid analgesics; <b>viscous lidocaine</b> for mouth burns. Surgery if healing fails — resection with <b>gastric pull-through</b> or a bowel segment; a <b>G-tube</b> for nutrition while healing.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Acids cause coagulation of the surface proteins, forming a crust that limits how deep they go; alkalis dissolve proteins and fats (liquefaction) and keep burning downward — the same chemistry that makes drain cleaner work. That is why a swallowed alkali is the more dangerous injury, perforating in minutes.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-adpie","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Every patient in this chapter is assessed and protected in the same order: <b>airway and swallowing first</b>, then <b>tissue integrity and pain</b>, then <b>nutrition</b> — because a sore mouth, a numbed throat, a narrowed esophagus or a fresh suture line can all turn a meal into an aspiration.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Mouth</div>oral hygiene habits, dentures and fit, tobacco and alcohol, recent infections, nutrition, stress, drugs (antibiotics, chemo, steroids, drying drugs); inspect with gloves, tongue blade and penlight: <b>ulcers, white plaques (wipe off?)</b>, red or white patches, <b>lesions not healed in 2 weeks</b>, bleeding, dryness, swelling; effect on <b>eating, swallowing and speaking</b>; body image.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Swallowing and airway</div><b>coughing or choking</b> with swallowing, food sticking, <b>dysphagia and odynophagia</b>, <b>stridor</b>, wheezes or <b>crackles</b> (aspiration), secretions, gag reflex and LOC; use <b>PASS</b> and involve the <b>SLP</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Esophagus</div><b>heartburn and regurgitation</b> — timing with meals, lying down and bending; <b>atypical chest pain</b>, night cough, hoarseness, new asthma; foods, drugs and habits that lower LES pressure; weight and BMI; <b>weight loss</b> and progressive dysphagia (cancer); older adults' atypical signs (anemia, anorexia, vomiting, cough).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>stomatitis: <b>impaired tissue integrity</b> and <b>pain</b>. GERD: <b>potential for compromised nutrition</b> and <b>acute pain</b>. Oral and esophageal cancer and surgery: <b>airway obstruction and aspiration</b>, <b>malnutrition</b>, anxiety and grief. After esophagectomy or fundoplication: <b>respiratory complications</b>, <b>anastomotic leak</b>, hypotension, fluid overload and dysrhythmia.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>healthy oral mucosa without inflammation or infection and with minimal pain; <b>no aspiration</b>; the patient follows diet, drug and lifestyle changes that reduce reflux and pain; weight stable or rising; after surgery, <b>clear lungs</b>, no leak, and a diet progressing as ordered.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Oral care and comfort</div>oral care twice daily and after meals (<b>every 2 hours</b> if uncontrolled) with a <b>soft or ultrasoft brush</b>, <b>no alcohol mouthwash, lemon-glycerin or foam swabs</b>; <b>saline or baking soda rinses</b>; soft, bland, cool foods; <b>nystatin, clotrimazole, chlorhexidine</b> as ordered; viscous lidocaine with <b>burn and aspiration</b> precautions; delegate oral care to AP and <b>inspect afterward</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Airway and swallowing</div><b>semi- or high-Fowler</b>; <b>suction (Yankauer)</b> at the bedside; check <b>gag reflex before fluids</b>; small bites, <b>thickened liquids</b>; visitors ask before feeding; steroids and cool mist for edema; <b>SLP</b> swallow evaluation.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">GERD teaching</div><b>4–6 small meals</b>; avoid <b>peppermint, chocolate, fatty food, caffeine, carbonated drinks, alcohol and tobacco</b>; <b>no eating 3 hours before bed</b>; <b>HOB on blocks or a wedge</b>; <b>upright an hour after meals</b>; no straining, lifting or bending; loose clothes; weight loss; antacids, <b>H₂ blockers or PPIs</b> as ordered (PPI fracture risk in older adults); LINX → <b>MRI alert</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">After surgery</div>fundoplication: <b>HOB ≥ 30°</b>, early walking, splint and cough; <b>anchor the NG tube</b> (never reinsert blindly), keep it patent; supervise first feedings; gas-bloat teaching. Esophagectomy: ventilator then <b>TCDB every 1–2 hours</b>; watch BP, fluid overload and <b>A-fib</b>; <b>don't irrigate or move the NG tube</b>; J-tube feeds without residual checks; <b>fever, fluid collection, tachycardia or tachypnea → surgeon and Rapid Response</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>mouth healing and pain controlled; swallowing safe with no coughing or choking and clear lung sounds; reflux symptoms reduced with diet, lifestyle and drugs; weight stable; after surgery, lungs clear, NG drainage as expected, no signs of leak; the patient can state which drugs to swish and spit, the GERD rules, and the symptoms to report (dysphagia, chest pain, fever, weight loss).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-overview","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">47 · Chapter 47</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Infection · inflammation · nutrition · pain</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Stomach conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> The stomach survives its own acid only because of a <b>protective mucosal barrier</b> kept up by <b>prostaglandins</b>. Break the barrier — with <b>NSAIDs</b>, alcohol, smoking or <b><i>Helicobacter pylori</b></i> — and acid and pepsin injure the lining: <b>gastritis</b> if it is inflamed, <b>peptic ulcer disease (PUD)</b> — the infection exemplar — if it is eroded through. The emergencies are the same in both: <b>upper GI bleeding</b>, <b>perforation</b> with peritonitis, and <b>gastric outlet obstruction</b>. The chapter ends with <b>gastric cancer</b> and the life after a gastrectomy, including <b>dumping syndrome</b>.</p><div id=\"ch47-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Parietal cells make acid and intrinsic factor; prostaglandins keep the mucus-bicarbonate barrier and blood flow that stop the stomach digesting itself.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>NSAIDs</b> block prostaglandins; <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> burrows under the mucus and neutralizes acid around itself while inflammation damages the lining; acid back-diffuses → edema, bleeding, erosion, ulcer.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Epigastric burning or gnawing pain (dyspepsia), nausea, early fullness, anorexia; <b>hematemesis</b> (red or coffee-ground) and <b>melena</b>; dizziness on standing; a rigid board-like abdomen if perforated.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>EGD with biopsy</b> (gold standard; rapid urease test); <i>H. pylori</i> serology, <b>urea breath test</b>, <b>stool antigen</b>; Hgb/Hct; stool occult blood; nuclear bleeding scan.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Remove the cause; <b>PPI-based triple or quadruple therapy</b> for <i>H. pylori</i>; PPIs, H₂ blockers, antacids, sucralfate; endoscopic hemostasis; surgery for perforation or bleeding that won't stop.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hemorrhage (most serious), perforation with peritonitis and sepsis, pyloric obstruction with metabolic alkalosis, pernicious anemia, gastric cancer.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hematemesis or melena with <b>falling BP, rising pulse</b>, confusion; <b>sudden severe epigastric pain spreading over the abdomen</b> with a rigid abdomen; persistent vomiting.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">NSAID or <i>H. pylori</i><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>prostaglandin barrier fails<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>acid and pepsin reach the mucosa<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>ulcer erodes into a vessel or through the wall<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>bleeding or perforation</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Gastric vs duodenal ulcer (Table 47.2)</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Gastric</b>: age 50–70, normal or low acid, pain <b>30–60 minutes after a meal</b>, <b>worsened by food</b>, rarely at night, more <b>hematemesis</b>, slightly higher cancer risk. <b>Duodenal</b>: age 20–50, more men, type O, <b>acid hypersecretion</b>, pain <b>1½–3 hours after meals</b> and <b>1–2 a.m.</b>, <b>relieved by food</b>, more <b>melena</b>, recurs often.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Early vs late dumping</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Early</b> (within ~20 min): fluid rushes into the jejunum → <b>vertigo, tachycardia, sweating, pallor, palpitations, syncope, wanting to lie down</b>. <b>Late</b> (1–3 h): sugar spike → <b>too much insulin</b> → <b>hypoglycemia</b> symptoms (dizziness, sweating, confusion).</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Aluminum vs magnesium antacids</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Aluminum → <b>constipation</b>, safe in kidney disease (binds phosphate). Magnesium → <b>diarrhea</b>, <b>avoid in kidney disease and heart failure</b> (hypermagnesemia). Combined, they cancel each other out.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">PPIs need food to switch on the pump they block → <b>30 minutes before the main meal</b>. Antacids bind other drugs → <b>space them 1–2 hours apart</b>. Bismuth is a salicylate → <b>no aspirin</b>, and it <b>blackens stool and tongue</b> harmlessly. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Vital signs and orthostatics, hemoglobin and hematocrit, stool and vomitus for blood, pain character, abdomen for rigidity, NG drainage, intake and output, electrolytes (K⁺, Na⁺) with vomiting or suction.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Upper GI bleeding → <b>ABCs, oxygen, two large-bore IVs</b>, isotonic fluids, labs, type and crossmatch. Sudden severe pain with a board-like abdomen → perforation (surgical emergency). After gastric surgery: a blocked NG tube with pain, fullness, hiccups, tachycardia and hypotension (acute gastric dilation).</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The mucosal barrier</b> — <b>prostaglandins</b> maintain the protective barrier (mucus, bicarbonate, mucosal blood flow) that keeps the stomach from digesting itself. When the barrier is disrupted, <b>histamine release</b> and <b>vagal stimulation</b> worsen the injury, and <b>hydrochloric acid diffuses back</b> into the mucosa and injures small vessels → <b>edema, bleeding and erosion</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why NSAIDs injure</b> — NSAIDs <b>inhibit prostaglandin production</b> (COX inhibition) — everywhere, not just at the site of pain — so the barrier thins wherever acid touches it. Steroids, aldosterone antagonists and SSRIs also contribute to ulceration.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>How <i>H. pylori</i> survives acid</b> — spread <b>oral-to-oral or fecal-to-oral</b>. Its <b>urease</b> turns urea into <b>ammonia</b>, which <b>neutralizes</b> the acid around it; its <b>helical shape</b> lets it <b>burrow into the mucus layer</b>, hidden from immune cells. The body's response — neutrophils, lymphocytes, cytokines — damages the lining. Most carriers never get sick, but it is a <b>major risk for ulcers and gastric cancer</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Chronic gastritis and B₁₂</b> — patchy chronic inflammation slowly <b>thins and atrophies</b> the stomach wall. <b>Parietal cells</b> fail, acid falls, and <b>intrinsic factor</b> is lost — without it <b>vitamin B₁₂</b> can't be absorbed and, once stores run out, <b>pernicious anemia</b> follows. <i>H. pylori</i> is the most common cause; alcohol, radiation, smoking, bile reflux after pyloric surgery, Crohn's and uremia also contribute.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>This is why the ulcer drugs come in three kinds: <b>lower the acid</b> (PPIs, H₂ blockers, antacids), <b>rebuild the barrier</b> (sucralfate, misoprostol, bismuth), and <b>remove the cause</b> (antibiotics for <i>H. pylori</i>, stopping NSAIDs). Healing an ulcer without killing <i>H. pylori</i> is why so many ulcers used to come back.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-gastritis","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Gastritis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Gastritis</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Acute gastritis</b> — inflammation of the mucosa or submucosa from local irritants: a thick, red mucosa with prominent folds and some necrosis. If the muscle layer isn't involved it <b>heals in a few days</b>; if it is, <b>bleeding or hemorrhage</b> can occur. Causes: <b>long-term NSAIDs</b> (the big one), <b>alcohol, coffee and caffeine, stress, smoking</b>, radiation, swallowed <b>corrosives</b> (acids, alkalis like lye and drain cleaner), steroids, aldosterone antagonists, SSRIs. Food poisoning from <b>staphylococcal endotoxin</b> starts abruptly with severe vomiting within hours.</div>\n<div><b>Acute signs</b> — <b>rapid onset of epigastric pain and dyspepsia</b>; bleeding shows as <b>hematemesis</b> (bright red or coffee-ground) or <b>melena</b> (black, tarry). Gastric <b>hemorrhage</b> can be the first sign — a life-threatening emergency.</div>\n<div><b>Chronic gastritis</b> — few symptoms unless it ulcerates: nausea, vomiting, upper abdominal discomfort, epigastric pain <b>after meals</b>, anorexia. The most common cause is <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> (the more organisms, the more damage). Chronic <i>H. pylori</i> and <b>autoimmune gastritis</b> raise the risk of <b>gastric cancer</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis</b> — <b>EGD with biopsy is the gold standard</b> — symptoms alone can't make the diagnosis, and the biopsy names the type (classed by cause, cell changes or lesion pattern, and as <b>erosive</b> — ulcerating — or <b>nonerosive</b>); several biopsies if lesions are patchy; cytology rules out cancer; tissue tested for <i>H. pylori</i> (<b>rapid urease test</b>, culture, PCR). Results are more reliable if <b>antacids and PPIs are stopped for at least a week</b> before.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — usually managed in the community; <b>admitted</b> only for a flare with <b>fluid and electrolyte imbalance, bleeding or worsening pain</b>. <b>Remove the cause</b> — the main treatment for acute gastritis; supportive care; it usually heals within days. Bleeding → transfusion and fluids; major bleeding or ulceration may need partial gastrectomy, pyloroplasty or vagotomy. Diet: avoid what causes distress — <b>caffeine, acidic foods (tomato, citrus), bell peppers and onions</b>; <b>bland, nonspicy, smaller and more frequent meals</b>; no alcohol or tobacco. Drugs: <b>famotidine</b> (H₂ blocker), <b>sucralfate</b>, <b>omeprazole or pantoprazole</b>, aluminum-magnesium antacids. <b>Calcium carbonate</b> is a potent antacid but triggers <b>gastrin</b> → <b>rebound acid</b>. Chronic gastritis may need <b>vitamin B₁₂ injections</b>; treat <i>H. pylori</i> if present.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention (Box 47.1)</b> — balanced diet and exercise; <b>limit alcohol</b>; <b>avoid long-term aspirin, NSAIDs and corticosteroids</b>; avoid excess coffee (<b>even decaf</b>); safe food and water; <b>manage stress</b> (relaxation, meditation, yoga); <b>stop smoking</b>; protect against workplace toxins (lead, nickel); get GERD treated. Respect patients who can't easily do these (money, time, family duties).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Even decaffeinated coffee is on the avoid list because coffee itself — not just its caffeine — stimulates gastric acid secretion. The rule is about the stomach's acid output, not the stimulant.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-ulcer-and-gastritis-drugs-table-47-1","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Ulcer and gastritis drugs (Table 47.1)","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Ulcer and gastritis drugs (Table 47.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Antacids</b> — raise gastric pH and deactivate pepsin. <b>Liquid suspensions work better than chewable tablets.</b> <b>No other drugs within 1–2 hours</b> (they block absorption). <b>Magnesium hydroxide with aluminum hydroxide</b>: give <b>2 hours after meals and at bedtime</b>; watch for <b>diarrhea</b>; check for <b>chronic kidney disease or heart failure</b> — poor kidney excretion causes <b>hypermagnesemia</b>. <b>Aluminum hydroxide</b>: give <b>1 hour after meals and at bedtime</b>; causes <b>constipation</b> (alternate with a magnesium antacid); <b>safe in CKD</b> — it binds phosphate and has no magnesium.</div>\n<div><b>H₂ blockers</b> — <b>famotidine, nizatidine</b> block <b>histamine receptors on parietal cells</b> → less acid. A <b>single dose at bedtime</b> suppresses <b>night-time acid</b> for heartburn and PUD. <b>IV famotidine</b> prevents surgical <b>stress ulcers</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Mucosal protectors</b> — <b>sucralfate</b> coats the ulcer: give <b>1 hour before and 2 hours after meals and at bedtime</b> (food interferes), and <b>not within 30 minutes of antacids</b> or other drugs. <b>Bismuth subsalicylate</b> stops <i>H. pylori</i> from binding to the lining and boosts mucosal protection and prostaglandin production. It is a salicylate — <b>no aspirin</b> with it (salicylate overdose); it <b>turns stool and tongue black</b> — temporary and harmless.</div>\n<div><b>PPIs — the drug of choice</b> — <b>omeprazole, lansoprazole, dexlansoprazole, esomeprazole, rabeprazole, pantoprazole</b> suppress the <b>H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (proton pump)</b>. Give <b>30 minutes before the main meal</b> (food activates the pump the drug blocks) — rabeprazole <b>after the morning meal</b>. <b>Don't crush</b> delayed-release capsules or enteric-coated tablets (rabeprazole, pantoprazole). <b>IV pantoprazole</b>: on a pump <b>with a filter, in a separate line</b>, with <b>no other IV drugs</b> (precipitates). Esomeprazole: low dose with severe liver disease. Watch <b>drug interactions</b> (they change how other drugs are metabolized). <b>Don't stop abruptly — step down over several days</b> (rebound); reassess long-term use (<b>osteoporotic fractures</b>).</div>\n<div><b>PPIs through a feeding tube</b> — omeprazole, lansoprazole and dexlansoprazole can be <b>dissolved in sodium bicarbonate</b> (protects the granules from acid) for any feeding tube; capsules can be opened into <b>apple or orange juice</b> for a large-bore tube; esomeprazole, omeprazole and pantoprazole come as <b>oral suspensions</b>; esomeprazole and pantoprazole come <b>IV</b> for NPO patients.</div>\n<div><b>Misoprostol</b> — a <b>prostaglandin analog</b>: protects the mucosa and lowers acid — mainly for patients who must take <b>NSAIDs</b>. Avoid <b>magnesium antacids</b> (diarrhea). <b>Never in pregnancy</b> — it can cause <b>abortion, premature birth or birth defects</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Antibiotics for <i>H. pylori</i></b> — <b>clarithromycin</b>: caution in kidney impairment (watch BUN). <b>Amoxicillin</b>: <b>with food</b> (GI upset). <b>Tetracycline</b>: <b>1 hour before or 2 hours after meals</b> (dairy blocks absorption); <b>sunscreen</b> (photosensitivity). <b>Metronidazole</b>: <b>with food</b>; <b>no alcohol during and for at least 3 days after</b> — severe nausea, vomiting and headache (drug-alcohol reaction).</div>\n<div><b>Regimens</b> — <b>PPI-based triple therapy</b>: a PPI (e.g., lansoprazole) <b>plus two antibiotics</b> — metronidazole + tetracycline, or clarithromycin + amoxicillin — for <b>10–14 days</b>. <b>Quadruple therapy</b> adds <b>bismuth</b> to a PPI and two antibiotics — often for <b>penicillin allergy</b>. No single drug eradicates <i>H. pylori</i>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A proton pump inhibitor only blocks pumps that are <b>switched on</b>, and food is the switch. Given 30 minutes before the main meal, the drug is in the blood just as the pumps activate — taken after the meal, many pumps have already done their work. That timing rule is the most commonly missed PPI teaching point.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Peptic ulcer disease — the infection exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition and cause</b> — mucosal defenses fail and <b>acid and pepsin</b> erode the epithelium. Most ulcers come from <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> or <b>long-term NSAIDs</b>; other risks are the same as gastritis (alcohol, smoking, stress, caffeine). Duodenal ulcers often run in <b>families</b>. About <b>4.5 million</b> Americans a year; visits, admissions and deaths have fallen with <b>PPIs</b> and early <i>H. pylori</i> treatment.</div>\n<div><b>Duodenal ulcers (most common)</b> — deep, sharply edged ulcers in the <b>upper duodenum</b>, through mucosa and submucosa into the muscle. Main feature: <b>high acid secretion</b> — protein-rich meals, calcium and vagal stimulation drive acid, and fast stomach emptying dumps a large acid load into the duodenum before food can buffer it.</div>\n<div><b>Gastric ulcers</b> — usually in the <b>antrum</b> near acid-secreting mucosa: a break in the barrier (<i>H. pylori</i>) lets acid in; <b>back-diffusion</b> of acid, a weak <b>pyloric sphincter</b> that lets <b>bile reflux</b>, <b>delayed emptying</b> and reduced mucosal blood flow add injury.</div>\n<div><b>Stress ulcers</b> — acute mucosal lesions after a <b>crisis</b>: sepsis, head injury, surgery (NPO patients), <b>burns (Curling ulcer)</b>, sepsis (ischemic ulcer), <b>raised intracranial pressure (Cushing ulcer)</b>. They start as <b>ischemic</b> areas, erode and can <b>hemorrhage</b> massively — the main sign is <b>bleeding</b>. They lengthen stays and raise mortality.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Complications</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Hemorrhage — the most serious</b> — more often with <b>gastric and stress ulcers</b> and in <b>older adults</b>; recurs if <i>H. pylori</i> stays untreated or therapy lacks an H₂ blocker or PPI. Massive bleeding → <b>hematemesis</b> (bright red, or <b>coffee-ground</b> when acid has digested the blood) — bleeding at or above the duodenojejunal junction. Slow bleeding → <b>melena</b> (more common with duodenal ulcers). Box 47.2: hematemesis, melena, <b>falling Hgb/Hct and BP, rising heart rate</b>, weak pulses, <b>acute confusion in older adults</b>, vertigo, dizziness, syncope. Early detection decides the outcome: <b>monitor vital signs often</b> and report changes at once. Common sources of upper GI bleeding (Fig. 47.2): <b>esophageal varices</b>, esophageal cancer, <b>gastritis</b>, <b>gastric ulcer</b>, gastric cancer, <b>duodenal ulcer</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Perforation</b> — the ulcer erodes through the <b>full thickness</b> and contents spill into the peritoneum: <b>sudden, sharp mid-epigastric pain spreading over the whole abdomen</b>; <b>tender, rigid, board-like abdomen</b> (<b>peritonitis</b>), rebound tenderness; the patient lies in a <b>fetal position</b>; bowel sounds hyperactive then diminishing → <b>paralytic ileus</b>; <b>septicemia and hypovolemic shock</b> within hours. <b>A surgical emergency.</b></div>\n<div><b>Pyloric (gastric outlet) obstruction</b> — scarring, edema or inflammation block the pylorus: <b>bloating, nausea, vomiting</b> of retained food. Persistent vomiting loses acid (H⁺ and Cl⁻) → <b>metabolic alkalosis</b> and <b>hypokalemia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Intractable disease</b> — recurrent pain despite treatment — from complications, life stressors or trouble with long-term therapy → refer to gastroenterology.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Assessment and diagnosis</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — past <i>H. pylori</i>; all drugs — <b>NSAIDs, corticosteroids, chemotherapy</b>; radiation; GI surgery (partial gastrectomy, <b>bariatric</b>); alcohol, tobacco, foods that worsen symptoms; stressors; pain pattern with eating and sleep and what relieves it. <b>A change in the pain</b> — intermittent and relieved by food or antacids becoming <b>constant and radiating to the back</b> — suggests <b>perforation</b>. Many people with active ulcers have <b>no symptoms</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Physical</b> — <b>epigastric tenderness</b> at the midline between umbilicus and xiphoid; <b>dyspepsia</b> — sharp, burning or gnawing, or pressure, fullness or hunger. Perforation: <b>rigid board-like abdomen, rebound tenderness, intense pain</b>. <b>Orthostatic BP</b> and dehydration signs for blood loss — <b>dizziness on standing</b>; older adults risk <b>falls</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Gastric vs duodenal (Table 47.2)</b> — <b>gastric</b>: age <b>50–70</b>; men and women equally; no blood-group link; <b>normal or low acid</b>; pain <b>30–60 minutes after a meal</b>, rarely at night, <b>worsened by eating</b>; bleeds more as <b>hematemesis</b> than melena; cancer risk raised but under 5%; heals with treatment but tends to <b>recur in the same spot</b>; the surrounding mucosa shows <b>atrophic gastritis</b>. <b>Duodenal</b>: age <b>20–50</b>; more often <b>men</b>; most often <b>blood type O</b>; <b>acid hypersecretion</b>; <b>family history</b>; pain <b>1½–3 hours after a meal</b> and often <b>wakes the patient at 1–2 a.m.</b>; <b>relieved by eating</b>; bleeds more as <b>melena</b>; no added cancer risk; remissions and flare-ups — <b>60% recur within 1 year, 90% within 2</b>; no surrounding gastritis.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — how the ulcer affects <b>lifestyle, work, family, social and leisure life</b>, and how lifestyle changes will affect the patient and family. This shows whether the patient can <b>adhere to treatment</b> and has the <b>social support</b> to change habits — unstable housing, no money for food or drugs, or depression are <b>barriers</b> to follow up.</div>\n<div><b>Tests for <i>H. pylori</i></b> — <b>serology</b> (antibodies) is the most common; the <b>urea breath test</b> (swallow ¹³C urea, exhale — labeled carbon means urease is present) and <b>stool antigen</b> are <b>more accurate</b>. <b>EGD is the most accurate test for PUD</b>: it shows the crater and takes biopsies (rapid urease test, cancer cytology); may be repeated every <b>4–6 weeks</b> to check healing. <b>Nuclear scan</b> (technetium) finds bleeding — no prep; a repeat scan <b>1–2 days after treatment</b> shows whether it worked. Bleeding → low <b>Hgb/Hct</b>, stool <b>occult blood</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — many have <b>undiagnosed <i>H. pylori</b></i> because symptoms are vague; the average age of gastric cancer diagnosis is <b>68</b> — teach PUD symptoms and consider <b>screening</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Treating the ulcer</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Priority problems and goals</b> — <b>acute or persistent pain</b> and <b>potential for upper GI bleeding</b>. Pain goal: <b>3 or less on a 0–10 scale</b>. Drug goals: <b>relieve pain, eradicate <i>H. pylori</i>, heal the ulcer, prevent recurrence</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diet</b> — no diet has been shown to lower acid or heal ulcers; a <b>bland</b> diet may ease symptoms during the acute phase. Food buffers acid for <b>30–60 minutes</b>, then acid <b>rebounds</b>. Avoid foods that cause discomfort; <b>avoid bedtime snacks</b> (stimulate night acid); six small meals are <b>no longer routine</b> (may stimulate acid); <b>no alcohol or tobacco</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Stress</b> — emotional stress worsens GI disorders through the <b>gut-brain axis</b>; teach relaxation, <b>imagery, hypnosis, yoga and meditation</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The pain clock separates the two ulcers. A duodenal ulcer hurts when the duodenum is bathed in acid and nothing buffers it — <b>hours after a meal and in the early morning</b> — and food brings relief. A gastric ulcer sits where the food goes, so eating triggers the pain <b>soon after the meal</b> and food makes it worse.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Upper GI bleeding and ulcer emergencies","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Upper GI bleeding and ulcer emergencies</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Critical rescue</b> — active upper GI bleeding is <b>life-threatening</b>: priority is <b>airway, breathing and circulation</b> — <b>oxygen</b> and ventilatory support as needed, <b>two large-bore IV lines</b> for fluids and blood, and monitoring of <b>vital signs, hematocrit and SpO₂</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fluids and blood</b> — start <b>isotonic</b> fluid at once (<b>0.9% normal saline or lactated Ringer's</b>); <b>packed RBCs</b> to restore volume and Hgb/Hct; <b>fresh frozen plasma</b> if the PT is more than <b>1.5 times</b> the control. Watch I&amp;O and, in <b>older adults, fluid overload</b>; check electrolytes and <b>replace</b> what vomiting and NG suction remove (e.g., <b>K⁺</b>); trend <b>Hgb, Hct and coagulation studies</b> against baseline. Under <b>500 mL</b> loss: mild weakness and sweating. <b>Over 1 L in 24 hours</b>: <b>hypovolemic shock</b> — hypotension, chills, palpitations, diaphoresis, weak thready pulse.</div>\n<div><b>NG tube</b> — a <b>large-bore NG tube</b> to: <b>find blood</b> in the stomach, <b>assess the rate</b> of bleeding, <b>prevent gastric dilation</b>, and <b>decompress</b> to rest the GI tract.</div>\n<div><b>Pyloric obstruction</b> — decompress with <b>NG suction</b>, then correct <b>metabolic alkalosis and dehydration</b>. Edema and spasm usually respond to medical treatment. The NG tube is typically <b>clamped after about 72 hours</b> and residual checked: <b>50 mL or less in 30 minutes</b> (or the ordered limit) → oral fluids may start. Some need surgery.</div>\n<div><b>Endoscopic hemostasis</b> — EGD stops bleeding by <b>injecting chemicals</b>, applying <b>heat, electric current or laser</b>, or <b>banding or clipping</b> the vessel. Before: one or two <b>large-bore IVs</b> (moderate sedation, e.g., <b>midazolam</b> plus an opioid; transfusion), <b>NPO 4–6 hours</b>, signed <b>consent</b>. <b>After: VS, heart rhythm and SpO₂ until baseline; check swallowing — no food or fluid until the gag reflex returns</b> (the throat was numbed).</div>\n<div><b>If bleeding continues</b> — <b>catheter-directed embolization</b> by interventional radiology (usually femoral access; an arteriogram finds the bleeder and material is injected to stop it) — care like any percutaneous vascular procedure. Then <b>aggressive acid suppression with PPIs</b> to <b>stabilize the clot</b> by raising gastric pH and prevent rebleeding; once bleeding stops, PPIs are also the main drug for <b>healing the ulcer</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — now less common thanks to <i>H. pylori</i> treatment and endoscopy: for ulcers that <b>don't respond</b> or <b>emergencies like perforation</b>. <b>Minimally invasive</b> (laparoscopic) when possible — less pain, shorter stay, fewer complications. Procedures: <b>subtotal gastrectomy</b>, <b>pyloroplasty</b> (opens the pylorus), <b>vagotomy</b> (cuts the vagus to reduce acid).</div>\n<div><b>Going home</b> — discharge when there is <b>no ongoing bleeding, no orthostatic changes and no cardiopulmonary distress</b>. After major surgery or hemorrhage, one or two <b>home care visits</b>, especially for <b>older adults</b>. Home care (Box 47.3): VS with orthostatics, skin color, pain, <b>stool color and consistency</b>, bowel pattern, <b>Hgb/Hct</b>, bowel sounds; diet and <b>caffeine and alcohol</b>; understanding of drugs (<b>avoid NSAIDs</b>), smoking cessation. <b>Action alert — seek care at once</b> for <b>sudden, sharp, severe epigastric or abdominal pain</b>, <b>bloody or black stools</b>, or <b>bloody or coffee-ground vomit</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — no active PUD or <i>H. pylori</i>; pain relieved; the drug regimen and lifestyle changes followed; no upper GI bleeding — or bleeding controlled promptly.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/><b>Coffee-ground</b> vomitus means blood has sat in the stomach long enough for acid to turn hemoglobin brown — bleeding that is slower or older. <b>Bright red</b> means fast, fresh bleeding. <b>Melena</b> is blood digested on its way through the small intestine. Reading the color tells you how fast and how far up the bleeding is.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-gastric-cancer-and-life-after-gastrectom","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Gastric cancer and life after gastrectomy","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Gastric cancer</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The disease</b> — mostly <b>adenocarcinoma</b> of the mucosal glands; more common in <b>men</b>, rising sharply <b>after 65</b>. Precancerous: <b>atrophic gastritis</b> and <b>intestinal metaplasia</b> — low acid lets bacteria (<i>H. pylori</i>) multiply; it can also cause <b>MALT lymphoma</b>. Spreads through the wall to <b>lymph nodes</b>, directly into the liver, pancreas and colon, through the <b>portal vein to the liver</b> and blood to <b>lungs and bones</b> (blood-borne spread is the <b>most common</b> route of metastasis), and by <b>peritoneal seeding</b>. <b>5-year survival ~30%</b> (late diagnosis) but <b>~70%</b> if found early.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> is the <b>largest</b> (CagA gene). <b>Pernicious anemia, gastric polyps, chronic atrophic gastritis and achlorhydria</b> raise risk 2–3 times. Diet: <b>pickled foods, nitrates in processed food, added salt</b>, <b>few fruits and vegetables</b> (screen for <b>food insecurity</b> and link the patient to community food resources). Past <b>gastric surgery</b>; <b>Barrett's esophagus</b> (cancer at the cardia). Higher incidence and mortality in <b>non-White groups</b>, especially Black and Hispanic people, and highest in <b>Korean and Japanese Americans</b> — smoking, untreated <i>H. pylori</i>, poor access to care and to pre-surgery chemotherapy.</div>\n<div><b>Signs (Box 47.4)</b> — <b>early</b>: <b>chronic dyspepsia</b> and abdominal discomfort <b>relieved by antacids at first</b> (often ignored), fullness, epigastric, back or retrosternal pain. <b>Advanced</b>: <b>nausea and vomiting</b>, <b>iron deficiency anemia</b>, a <b>palpable epigastric mass</b>, <b>enlarged lymph nodes</b>, weakness, fatigue, <b>progressive weight loss</b>. Labs: low Hgb/Hct (micro- or macrocytic), <b>occult blood</b>, low albumin, abnormal liver tests with metastasis, <b>elevated CEA</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnose and treat</b> — <b>EGD with biopsy</b> (definitive) plus <b>endoscopic ultrasound</b> for depth and nodes; CT, PET and MRI for extent. Treatment depends on the <b>stage</b>: <b>chemotherapy</b> before and after surgery (oxaliplatin, fluorouracil, cisplatin, epirubicin — marrow suppression, nausea, vomiting); chemo and radiation <b>prolong survival</b> in advanced disease, but <b>radiation</b> is limited (spread, organ tolerance). <b>Surgery is preferred</b>: <b>subtotal gastrectomy</b> for mid or distal tumors, <b>total gastrectomy</b> (esophagus joined to the jejunum or duodenum; Fig. 47.3) for proximal tumors, with omentum and nodes; in early stages <b>laparoscopic resection plus chemo or radiation may cure</b>, and <b>laparoscopic + ERAS</b> recovers faster. Spread to supraclavicular or inguinal nodes, liver, umbilicus or perirectal wall means <b>cure is no longer possible</b> — <b>palliative</b> surgery (e.g., <b>gastroenterostomy</b> for outlet obstruction) for obstruction, bleeding or pain.</div>\n<div><b>Before surgery</b> — open surgery: an <b>NG tube to suction</b> empties the stomach so secretions don't contaminate the peritoneum, and stays a few days after (prevents vomiting and distention). Laparoscopic surgery doesn't need one. Correct <b>malnutrition</b> first — enteral supplements or <b>TPN</b>, vitamins, minerals, <b>iron and protein</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">After gastric surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Routine care</b> — prevent <b>atelectasis, paralytic ileus, wound infection and peritonitis</b>: lung sounds, <b>return of bowel sounds</b>, vital signs for infection or bleeding, aggressive pulmonary exercises and <b>early walking</b> (lungs, DVT), incision checks <b>every 8–12 hours</b>, <b>head of bed up</b> (reflux and aspiration).</div>\n<div><b>Acute gastric dilation</b> — a <b>clogged NG tube</b> lets the stomach distend: <b>epigastric pain, fullness, hiccups, tachycardia, hypotension</b> → notify the surgeon for an order to <b>irrigate or replace</b> the tube.</div>\n<div><b>Early dumping syndrome</b> — vasomotor symptoms <b>within about 20 minutes</b> of eating as food rushes into the small intestine and <b>pulls fluid into the gut</b> → distention and a drop in circulating volume: <b>vertigo, tachycardia, syncope, sweating, pallor, palpitations, the urge to lie down</b>. Report and <b>have the patient lie down</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Late dumping syndrome</b> — <b>1–3 hours</b> after eating: carbohydrate enters the jejunum fast → glucose spikes → <b>too much insulin</b> → <b>hypoglycemia</b>: <b>dizziness, light-headedness, palpitations, diaphoresis, confusion</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Managing dumping</b> — <b>small amounts at a time</b>; <b>no liquids with meals</b>; <b>high-protein, high-fat, low- to moderate-carbohydrate</b> diet (with the dietitian); <b>avoid concentrated sweets</b>; <b>lie down flat for a short time after eating</b> (slows emptying). Drugs: <b>acarbose</b> (slows carbohydrate absorption); in severe cases <b>octreotide</b> (somatostatin analog) 2–4 times a day <b>30 minutes before meals</b> to slow transit and reduce gut hormones.</div>\n<div><b>Other problems</b> — <b>delayed gastric emptying</b> for about a week (edema at the anastomosis, adhesions, or low K⁺, protein or Na⁺) — resolves with NG suction, fluids, electrolytes and nutrition. <b>Vitamin B₁₂, folic acid and iron deficiency</b>, poor <b>calcium and vitamin D</b> absorption — less <b>intrinsic factor</b> and fast transit. Watch for <b>pernicious anemia</b>, <b>atrophic glossitis</b> (shiny, smooth, <b>\"beefy\" tongue</b>), <b>megaloblastic anemia and leukopenia</b> on the CBC → <b>vitamin B₁₂</b> (plus folic acid or iron).</div>\n<div><b>Going home</b> — total gastrectomy or advanced disease → maximal help at home, transitional care or a skilled facility; subtotal gastrectomy without debility → home with partial help with ADLs; <b>recurrence is common</b> — regular follow-up and imaging, coordinated with the case manager. Teach dressing changes and <b>infection signs</b>; chemo and radiation side effects and <b>antiemetics</b>. Diet: <b>small, frequent meals</b>, <b>no liquids with meals</b>, avoid uncomfortable foods, <b>no caffeine or alcohol</b>, stop smoking, <b>B₁₂ injections as prescribed</b>, <b>lie flat briefly after eating</b>. Support coping; <b>hospice</b> and end-of-life care when needed; home care, dietitian, counselor or spiritual referral; cancer support groups.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Without a stomach to hold food and release it slowly, a meal hits the small intestine all at once. The concentrated contents <b>draw water into the bowel</b> (early dumping looks like a sudden drop in blood volume), and the sugar is absorbed in a rush that provokes an <b>insulin overshoot</b> an hour or two later (late dumping looks like hypoglycemia). Every dumping rule — small meals, no fluids with meals, little sugar, more protein and fat, lying down — <b>slows the delivery</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-adpie","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Stomach care is about <b>acid, blood and the barrier</b>: find what broke the barrier (NSAIDs, <i>H. pylori</i>, alcohol, smoking), protect against the bleeding and perforation it can cause, heal it with the right drug at the right time, and — after a gastrectomy — rebuild eating habits around a stomach that can no longer hold a meal.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div><b>NSAIDs, aspirin, steroids</b>, SSRIs, chemotherapy; <i>H. pylori</i> history; <b>alcohol, tobacco, caffeine</b>; stress; diet (pickled, salted, processed foods; fruit and vegetable intake; <b>food insecurity</b>); GI and <b>bariatric</b> surgery; family history; <b>pain pattern</b> with meals and night waking and what relieves it; <b>any change in pain</b> (constant, radiating to the back). <b>Psychosocial</b>: effect on work and family, support, and <b>barriers to adherence (housing, money, mental health)</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical and labs</div>epigastric tenderness; abdomen for <b>rigidity and rebound</b>; bowel sounds; <b>vital signs and orthostatics</b>; skin color; <b>vomitus and stool</b> for red, coffee-ground or black blood; <b>Hgb/Hct</b>, BUN, Na⁺, K⁺, WBC; <i>H. pylori</i> tests; signs of <b>B₁₂ deficiency</b> (beefy tongue).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>BUN rising with a normal creatinine in a vomiting or bleeding patient is a clue in itself: digested blood is a protein load the liver turns into urea, and dehydration concentrates it — so a high BUN supports an upper GI bleed.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>PUD: <b>acute or persistent pain</b> and <b>potential for upper GI bleeding</b> (and perforation, obstruction). Active bleeding: <b>hypovolemia</b> — the priority over pain and vomiting. Gastritis: pain and nutrition. After gastrectomy: <b>dumping</b>, <b>nutrition deficits</b> (B₁₂, iron, folate, calcium), respiratory and wound complications. Gastric cancer: grief, coping, nutrition.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>pain <b>≤ 3/10</b>; <i>H. pylori</i> eradicated and the ulcer healed; <b>no bleeding</b> — or bleeding controlled with vital signs back to normal; stable weight; after gastrectomy, <b>no dumping</b> symptoms with the meal plan and normal B₁₂ and CBC.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Drugs, timed right</div><b>triple or quadruple therapy</b> for 10–14 days — stress <b>finishing it</b>; <b>PPI 30 minutes before the main meal</b>, don't crush, taper rather than stop; <b>antacids 1–2 hours from other drugs</b>; <b>sucralfate 1 hour before meals, 30 minutes from antacids</b>; <b>no aspirin with bismuth</b> (black stool is harmless); <b>metronidazole — no alcohol</b>; <b>misoprostol never in pregnancy</b>; <b>avoid NSAIDs</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Bleeding</div><b>ABCs</b>, <b>oxygen</b>, <b>two large-bore IVs</b>, <b>NS or LR</b>, PRBCs (FFP if PT &gt; 1.5×), <b>large-bore NG</b>, VS, <b>Hct and SpO₂</b>, I&amp;O, electrolytes (<b>replace K⁺</b> lost to vomiting and suction); prepare for <b>EGD</b> (NPO 4–6 h, consent) and afterward <b>nothing by mouth until the gag reflex returns</b>; <b>PPIs</b> to stabilize the clot; watch for <b>perforation</b> (sudden pain, rigid abdomen).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Diet and lifestyle</div>bland during flares, avoid irritating foods, <b>no bedtime snacks</b>, <b>no alcohol or tobacco</b>, stress reduction; after gastrectomy — <b>small meals, no fluids with meals, high protein and fat, low sugar, lie down after eating</b>, <b>B₁₂ injections</b>, acarbose or octreotide if ordered.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>pain controlled; stools and vomitus free of blood; <b>Hgb/Hct, BP and pulse stable</b> with no orthostatic drop; <i>H. pylori</i> test negative after treatment; after gastrectomy, eating small meals <b>without dumping</b>, weight stable, B₁₂ given; the patient states when to <b>seek care at once</b> (sudden severe pain, black or bloody stool, coffee-ground vomit).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-overview","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">48 · Chapter 48</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Elimination · fluid &amp; electrolyte balance · pain</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Noninflammatory intestinal conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> This chapter is about the bowel when something <b>blocks, grows in, injures or pushes through</b> it — without primary inflammation. The exemplar is <b>intestinal obstruction</b>, where elimination stops and fluid shifts out of the circulation; next to it sit <b>colorectal cancer</b> (and the <b>colostomy</b> that may follow), <b>irritable bowel syndrome</b>, <b>abdominal trauma</b>, <b>hernias</b> and <b>hemorrhoids</b>. The common thread for the nurse: watch <b>fluid and electrolytes</b>, watch the <b>character of the pain</b> (a change means a complication), and teach the habits — fiber, fluids, no straining — that prevent half of these problems.</p><div id=\"ch48-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Peristalsis moves contents along; the small bowel absorbs nutrients and much of the fluid; the colon absorbs water and electrolytes and stores stool; the abdominal wall holds the contents in.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A physical block (adhesions, tumor, hernia, impaction, volvulus, intussusception) or lost peristalsis (paralytic ileus); cancer from adenomatous polyps; disordered motility (IBS); injury; a weak wall (hernia); swollen veins (hemorrhoids).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Distention, cramping or constant pain, vomiting (profuse and early when the block is high), obstipation; blood in or on the stool, change in stool shape; a bulge that changes with straining; bruising patterns after trauma.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">CT or MRI, ultrasound, endoscopy; Hgb/Hct, BUN and electrolytes; FIT/FOBT and <b>colonoscopy</b> (definitive for CRC); CEA to monitor; FAST scan in trauma; hydrogen breath test in IBS.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">NPO, <b>NG decompression</b>, IV fluids with potassium, alvimopan for post-op ileus; surgery to relieve obstruction or remove cancer (resection, colostomy, AP resection); chemo; fiber, drugs and stress care for IBS; hernia repair; hemorrhoid care.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hypovolemic shock, acute kidney injury, <b>strangulation</b> with ischemia, perforation, peritonitis and sepsis; metastasis to the liver; abdominal compartment syndrome; stoma necrosis.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Pain that changes from <b>colicky to constant</b> or stops and becomes tenderness; <b>temperature above 100°F</b> with rising pulse; a hernia that won't reduce; a dark or black stoma; Cullen, Turner or Kehr sign after trauma.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Bowel blocked<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>contents and gas pile up above it<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>peristalsis and secretions increase → more distention<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>bowel wall swells and leaks plasma<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>hypovolemia and electrolyte loss<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>strangulation → ischemia → sepsis</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Small- vs large-bowel obstruction (Box 48.1)</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Small</b>: upper/epigastric distention, visible peristaltic waves in the upper and middle abdomen, <b>early profuse vomiting</b> (may be fecal), obstipation, <b>severe fluid and electrolyte loss</b>, possible <b>metabolic alkalosis</b>. <b>Large</b>: <b>lower distention</b>, intermittent lower cramping, <b>little or no vomiting</b>, <b>ribbon-like stools</b> or obstipation, <b>no major fluid and electrolyte imbalance</b>, possible <b>metabolic acidosis</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Mechanical vs paralytic ileus</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Mechanical</b>: <b>colicky, cramping</b> pain, <b>high-pitched bowel sounds (borborygmi) above the block</b> early, then silence. <b>Paralytic (nonmechanical)</b>: <b>constant, diffuse</b> discomfort, bowel sounds <b>decreased then absent</b>, vomitus rarely foul or profuse.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Right- vs left-sided colon cancer</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Right</b>: liquid stool, so tumors grow large without changing bowel habits — <b>intermittent dark (mahogany) blood</b>, <b>anemia</b>, a mass in the right lower quadrant. <b>Left / rectosigmoid</b>: <b>obstruction</b>, \"gas pains\", <b>narrow stools</b>, <b>bright red blood</b>, straining.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Alvimopan</b> blocks opioid receptors <b>only in the gut</b>, so it restores motility after surgery without taking away pain relief. <b>Alosetron</b> (a 5-HT₃ blocker) slows the gut — and can cause severe constipation or <b>ischemic colitis</b> — so report constipation or bloody diarrhea at once. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">VS and urine output, daily weight (best fluid measure), electrolytes (K⁺, Na⁺, Cl⁻), bowel sounds and flatus, distention, NG output and placement, pain character, stoma color, perineal wound drainage.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Pain that becomes constant or localized, abdominal rigidity, fever &gt; 100°F with tachycardia, hypotension (strangulation, perforation, peritonitis); a <b>purple, black or dry stoma</b>; an irreducible hernia; signs of hemorrhage after trauma.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why obstruction causes hypovolemia</b> — contents (fluid, food, saliva, gastric, pancreatic and biliary secretions, swallowed air) <b>pile up at and above the block</b>. Peristalsis speeds up to push them on, which <b>stimulates more secretions</b> and more distention. The swollen bowel wall becomes <b>leaky</b>: plasma escapes into the peritoneum and fluid is trapped in the lumen, so less returns to the circulation → <b>hypovolemia</b> (mild to shock) and electrolyte loss. Severe hypovolemia can cause <b>acute kidney injury</b> or death.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Acid-base by location</b> — <b>High small-bowel</b> block → loss of gastric <b>hydrochloric acid</b> → <b>metabolic alkalosis</b>. Below the duodenum but above the colon → <b>acids and bases</b> both lost. At the end of the small bowel and below → loss of <b>alkaline</b> fluid → <b>metabolic acidosis</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Strangulation and sepsis</b> — stagnant bacteria in a blocked loop are harmless <b>until blood flow fails</b>: in a <b>closed-loop</b> (blocked at two points) <b>or strangulated</b> obstruction, ischemic bowel releases <b>endotoxins</b> into the peritoneum and blood → <b>peritonitis and septic shock</b>; blood is lost into the bowel and peritoneum; rising pressure can cause <b>abdominal compartment syndrome</b>. Mesenteric arterial occlusion causes the same gangrene and sepsis.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>From polyp to cancer</b> — most colorectal cancers are <b>adenocarcinomas</b> from glandular epithelium. Loss of tumor-suppressor genes and activation of oncogenes speed cell division in the mucosa, forming <b>adenomatous polyps</b> that can turn malignant. <b>Polyps</b> are small mucosa-covered growths on the bowel surface — <b>most are benign</b>, but some can become cancer, so they are removed when seen at sigmoidoscopy or colonoscopy. More than half of cancers arise in the <b>rectosigmoid</b> colon (Fig. 48.2). Spread is by direct extension through the bowel wall, by <b>lymph</b>, by blood — <b>the liver is the most common metastatic site</b> (then lungs, brain, bones, adrenals) — and by <b>peritoneal seeding</b> during surgery.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Pain is the best real-time monitor in this chapter. Colicky, coming-and-going pain is the bowel <b>fighting</b> a block; when it turns <b>constant and localized</b> — or suddenly stops and becomes tenderness — the bowel has stopped fighting because it is <b>losing its blood supply or has perforated</b>. That is why analgesics are sometimes held during the workup: the pain pattern is the diagnosis.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Intestinal obstruction — the elimination exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Causes and who gets it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Mechanical obstruction</b> — the bowel is <b>physically blocked</b> — from outside (<b>adhesions</b>, the most common cause after surgery), in the wall (Crohn's strictures, radiation), or in the lumen (<b>tumors</b>). Causes: adhesions, benign or malignant <b>tumors</b>, appendicitis complications, <b>hernias</b>, <b>fecal impaction</b> (especially older adults), strictures, <b>intussusception</b> (bowel telescoping into itself), <b>volvulus</b> (twisting; both in Fig. 48.1), fibrosis (endometriosis). The <b>ileum</b>, the narrowest part, is the most common site.</div>\n<div><b>Nonmechanical (paralytic ileus)</b> — no physical block — <b>peristalsis decreases or stops</b> from a neuromuscular disturbance. <b>Postoperative ileus</b> from handling the bowel in abdominal surgery lasts hours to days; <b>hypokalemia</b> predisposes to it. Also <b>peritonitis</b>, and <b>intestinal ischemia</b> from mesenteric thrombosis or embolus (→ infarction, gangrene, sepsis).</div>\n<div><b>Who and prevention</b> — most common after <b>bowel surgery</b>, with <b>intestinal tumors</b>, and in <b>older adults</b> (fecal impaction, cancer). Prevent impaction: <b>fluids, exercise, high fiber, stool softener</b> if needed; get <b>colon cancer screening</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — GI disorders and surgeries; <b>nausea and vomiting and its color</b> (green-yellow, bilious, bloody); a full pain assessment — <b>severe pain that stops and becomes tenderness may mean perforation</b> (report promptly); <b>flatus and the last bowel movement</b>; <b>hiccups (singultus)</b> are common. <b>Suspect obstruction → keep NPO and call the provider.</b> Ask about family history of colorectal cancer and blood or change in the stool.</div>\n<div><b>Temperature and vital signs</b> — uncomplicated obstruction rarely raises temperature above <b>100°F (37.8°C)</b>. A higher temperature, with or without guarding and tenderness, and a <b>sustained rise in pulse</b> suggest <b>strangulation, peritonitis or ischemia</b>. Report at once: <b>fever, tachycardia, hypotension, increasing pain, rigidity, or a color change of the abdominal skin</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Small-bowel obstruction</b> — <b>mid-abdominal cramping</b> that comes and goes (comfortable between); <b>localized, persistent</b> pain if strangulated; <b>vomiting</b> — more profuse the higher the block; bile and mucus, or <b>orange-brown and foul (feculent)</b> with a low ileal block. <b>Obstipation</b> and no flatus with complete obstruction; <b>diarrhea</b> may occur with partial obstruction. Prolonged vomiting upsets fluid and electrolyte balance — Box 48.1 lists <b>severe fluid and electrolyte imbalance</b> and possible <b>metabolic alkalosis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Large-bowel obstruction</b> — <b>mild, intermittent, colicky</b> lower abdominal pain; <b>lower distention</b>; <b>minimal or no vomiting</b>; obstipation or <b>ribbon-like stools</b> if partial; bowel changes and <b>blood</b> if the cause is diverticulitis or cancer. Unlike a small-bowel block there is <b>no major fluid and electrolyte imbalance</b>; <b>metabolic acidosis</b> is possible (Box 48.1).</div>\n<div><b>The abdomen</b> — <b>distention</b> in all types; maybe <b>visible peristaltic waves</b>. Mechanical: <b>high-pitched bowel sounds (borborygmi) above the block</b> early as the bowel tries to push through, then <b>absent</b> — especially below it; tenderness and rigidity usually minimal. A tense, fluid-filled loop felt like a mass suggests a <b>closed-loop strangulating</b> obstruction. Nonmechanical: <b>constant diffuse</b> discomfort (severe and constant with vascular causes), bowel sounds <b>decreased then absent</b>, frequent but rarely foul vomiting.</div>\n<div><b>Labs and imaging</b> — no single lab confirms it. <b>WBC normal</b> unless strangulation, infarction or gangrene. <b>Hgb, Hct and BUN rise</b> (dehydration); <b>Na⁺, Cl⁻ and K⁺ fall</b>; <b>amylase</b> may rise with strangulation. <b>CT or MRI</b> as soon as obstruction is suspected (fluid and gas in the small bowel with <b>no gas in the colon</b> = small-bowel obstruction); ultrasound; sigmoidoscopy or colonoscopy — <b>not if perforation or complete obstruction is suspected</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problem</b> — <b>potential for life-threatening complications</b> from reduced or blocked flow of intestinal contents.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Caring for obstruction — the NG tube, fluids and surgery","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nonsurgical care (Box 48.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>When</b> — partial obstruction without strangulation or ischemia, <b>paralytic ileus</b> (responds very well), and patients with terminal disease. <b>Fecal impaction</b> resolves with <b>disimpaction and enemas</b>; <b>intussusception</b> may reduce with a <b>barium enema</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The NG tube</b> — <b>NPO</b> plus a <b>Salem sump</b> NG tube to <b>low continuous suction</b> to drain fluid and air; its <b>vent (\"pigtail\")</b> keeps the stomach lining from being pulled into the tube. <b>At least every 4 hours</b>: check <b>placement, patency and output</b> (amount and character); check the <b>nasal skin</b>; secure with an approved device. <b>Listen for bowel sounds with the suction turned off</b> (suction noise masks them); assess the abdomen for <b>bowel sounds, distention and flatus at least twice a day</b> (Box 48.2). Less output or stasis, nausea, vomiting or more distention → the tube may be <b>out of position or plugged</b>; a repositioned or new tube may need <b>x-ray confirmation</b>; then aspirate and <b>irrigate with 30 mL normal saline every 4 hours</b> or as ordered. <b>Frequent mouth and nares care</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fluids and electrolytes</b> — vomiting, NPO and NG suction drain <b>potassium and sodium</b>: <b>IV isotonic fluid (NS or LR), often 2–4 L, with potassium</b>, guided by electrolytes and BUN — careful in older adults with heart or kidney disease (<b>overload</b>). <b>Daily weight is the most reliable fluid measure</b>; also lung sounds and I&amp;O. VS, urine output, turgor and mucous membranes <b>every 2–4 hours</b>. Blood for strangulation; <b>parenteral nutrition</b> if long NPO or malnourished.</div>\n<div><b>Comfort</b> — thirst → <b>frequent mouth care</b>, delegated to assistive personnel (some older adults don't feel thirst); <b>a few ice chips</b> only if no surgery is planned and per protocol. <b>Report pain that increases or changes from colicky to constant</b> (perforation, peritonitis). Analgesics may be <b>held during the workup</b> so perforation signs aren't masked — explain why; <b>morphine slows motility and can cause vomiting</b> (also a sign of a blocked tube or worsening obstruction). Use non-drug comfort, frequent position changes (promote peristalsis) and <b>semi-Fowler</b> (less pressure on the chest, easier breathing). Food and fluids worsen pain; pain is usually <b>milder with ileus</b> than with mechanical obstruction.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — <b>alvimopan</b> — an oral, <b>peripherally acting mu-opioid antagonist</b> — for short-term use in <b>postoperative ileus</b> (raises GI motility). <b>IV broad-spectrum antibiotics</b> if strangulation is suspected. <b>Metoclopramide</b> (prokinetic) for partial obstruction or ileus.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Surgery and going home</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>When and what</b> — <b>complete</b> mechanical obstruction (and some incomplete) needs surgery; <b>strangulation always does</b>. <b>Exploratory laparotomy</b> finds the cause: <b>adhesions are lysed</b>; tumor or diverticulitis → <b>colon resection</b> with anastomosis or a <b>colostomy</b>; infarction → <b>embolectomy, thrombectomy or resection</b> of gangrenous bowel; severe cases → <b>colectomy</b>. Most now have <b>laparoscopic surgery</b> (longer to do, less blood loss, faster healing, often <b>no NG tube</b>, home in 1–2 days). If the patient is too ill to absorb teaching, reinforce it with the family.</div>\n<div><b>After open surgery</b> — NG tube until <b>peristalsis returns</b>; clear liquids are tried with the tube <b>clamped for 1–2 hours</b> — vomiting means suction resumes; when peristalsis is back the tube is clamped on schedule and removed if there is no nausea.</div>\n<div><b>Prevent fecal impaction (older adults)</b> — <b>high-fiber foods</b> — raw fruit, vegetables, whole grains (mind the cost); <b>plenty of fluids, especially water</b>; <b>avoid routine laxatives</b> (overuse weakens abdominal tone → <b>atonic colon</b>); <b>daily walks</b>; <b>warm drinks and prune juice</b>; <b>bulk-forming products</b> and <b>stool softeners</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Teaching</b> — report <b>abdominal pain or distention, nausea or vomiting</b>, with or without constipation (recurrence) — though recurrent ileus is uncommon. Incision care, activity limits; with opioid analgesics add a <b>stool softener plus laxative</b> (e.g., <b>docusate with senna</b>). Home health for incision or colostomy care if needed. <b>Evaluate</b>: obstruction relieved, no life-threatening complications, usual bowel elimination back.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Listening for bowel sounds with the suction running is a classic error: the suction itself gurgles, so the nurse \"hears\" peristalsis that isn't there. The same logic explains the clamp trial — the only reliable proof that the bowel is working again is that it can <b>move contents forward on its own</b> without the tube draining them.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Colorectal cancer — risk, screening and diagnosis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Risk</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>How common</b> — the <b>4th most common cancer</b> and <b>2nd most common cause of cancer death</b> in US adults — yet <b>highly curable when found early</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — <b>age over 50</b>, genetic predisposition, personal or <b>family history</b> (a first-degree relative → <b>3–4 times</b> the risk); a rising number of adults <b>under 50</b> without clear cause; <b>FAP, Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis</b>; possibly <i>H. pylori</i>, <i>Streptococcus bovis</i>, <b>HPV</b>; <b>long-term smoking, obesity, inactivity, heavy alcohol</b>; a <b>high-fat diet</b> (red-meat animal fat → more bile acids and anaerobic bacteria, thought carcinogenic); <b>refined carbohydrates low in fiber</b> (alter transit, favor polyps).</div>\n<div><b>Inherited syndromes</b> — <b>familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)</b>: autosomal dominant <b>APC</b> gene mutation, ~1% of CRC — <b>thousands of polyps</b> over 10–15 years with <b>nearly 100%</b> chance of cancer → most need <b>colectomy (with ileostomy or ileoanal pull-through) by age 20</b>. <b>Lynch syndrome (HNPCC)</b>: autosomal dominant mismatch-repair mutations (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS1, PMS2), 3–5% of CRC — <b>80% lifetime risk</b> at an average age of <b>45</b>, plus endometrial, ovarian, stomach, small-bowel, brain and ureteral cancers. <b>Genetic testing</b> is available — teach families of hereditary CRC to be tested.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Prevention and screening</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Healthy People 2030 (Box 48.3)</b> — the national colorectal cancer objectives: <b>reduce the CRC death rate</b> and <b>increase the proportion of adults screened</b> for CRC.</div>\n<div><b>Screening schedule</b> — discuss at <b>40</b>; average-risk adults from <b>45</b>: <b>FOBT/FIT yearly</b>, <b>stool DNA every 3 years</b>, <b>colonoscopy every 10 years</b>, or <b>flexible sigmoidoscopy or CT colonography every 5 years</b>. Personal or family history → <b>earlier and more often</b>. <b>75–85</b>: optional by life expectancy; <b>over 85</b>: not recommended (treatment risks), and surgery is withheld when CRC is diagnosed after 85 — an ethical question as CRC rises after 80. When everyone has access to needed screening and treatment, the ethical principle of <b>justice</b> is upheld. Screening rates are lowest among the <b>uninsured</b> and those with <b>less than a high school education</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — <b>5-year survival is lowest for Black people</b>, especially under 50; mortality is <b>30–40% higher in poor regions</b> (Appalachia, parts of the South and Midwest) — lack of screening, insurance, education and modern cancer care. Teach early screening and point to resources.</div>\n<div><b>Lifestyle</b> — less <b>fat, refined carbohydrate and low-fiber food</b>; <b>baked or broiled</b>, high-fiber, low animal fat; more <b>broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, sprouts</b>; healthy weight (obesity is a major cancer risk); <b>stop smoking, limit alcohol, exercise</b>; check <b>food security</b> and access to online cessation programs.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Signs and diagnosis</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Signs</b> — ask about <b>changes in bowel habits or stool shape</b>, constipation, vomiting, <b>blood</b>; fatigue (anemia), fullness, vague pain and <b>weight loss</b> suggest <b>advanced</b> disease. The most common signs: <b>rectal bleeding, anemia, and a change in stool consistency or shape</b> — occult, <b>mahogany (dark)</b> or bright red blood. Ascending colon: gross blood uncommon; descending colon and rectum: common but not massive. Other common sources of lower GI bleeding: <b>ulcerative colitis, polyps, diverticulosis or diverticulitis, hemorrhoids</b> (Fig. 48.3).</div>\n<div><b>By location</b> — <b>transverse and descending</b>: <b>obstruction</b> symptoms — \"gas pains\", cramping, incomplete evacuation. <b>Rectosigmoid</b>: <b>hematochezia</b> (red blood per rectum), straining, <b>narrow stools</b>, dull pain. <b>Right side (ascending)</b>: stool is liquid, so tumors grow <b>large without changing bowel habits</b>; they ulcerate and bleed intermittently (<b>mahogany</b> stool); <b>RLQ mass</b>; <b>anemia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Stool blood tests</b> — a positive <b>FOBT or FIT</b> means GI bleeding. Some vitamins and drugs cause <b>false positives</b>, so the <b>preparation depends on the type of test</b>: some call for <b>no aspirin, vitamin C, iron or red meat for the 48 hours before</b> and stop antiinflammatory drugs (ibuprofen, corticosteroids, salicylates) for a set period first <i>(ch 45 gives the guaiac test's own schedule — no NSAIDs for 7 days, no red meat or vitamin C over 250 mg for 3 days — and FIT needs no preparation)</i>. For the FOBT, test <b>2–3 samples on 3 consecutive days</b>. A <b>negative result doesn't rule out</b> cancer.</div>\n<div><b>Other tests</b> — <b>CEA</b> (normal <b>&lt; 5 ng/mL</b>) is high in many with CRC but also in other diseases and <b>smokers</b> — used to <b>monitor treatment and detect recurrence</b>. <b>CT colonography (virtual)</b> — no biopsy or treatment possible. <b>Sigmoidoscopy</b> — lower colon, polyps removed. <b>Colonoscopy</b> — rectum to ileocecal valve, polyps removed and biopsied — <b>the definitive test</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Where the tumor sits decides how it shows itself. Stool in the right colon is still <b>liquid</b>, so it flows around a growing mass — the first clue is often <b>anemia</b> from slow bleeding. By the left colon and rectum, stool is <b>formed</b>, so the same mass narrows it, blocks it and streaks it with <b>bright red</b> blood that hasn't had time to darken.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Colorectal cancer — treatment, surgery and colostomy care","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Treatment</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The plan</b> — goal: <b>remove all or as much of the tumor as possible</b>; staging uses <b>TNM</b>. <b>Surgery</b> is primary; <b>adjuvant</b> therapies before or after aim to cure and prevent recurrence.</div>\n<div><b>Radiation</b> — pre-op radiation doesn't improve survival in <b>colon</b> cancer but helps local control; post-op radiation shows no consistent benefit. <b>Rectal</b> cancer: radiation is <b>often part of the plan</b>. <b>Palliative</b> radiation for pain, hemorrhage, obstruction or lung metastases. Side effects: <b>diarrhea, fatigue</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Chemotherapy and targeted drugs</b> — adjuvant chemo for <b>stage II or III</b>: <b>IV 5-fluorouracil with leucovorin (5-FU/LV)</b>, <b>capecitabine</b>, <b>irinotecan</b>, <b>oxaliplatin</b>, usually combined. They don't spare healthy cells: <b>diarrhea, mucositis, leukopenia, mouth ulcers, peripheral neuropathy</b> (some use cannabis for side effects). <b>Bevacizumab</b> (<b>VEGF</b> inhibitor) <b>cuts the tumor's blood supply</b> in metastatic CRC; <b>cetuximab, panitumumab</b> (<b>EGFR</b> inhibitors) block growth signals. <b>Intrahepatic arterial</b> 5-FU for liver metastases; opioids and antiemetics for symptoms.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — removal with <b>cancer-free margins</b> plus <b>lymph nodes</b> (the number with cancer predicts prognosis). Procedures: <b>colon resection</b> with reanastomosis; partial colectomy with a temporary or permanent <b>colostomy</b>; total colectomy with <b>ileostomy or ileoanal pull-through</b>; <b>abdominoperineal (AP) resection</b> for <b>rectal</b> tumors — removes the <b>sigmoid colon, rectum and anus</b> through abdominal and perineal incisions → <b>permanent colostomy</b>. <b>Laparoscopic</b> resection is common: shorter stay, less pain, faster recovery.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Before surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Preparing the patient</b> — explain incisions and drains; the surgeon may not know until surgery whether a <b>colostomy</b> is needed — the patient is told it is <b>possible</b>. A <b>certified wound, ostomy and continence nurse (CWOCN)</b> teaches ostomy care and <b>marks the stoma site</b> (Box 48.4): knowledge, education, <b>sensory limits</b>, support, work, hobbies, <b>money for supplies</b>; abdominal contour <b>lying, sitting and standing</b>, <b>skinfolds, creases, bony prominences, scars</b>, the <b>belt line</b>, a site the patient can <b>see</b>, ideally in the <b>rectus muscle</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Low rectal surgery</b> — <b>AP resection</b> risks <b>sexual dysfunction and urinary incontinence</b> from nerve damage — the surgeon discusses it before consent; let the patient voice concerns. Teach turning, deep breathing, and the pain plan (<b>PCA</b>, epidural).</div>\n<div><b>Bowel prep and antibiotics</b> — for elective surgery (no obstruction or perforation) a <b>bowel prep</b> (laxatives, enemas or whole-gut lavage) may be ordered — <b>controversial</b>; <b>older adults can become dehydrated</b>. One dose of <b>oral or IV antibiotic</b> before the incision. An NG tube after open surgery (not after laparoscopic); IV or central line for fluids while NPO. After <b>open</b> resection expect the NG tube plus <b>IV PCA for the first 24–36 hours</b>, then a slow diet advance from liquids to solids once the tube is out; after <b>laparoscopic</b> resection, <b>solid food very soon</b>, earlier walking and home in <b>1–2 days</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The colostomy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Types</b> — <b>loop</b> stoma — a loop of (usually <b>transverse</b>) colon brought out and opened, usually <b>temporary</b>, often supported by an <b>external rod</b> (don't dislodge it during pouch changes). <b>End</b> stoma — usually <b>descending or sigmoid</b>, often <b>permanent</b>. <b>Double-barrel</b> (least common) — two stomas: the <b>proximal</b> one (closer to the head) <b>passes stool</b>, the <b>distal</b> one is a <b>mucous fistula</b>. The site follows the tumor (Fig. 48.4): <b>ascending</b> for right-sided tumors, <b>descending</b> for left-sided tumors, <b>sigmoid</b> for rectal tumors; a <b>transverse (double-barrel)</b> colostomy is often made in <b>emergencies</b> such as obstruction or perforation because it is <b>quick to create</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Stoma assessment</b> — after surgery: a <b>clear pouch</b> to see the stoma (or <b>petrolatum gauze</b> to keep it moist, under a dry sterile dressing) — then a pouch system as soon as possible. A healthy stoma is <b>reddish pink to dark red-pink, moist</b>, protruding <b>1–3 cm (usually ~2 cm)</b>; slight <b>edema</b> and a little <b>bleeding</b> are common early and settle in <b>6–8 weeks</b>. <b>Report at once</b>: <b>ischemia or necrosis</b> (<b>dark red, purple or black, dry</b>), <b>continuous heavy bleeding</b>, <b>mucocutaneous separation</b> (the suture line between stoma and skin breaking down).</div>\n<div><b>Output</b> — the colostomy usually starts working <b>2–3 days</b> after surgery, with lots of gas at first. <b>Empty the pouch at one-third to one-half full</b>. Stool consistency follows location: <b>ascending → liquid</b>, <b>transverse → pasty</b>, <b>descending → formed</b>. An <b>ascending</b> colostomy keeps draining <b>liquid</b> stool, so its pouch is <b>worn at all times</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Peristomal skin and fit</b> — skin should be <b>intact, smooth, not red</b>. Watch for <b>irritant dermatitis</b> (stool), <b>skin stripping</b> (adhesive), <b>candidiasis</b> (under the wafer). Choose the pouch for the abdomen: flat firm → flexible or nonflexible; creases → <b>flexible</b>; deep creases, soft abdomen, <b>retracted, flush or concave stoma</b> → <b>convex appliance with a belt</b> (pushes the stoma out, prevents leaks). The stoma <b>shrinks over 6–8 weeks</b> → <b>measure weekly</b> (and with weight change); cut the opening <b>⅛–1/16 inch larger</b> than the stoma.</div>\n<div><b>Changing the pouch</b> — clip or shave hair <b>away from the stoma</b>; clean with <b>mild soap and water</b> — <b>not moisturizing soap</b> (blocks adhesion); apply <b>skin sealant (preferably alcohol-free) and let it dry</b>; <b>stoma powder or paste</b> for raw skin, paste to fill creases; <b>antifungal cream or powder</b> for candidiasis.</div>\n<div><b>Gas and odor</b> — odor usually means a <b>leaking or poorly closed pouch</b>; <b>no foods are strictly forbidden</b>, but some (vegetables) add gas, or odor when the pouch is open. <b>Charcoal filters, pouch deodorizers, a breath mint</b> in the pouch, vented filters. <b>Never put aspirin in the pouch</b> — it can <b>ulcerate the stoma</b>. Avoid <b>gas-producing foods and carbonated drinks</b>; it can take <b>4–6 weeks</b> to learn how foods affect output. A <b>sigmoid</b> colostomy may be regulated by <b>irrigation</b> (like an enema through the stoma) or by diet.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">AP resection wound and recovery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Perineal wound</b> — usually closed with <b>two Jackson-Pratt bulb drains</b>. <b>Serosanguineous drainage for 1–2 months</b>; complete healing <b>6–8 months</b>; it can hurt <b>more than the abdominal incision</b>. <b>Phantom rectal sensations</b>, later pain and itching (benzocaine, warm compresses). Watch for <b>infection and abscess</b>. Box 48.5: an <b>absorbent pad</b> (a feminine napkin works) and <b>jockey-type briefs</b>; <b>sitz baths 10–20 minutes, 3–4 times a day</b> or warm compresses; analgesics; <b>side-lying</b> in bed; <b>avoid long sitting</b>; sit on <b>foam pads or a soft pillow — never an air ring or rubber donut</b>; <b>I&amp;O including wound drainage</b>; check for redness, swelling, bleeding, odor, constant pain.</div>\n<div><b>Going home</b> — no <b>heavy lifting or straining</b> (protects the anastomosis); open surgery → <b>no driving or vigorous activity for 4–6 weeks</b>; laparoscopic → usual activity in <b>1–2 weeks</b>. <b>Stool softeners</b>; note stool frequency and character; <b>report cramping, abdominal pain, nausea or vomiting</b> (obstruction, perforation). Store ostomy supplies where it is <b>neither hot nor cold</b> (barriers stiffen or melt).</div>\n<div><b>Teaching the ostomy</b> — practice time to <b>handle, assemble and apply</b> equipment; the <b>normal stoma</b>, complications, <b>measuring</b>, choosing and applying the appliance, <b>skin protection</b>, diet for gas and odor, expected stool, and <b>resuming work, travel and sex</b>. Home care (Box 48.6) checks intake, weight, bowel pattern, <b>stoma (size, color, retraction, prolapse, stenosis, hernia, ischemia)</b>, skin, appliance fit and coping.</div>\n<div><b>Feelings and resources</b> — fear of <b>rejection</b>, <b>grief over body image</b>, concerns about <b>sexuality</b> (all patients in one study reported sexual problems 6 months after rectal surgery — prepare them before surgery). Invite feelings, involve family, teach about <b>leaks, odor control and intimacy</b>. Refer to case managers and social workers; the <b>United Ostomy Associations of America (UOAA)</b> and its <b>ostomate visitor program</b> (with consent); the <b>American Cancer Society</b>; home health; <b>hospice</b> for advanced disease.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A stoma has <b>no nerve endings</b> for pain, so the patient can't feel it being cut by a too-small wafer opening or rubbed by a belt — and its color is the only window into its blood supply. That is why the opening is measured and cut slightly larger every week while it shrinks, and why a stoma that turns dark or dry is reported at once: it is the bowel itself, dying.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-irritable-bowel-syndrome","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Irritable bowel syndrome","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Irritable bowel syndrome</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — a <b>functional</b> GI disorder (no structural damage) with chronic or recurrent <b>diarrhea, constipation, or both</b>, plus <b>abdominal pain and bloating</b> (also called spastic, mucous or nervous colon). The <b>most common digestive disorder</b> in practice (<b>10–15%</b> worldwide); begins in <b>young adulthood</b>, lifelong. Types: <b>IBS-D</b> (diarrhea), <b>IBS-C</b> (constipation), <b>IBS-M</b> (mixed). In the US <b>women are affected twice as often</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Causes</b> — unclear — environmental (<b>caffeinated or carbonated drinks, dairy</b>), <b>small-bowel bacterial overgrowth</b> (bloating), immune and genetic factors (cytokines such as IL-6, IL-1β, TNF-α), hormones, and <b>stress</b>. Strong links to <b>anxiety and depression</b> — primary, or secondary to chronic pain and fear of having no bathroom nearby (staying home, avoiding travel).</div>\n<div><b>Assess</b> — fatigue, malaise, pain, <b>bowel pattern</b> and stool consistency, <b>mucus</b>; usually <b>no weight loss</b>; past GI infections; drugs that mimic IBS; <b>caffeine, sorbitol or fructose</b> drinks; triggers (diet, stress, anxiety); <b>dairy, raw fruit, grains</b> → bloating and gas; a <b>food diary</b>. Pain is often in the <b>left lower quadrant</b> (visceral hypersensitivity); nausea with meals or defecation; <b>small hard stools followed by several softer ones</b>, or soft watery stools with <b>mucus</b>; belching, gas, anorexia. The patient <b>looks well</b>; weight and fluids normal; bowel sounds normal (hypo with constipation, hyper with diarrhea).</div>\n<div><b>Tests</b> — routine labs are <b>normal</b> (CBC, albumin, ESR, occult blood). <b>Hydrogen breath test</b>: bacterial overgrowth or malabsorption makes <b>extra hydrogen</b>, which is exhaled. <b>NPO (water allowed) for at least 12 hours</b>; blow a baseline, drink <b>lactulose</b>, then breath samples <b>every 20 minutes for 1–2 hours</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Self-management</b> — outpatient: teaching, drugs, <b>stress reduction</b>, holistic care. <b>30–40 g of fiber a day</b>, <b>regular meals</b>, <b>8–10 glasses of water</b>, <b>chew slowly</b>; <b>regular exercise</b>. Stress: relaxation, meditation, yoga, counseling, problem solving. <b>Probiotics</b> for bacterial overgrowth; <b>peppermint oil capsules</b> may help.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs for IBS-C</b> — <b>psyllium</b> (bulk former) at meals with a glass of water; <b>lubiprostone</b> — for <b>women</b> with IBS-C, raises intestinal fluid — <b>with food and water</b>; <b>linaclotide</b> — stimulates guanylate cyclase receptors (more fluid, faster transit, less pain and cramping) — <b>once a day, about 30 minutes before breakfast</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs for IBS-D and pain</b> — <b>loperamide</b> and <b>psyllium</b>. <b>Alosetron</b> (5-HT₃ antagonist) — <b>women only, last resort</b> — can cause <b>ischemic colitis</b>: take a full drug history (many interactions), <b>no psychoactive drugs or antihistamines</b>, and <b>stop and report</b> severe constipation, fever, worsening pain or fatigue, dark urine, <b>bloody diarrhea or rectal bleeding</b>. <b>Rifaximin</b> (gut-acting antibiotic, barely absorbed) for bloating and IBS-D. <b>Tricyclic antidepressants</b> (amitriptyline) for pain — <b>30–45 minutes before meals</b> if pain follows eating.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Fiber helps <b>both</b> halves of IBS, which seems contradictory until you see what it does: soluble fiber like psyllium <b>holds water</b> — it softens hard stool and firms loose stool toward the same bulky, soft middle. That is why the same advice (fiber plus plenty of water) is given whether the patient's problem is constipation or diarrhea.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-abdominal-trauma","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Abdominal trauma","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Abdominal trauma</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Mechanisms</b> — the <b>3rd leading cause of death</b> from injury. <b>Blunt</b>: direct impact (punch, kick) or <b>sudden deceleration</b> (car crash, fall). <b>Penetrating</b>: <b>stab and gunshot wounds</b>. Injuries: hematomas, lacerations, ruptures of the stomach, <b>liver, spleen</b>, bladder and bowel.</div>\n<div><b>Complications</b> — two most common killers: <b>hemorrhage → hypovolemic shock</b> (liver or spleen; graded I–V) and <b>hollow-viscus perforation → peritonitis → septic shock</b>. Also abscess, paralytic ileus, bowel obstruction, and <b>abdominal compartment syndrome</b> — leaky capillaries → tissue edema → <b>intraabdominal hypertension</b> → pain, organ compression, ischemia, organ failure; often needs <b>surgical decompression</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Assess</b> — after the <b>primary survey</b> and resuscitation, learn the <b>mechanism of injury</b>. Inspect lying flat for abrasions, discoloration, distention; <b>significant palpation and percussion by the trained provider</b>; repeat often. <b>Box 48.7</b>: <b>Cullen sign</b> (purple around the <b>umbilicus</b> — blood in the abdominal wall); <b>Turner sign</b> (bruising of the <b>flank</b> — retroperitoneal bleeding or pancreatic injury); <b>flank hematoma</b> (kidney); <b>Kehr sign</b> (pain referred to the <b>left shoulder</b> — ruptured <b>spleen</b> or diaphragm irritation); <b>distention</b> (bleeding, ascites, leaking contents). Bowel sounds ↓ or absent; nausea, vomiting (± blood); frequent VS; <b>worsening pain</b> = bleeding, perforation or compartment syndrome.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnose and treat</b> — <b>FAST scan</b> at the bedside finds <b>free fluid</b> (blood, GI contents) fast; <b>CT</b> after resuscitation. Unstable or actively bleeding → straight to <b>surgery (exploratory laparotomy)</b>. Stable → <b>ICU</b> monitoring: focused assessments, pain, VS, <b>Hgb/Hct</b> for bleeding; <b>transfusion</b>; wound care for penetrating injury; oxygen or ventilation; <b>antibiotics</b> for hollow-viscus injury; laparotomy if they deteriorate; transfer out after <b>24–48 stable hours</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Kehr sign is referred pain: blood under the diaphragm irritates the <b>phrenic nerve</b>, which enters the spinal cord at the same level (C3–C5) as nerves from the shoulder — so the brain reports diaphragm irritation as <b>left shoulder pain</b>. A trauma patient with left shoulder pain and no shoulder injury may be bleeding from the spleen.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Hernias and hemorrhoids","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hernias</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and why</b> — bowel or another structure <b>protrudes through a weak spot</b> in the abdominal wall (or diaphragm). Two ingredients: <b>muscle weakness</b> (congenital or acquired) and <b>raised intraabdominal pressure</b> — <b>obesity, pregnancy, heavy lifting</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Types</b> — <b>indirect inguinal</b> (most common; mostly <b>men</b> — follows the tract of the descended testes, may reach the <b>scrotum</b>); <b>direct inguinal</b> (through a weak point; <b>older adults</b>); <b>femoral</b> (through the femoral ring; pregnant or obese women); <b>umbilical</b> (congenital, or acquired with <b>obesity</b>); <b>incisional (ventral)</b> at a past incision (infection, poor nutrition, obesity).</div>\n<div><b>Reducible, incarcerated, strangulated</b> — <b>reducible</b>: contents go back with gentle pressure. <b>Irreducible (incarcerated)</b>: can't be pushed back → <b>immediate surgical evaluation</b>. <b>Strangulated</b>: the hernial ring cuts off the <b>blood supply</b> → ischemia, obstruction, <b>necrosis, sepsis, perforation</b> — signs: <b>distention, nausea, vomiting, pain, fever, tachycardia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Assess</b> — a <b>lump</b> felt, often after straining or lifting. Inspect <b>lying and standing</b> (a reducible hernia may vanish lying flat); the provider has the patient <b>strain or cough</b> and feels the ring (men standing, finger up the spermatic cord). <b>Absent bowel sounds</b> → obstruction or strangulation = emergency. <b>Never force a hernia back</b> — strangulated bowel could rupture.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — non-surgical candidates: a <b>truss</b> (firm pad on a belt) applied <b>after the hernia is reduced</b>, put on <b>on waking</b>; check and lightly powder the skin daily. Surgery: <b>herniorrhaphy</b>, preferably <b>minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair (MIIHR)</b>; <b>hernioplasty</b> reinforces the wall with <b>mesh</b>; strangulation may need <b>bowel resection or colostomy</b>. The <b>most important</b> pre-op teaching is to stay <b>NPO</b> for the hours the surgeon specifies; for same-day surgery arrange <b>a ride and an adult at home</b> for the rest of the day.</div>\n<div><b>After repair (Box 48.8)</b> — home in <b>3–5 hours</b> after MIIHR. VS for <b>internal bleeding</b>; analgesics (report severe uncontrolled pain); <b>deep breathing and incentive spirometry but avoid coughing</b>; walk within hours; <b>ice packs</b>; indirect inguinal repair → <b>scrotal support, ice and elevation</b> on a pillow. <b>Help men stand to void</b>; report <b>urine output &lt; 30 mL/hr</b>; running water; <b>1500–2500 mL fluid a day</b>; straight catheter if unable to void. Teach: rest several days and avoid strenuous activity; <b>soreness is expected, severe pain is not</b>; a <b>stool softener</b> may be needed; <b>no driving or operating machinery while taking opioids</b>; report <b>fever, chills, redness, swelling, heat, drainage, separation or increasing pain</b> at the incisions; keep them clean and dry; <b>shower 24–36 hours after removing the bandage</b>, leave closure strips (fall off in ~1 week); <b>lift no more than 10 lb</b> until cleared, and avoid straining and lifting for several weeks; fiber and fluids (no constipation); work in <b>1–2 weeks</b>; follow-up call the next day.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hemorrhoids</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and why</b> — swollen anorectal veins (normally part of continence). Raised <b>intraabdominal pressure</b> → raised venous pressure, plus arterioles shunting blood in → veins distend and <b>separate from their supporting muscle → prolapse</b>. <b>Internal</b> (above the sphincter, not visible) or <b>external</b> (below, visible); prolapsed ones can <b>thrombose, inflame or bleed</b>. Worse with <b>pregnancy, constipation and straining, obesity, heart failure, prolonged sitting or standing</b>, strenuous exercise and weightlifting, <b>low fluid intake</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention and signs</b> — prevent <b>constipation</b>: whole grains, raw fruits and vegetables, <b>water</b> (unless heart or kidney disease limits fluids), <b>no straining</b>, gradual exercise, healthy weight. Signs: <b>bleeding</b> — <b>bright red on the toilet tissue or streaked on stool</b> — swelling, <b>prolapse</b>, <b>pain</b> (especially sudden <b>thrombosis</b>), itching, mucous discharge; diagnosed by inspection and digital exam.</div>\n<div><b>Conservative care</b> — <b>cold packs</b> a few minutes at a time from the onset of pain and <b>tepid sitz baths 3–4 times a day</b>; <b>topical lidocaine</b> for severe pain; OTC <b>dibucaine</b> only <b>short term</b> (can mask a worse disorder); <b>hydrocortisone</b> for itching and inflammation; <b>moist cleansing tissues</b> and <b>dab, don't wipe</b>; <b>high fiber and fluids</b>; <b>docusate</b> short term; <b>no irritating laxatives</b>; <b>avoid long sitting</b>; mild oral analgesics. Relief expected in <b>3–5 days</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Hemorrhoidectomy</b> — outpatient removal (ultrasound, laser, or a <b>circular stapler</b> for prolapse). Complications: pain, thrombosis, infection, <b>bleeding</b>, abscess. <b>Moist heat</b> (sitz baths, warm compresses) 3–4 times a day; <b>stool softeners</b> before and after; analgesics and anti-inflammatories; a <b>mild laxative if no BM by day 3</b>; avoid <b>habit-forming stimulant laxatives</b>. <b>Action alert: the first bowel movement can be very painful</b> — make sure someone is <b>with or near</b> the patient: <b>vasovagal</b> light-headedness, sweating and <b>syncope</b> can occur.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Hernias and hemorrhoids share a cause — <b>pressure from inside the abdomen</b> — which is why the same teaching prevents and protects both: avoid constipation and straining, lift with care, keep a healthy weight, and after surgery avoid the coughing, lifting and straining that would push on a fresh repair.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-adpie","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Every patient in this chapter is watched for the same turning points — <b>fluid loss</b>, <b>pain that changes character</b>, and <b>signs that blood supply to the bowel is failing</b> — while being taught the elimination habits (fiber, fluids, no straining, timely screening) that prevent obstruction, hemorrhoids, hernias and late-found cancer.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>GI disease and <b>past surgery</b> (adhesions); vomiting and its color; <b>last bowel movement and flatus</b>; stool shape, blood and color; weight loss and fatigue; <b>family history</b> of CRC, FAP or Lynch; screening history; diet, fiber, fluids, laxative use; mechanism of any injury; triggers and stress in IBS; lifting or straining with a lump.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div>vital signs and <b>temperature</b> (&gt; 100°F with tachycardia = strangulation?); distention, visible peristalsis; bowel sounds (<b>borborygmi</b> above a block, then silence; listen with suction off); pain character; rigidity and guarding; <b>Cullen, Turner and Kehr</b> signs; a hernia lying and standing; <b>stoma color, size and skin</b>; perineal wound; hemorrhoids; hydration and daily weight.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Labs and tests</div><b>Hgb, Hct, BUN</b> (dehydration, bleeding), <b>Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻</b>, WBC (strangulation), amylase; FIT/FOBT; <b>CEA</b>; CT/MRI, ultrasound, <b>FAST</b>; colonoscopy; hydrogen breath test.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>obstruction: <b>potential for life-threatening complications</b> (hypovolemia, electrolyte imbalance, strangulation, perforation, sepsis). Colorectal surgery: <b>infection</b> (AP resection perineal wound), stoma complications, body image, sexuality and grief. Trauma: <b>hemorrhage and shock</b>. Hernia: <b>strangulation</b>; urinary retention after repair. IBS: pain, altered elimination, anxiety.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>the obstruction resolves with normal elimination and <b>no life-threatening complications</b>; fluid and electrolytes stay balanced (<b>stable weight</b>, urine output ≥ 30 mL/hr); the stoma stays <b>pink and moist</b> with intact skin and the patient manages the pouch; the perineal wound heals without infection; IBS symptoms are controlled; screening happens on schedule.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Obstruction</div><b>NPO</b>; <b>NG to low continuous suction</b> (placement, patency, output <b>every 4 hours</b>; irrigate 30 mL NS as ordered; <b>bowel sounds with suction off</b>); <b>IV isotonic fluid with K⁺</b>; <b>daily weight</b>, I&amp;O, VS every 2–4 hours; mouth and nares care; <b>semi-Fowler</b>; analgesia as allowed; <b>alvimopan</b> for post-op ileus; report <b>pain that becomes constant</b>, fever &gt; 100°F, tachycardia or rigidity; prepare for surgery if complete or strangulated.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Colorectal surgery and ostomy</div>CWOCN site marking; discuss colostomy possibility and sexual and urinary risks; antibiotic before incision; post-op <b>stoma checks</b> (report purple, black, dry, heavy bleeding, separation); empty at <b>⅓–½ full</b>; <b>measure weekly</b> and cut <b>⅛–1/16 inch larger</b>; <b>alcohol-free sealant</b>; <b>no aspirin in the pouch</b>; perineal care — <b>sitz baths, side-lying, foam cushion not a donut</b>, JP drains, I&amp;O; no lifting or straining; <b>UOAA visitor</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">IBS, trauma, hernia, hemorrhoids</div>IBS: <b>fiber 30–40 g, 8–10 glasses of water</b>, exercise, stress care, drugs by type (<b>alosetron warnings</b>). Trauma: <b>primary survey</b>, FAST, VS and Hgb/Hct, transfusion, antibiotics, prepare for laparotomy. Hernia: <b>never force reduction</b>; after repair <b>deep breathe, don't cough</b>, ice, <b>scrotal support</b>, <b>stand to void</b>, report UO &lt; 30 mL/hr, ≤ 10 lb lifting. Hemorrhoids: <b>cold packs, sitz baths</b>, fiber and fluids, softeners; <b>stay near for the first post-op BM</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>bowel sounds and flatus return and the patient tolerates diet; weight, electrolytes and urine output are stable; no fever, rigidity or new constant pain; the stoma stays healthy and the patient can <b>demonstrate pouch care</b> and state complications to report; the perineal wound drains less over weeks without infection; IBS flares are fewer; the patient knows their <b>screening schedule</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"49-overview","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">49 · Chapter 49</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Infection · inflammation · nutrition, elimination &amp; pain</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Inflammatory intestinal conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> This chapter is about the bowel when it is <b>inflamed or infected</b>. The acute problems — <b>peritonitis</b> (the infection exemplar), <b>appendicitis</b> and <b>gastroenteritis</b> — can become life-threatening within hours. The chronic ones — <b>ulcerative colitis</b> (the inflammation exemplar), <b>Crohn's disease</b> and <b>diverticular disease</b> — come and go for years, along with <b>celiac disease</b> and <b>parasitic infections</b>. The common thread for the nurse: inflammation pulls <b>fluid</b> out of the circulation, chronic diarrhea and malabsorption drain <b>nutrition and electrolytes</b>, digestive enzymes injure <b>skin</b>, and the <b>pain</b> pattern tells you when a local problem has become peritonitis.</p><div id=\"ch49-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">The small intestine digests food and absorbs nutrients; the large intestine reabsorbs water to keep fluid balance; the peritoneal cavity holds about 50 mL of sterile fluid so organs glide during peristalsis; inflammation normally walls off contamination.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Bacteria or chemicals (bile, pancreatic enzymes, gastric acid) reach the peritoneum, usually by perforation; a fecalith blocks the appendix; viruses or bacteria inflame the mucosa; immune-driven chronic inflammation (UC continuous in the lining from the rectum, Crohn's patchy through all layers); food or bacteria trapped in diverticula; gluten triggers an autoimmune attack; parasites invade.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Rigid, boardlike abdomen, rebound tenderness, fever, tachycardia; periumbilical pain that moves to the McBurney point; vomiting then diarrhea and dehydration; 10–20 bloody stools with tenesmus (UC); 5–6 loose nonbloody stools, steatorrhea, weight loss, fistulas (Crohn's); left lower quadrant pain with fever (diverticulitis).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">WBC elevation (about 20,000 in peritonitis; 10,000–18,000 with a shift to the left in appendicitis); blood cultures; x-ray free air; ultrasound and CT; CRP and ESR; Hgb/Hct, albumin, electrolytes; MR enterography; colonoscopy; stool analysis for parasites; celiac blood test with endoscopy.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Broad-spectrum antibiotics, IV fluids, NG decompression and surgery (laparotomy or laparoscopy, appendectomy, colon resection); oral rehydration; 5-ASA drugs, steroids, immunomodulators and biologics; bowel rest and TPN; RPC-IPAA or ileostomy; fiber timing in diverticular disease; gluten-free diet; metronidazole for parasites.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Third spacing → hypovolemic shock → acute kidney injury; ileus; bacteremia → septic shock; breathing compromise; perforation; toxic megacolon; GI hemorrhage; fistulas, abscesses and malnutrition; colorectal cancer after 10 years of UC.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A boardlike abdomen, or pain that suddenly eases then spreads (perforation); fever with tachycardia and falling blood pressure or urine output (sepsis); bright red or tarry stools with falling Hgb; a pale, gray, bluish or dark stoma; an ileostomy that stops draining; new confusion, weakness or numbness on natalizumab (PML).</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Bacteria or chemicals reach the peritoneum<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>inflammation tries to wall it off<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>walling off fails → diffuse infection<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>leaky vessels shift fluid into the abdomen (third spacing)<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>hypovolemia → kidney injury<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>bacteria enter the blood → septic shock</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Ulcerative colitis vs Crohn's disease (Table 49.2)</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>UC</b>: starts in the <b>rectum</b> and spreads <b>continuously</b> toward the cecum, in the <b>lining</b>; <b>10–20 liquid, bloody stools</b> a day; <b>hemorrhage</b> is the classic complication; peaks at 15–35 and 55–70; <b>25–35%</b> need surgery (and it works well). <b>Crohn's</b>: most often the <b>terminal ileum</b>, <b>patchy (skip lesions) through all layers</b>; <b>5–6 soft, loose, nonbloody</b> stools; <b>fistulas</b> (common) and <b>malabsorption</b>; peaks at 15–40; <b>65–75%</b> need surgery (less successful). <b>Both</b> list <b>nutritional deficiencies</b> as a complication.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Appendicitis vs gastroenteritis — which came first?</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Pain first, then nausea and vomiting</b> → think <b>appendicitis</b>. <b>Nausea and vomiting first, then cramping and diarrhea</b> → think <b>gastroenteritis</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Diverticulosis vs diverticulitis</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Diverticulosis</b>: pouches, usually <b>no symptoms</b> → prevent with a <b>high-fiber</b> diet. <b>Diverticulitis</b>: an <b>inflamed or infected</b> pouch → LLQ pain, fever, ↑ WBC → <b>low fiber, clear liquids or NPO</b> while active, antibiotics, rest, <b>no laxatives or enemas</b>; fiber returns <b>gradually</b> once it heals.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Sulfasalazine</b> is split by gut bacteria into <b>5-ASA</b> (calms the lining) and <b>sulfapyridine</b> (most side effects, and the <b>sulfa</b>) → check sulfa allergy, give <b>folic acid</b>. <b>Mesalamine</b> is the 5-ASA alone → better tolerated. <b>Infliximab and adalimumab</b> block <b>TNF</b> → infection risk → <b>avoid crowds</b>. <b>Natalizumab</b> stops white cells from entering tissue → risk of <b>PML</b> in the brain. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">VS (fever, tachycardia, hypotension, narrowing pulse pressure = sepsis), pain and the abdomen (rigidity, rebound, distention, bowel sounds), <b>I&amp;O and daily weight</b> (hourly I&amp;O right after abdominal surgery), mental status (the first sign of dehydration in older adults), stools (number, blood, mucus, fat), Hgb/Hct, WBC, CRP/ESR, K⁺ and Mg²⁺, albumin, stoma color and output, perianal and peristomal skin, fistula output.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A <b>rigid, boardlike abdomen</b>; pain that eases then becomes diffuse (perforation); fever &gt; 101°F with tachycardia; hypotension or falling urine output (septic or hypovolemic shock); <b>frank or tarry blood</b> with falling Hgb — call the <b>Rapid Response Team</b>; a <b>pale, gray, bluish or dark stoma</b>; an ileostomy that stops draining with cramps; new cognitive, motor or sensory changes on natalizumab.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why peritonitis empties the circulation</b> — inflammation <b>dilates vessels and makes them leaky</b> so white cells can reach the bacteria. If walling off fails, blood is shunted to the area (<b>hyperemia</b>) and fluid shifts from the extracellular space into the peritoneal cavity, connective tissue and GI tract (<b>third spacing</b>) → <b>hypovolemic shock</b> → poor kidney perfusion → <b>acute kidney injury</b>. <b>Peristalsis stops</b>, and the <b>7–8 L</b> of fluid that normally flows on for reabsorption collects in the bowel. Toxins or bacteria entering the blood cause <b>septicemia → septic shock</b>. Distention pushes on the <b>diaphragm</b> and pain limits breathing just when oxygen demand is high.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why the appendix perforates</b> — the lumen is <b>blocked, usually by a fecalith</b>; the lining keeps secreting, <b>pressure rises</b> and <b>blood flow is cut off</b> → pain and <b>bacteria invade the wall</b>. Slowly → an <b>abscess</b>; quickly → <b>peritonitis</b>. <b>Gangrene and sepsis within 24–36 hours</b>; <b>perforation within 24 hours</b>, with risk rising rapidly <b>after 48 hours</b> (temperature &gt; 101°F and a rising pulse).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Lining vs all layers</b> — <b>UC</b> inflames the <b>mucosa</b>: it becomes <b>hyperemic, edematous and red</b>, then <b>bleeds</b> and forms <b>erosions, ulcers and abscesses</b> — hence <b>bloody stools with mucus</b> and tenesmus; thickening can narrow the colon. <b>Crohn's</b> goes <b>through all layers (transmural)</b>: the wall <b>thickens</b>, <b>strictures and deep \"cobblestone\" ulcers</b> form, and the inflammation <b>tunnels into neighbors as fistulas</b> — with <b>malabsorption</b>, steatorrhea and anemia. In both, <b>TNF-alpha and interleukins</b> drive the damage — which is why drugs that block TNF work.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why diverticula form and get infected</b> — a <b>low-fiber</b> diet makes small, hard stools; the colon muscle <b>thickens and stiffens</b>, and <b>high pressure inside</b> pushes the lining out through <b>weak points where blood vessels cross the muscle</b> — usually the <b>sigmoid</b>. <b>Undigested food or bacteria trapped</b> in a pouch reduce its blood supply, bacteria invade → <b>diverticulitis</b> → <b>perforation, abscess, peritonitis</b> or bleeding.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>In this chapter the body's defense is also the danger. Inflammation makes vessels <b>leaky</b> on purpose, so white cells can reach and wall off bacteria. When walling off fails, the same leak pours fluid into the abdomen — which is how a patient with peritonitis can go into <b>hypovolemic shock without losing a drop you can see</b>. So peritonitis is treated as a <b>fluid problem</b> as much as an infection: hourly I&amp;O after surgery, daily weights and IV fluids go alongside the antibiotics.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Peritonitis — the infection exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is and what causes it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The peritoneum</b> — the peritoneal cavity normally holds about <b>50 mL of sterile fluid</b> (transudate) that prevents friction during peristalsis. <b>Peritonitis</b> is a life-threatening, acute <b>inflammation and infection</b> of the visceral and parietal peritoneum and the lining of the abdominal cavity. As a <b>post-op complication</b> its mortality is <b>up to 50%</b>.</div>\n<div><b>How contamination gets in</b> — most often <b>bacteria or chemicals</b>. Bacteria enter by <b>perforation</b> (appendicitis, diverticulitis, <b>peptic ulcer</b>), a <b>penetrating wound</b>, a <b>gangrenous gallbladder or bowel segment</b>, <b>bowel obstruction</b>, or infection ascending through the <b>genital tract</b>; less often invasive tumors, leakage or contamination <b>during surgery</b>, or skin organisms in <b>continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD)</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Organisms and chemicals</b> — common bacteria: <b><i>Escherichia coli</b></i>, <b><i>Streptococcus</b></i>, <b><i>Staphylococcus</b></i>, <b>pneumococci</b>, <b>gonococci</b>. <b>Chemical peritonitis</b> comes from leaking <b>bile, pancreatic enzymes or gastric acid</b>. There is <b>no genetic risk</b>. Most common in <b>young adults with appendicitis</b> and in <b>older adults</b>, whose immunity may be lower.</div>\n<div><b>Walled off or spread</b> — the body first <b>walls off</b> a localized area: vessels dilate and capillaries leak so <b>leukocytes</b> arrive and <b>phagocytose</b> the organisms. If walling off fails, contamination becomes massive → <b>diffuse (generalized) peritonitis</b>, third spacing, ileus and possible <b>sepsis</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it (Box 49.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — ask about <b>abdominal pain</b> — its <b>character</b> (cramping, sharp, aching), <b>location</b>, and whether it is <b>localized or generalized</b> — and a history of <b>low-grade fever</b> or recent <b>temperature spikes</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Key features</b> — <b>rigid, boardlike abdomen</b> (classic); abdominal pain — localized, poorly localized, or <b>referred to the shoulder or chest</b>; <b>distention</b>; nausea, anorexia, vomiting; <b>diminishing bowel sounds</b>; <b>no flatus or feces</b>; <b>rebound tenderness</b>; <b>high fever</b>; <b>tachycardia</b>; <b>dehydration</b> from fever (poor skin turgor); <b>decreased urine output</b>; <b>hiccups</b>; possible <b>respiratory compromise</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Cardinal signs</b> — <b>abdominal pain, tenderness and distention</b>. <b>Localized</b> peritonitis → a tender abdomen with rebound tenderness in <b>one well-defined area</b>; <b>generalized</b> peritonitis → tenderness is <b>widespread</b>.</div>\n<div><b>How the patient looks</b> — acutely ill, <b>lying still, possibly with the knees flexed</b>; movement is <b>guarded</b>; grimacing with <b>coughing or any movement</b>. Watch for <b>progressive distention</b> as motility falls; bowel sounds usually <b>disappear</b> as it progresses.</div>\n<div><b>Feelings</b> — fear and anxiety about the diagnosis and distress from the pain → provide a <b>calm presence</b>, reassure them you will be there, let them <b>express fear</b>, and listen without judgment.</div>\n<div><b>Labs</b> — <b>WBC often about 20,000/mm³</b> with many neutrophils (normal <b>5000–10,000</b>). <b>Blood cultures</b> — has septicemia occurred, and which antibiotic? <b>BUN, creatinine, Hgb and Hct</b> for fluid, electrolyte and kidney status; <b>SpO₂ and ABGs</b> for breathing and acid-base balance.</div>\n<div><b>Imaging</b> — <b>abdominal x-ray</b>: <b>free air or fluid = perforation</b>; also dilation, edema and inflammation of the bowel. <b>Ultrasound or CT</b> may follow.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. <b>Acute pain</b> from abdominal inflammation and infection. 2. <b>Potential for fluid volume shift</b> — fluid moving into the interstitial or peritoneal space.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Treating it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Watch for septic shock</b> — patients are <b>hospitalized</b> — in <b>critical care</b> if complications are extensive. Treatment targets the <b>cause</b> and the <b>infection</b>, usually nonsurgically at first. Check <b>vital signs often</b> for septic shock: <b>unresolved or progressive hypotension</b>, <b>decreased pulse pressure</b>, <b>tachycardia</b>, fever, skin changes, <b>tachypnea</b>; and <b>mental status</b> — confusion or a lower level of consciousness. Expected outcome: pain <b>2–3 on a 0–10 scale</b> (or acceptable) as the infection resolves.</div>\n<div><b>Infection control</b> — <b>handwashing</b> and <b>strict asepsis</b> with wounds, drains and dressings; <b>sterile technique</b> and catheter care for a urinary catheter; <b>document wound drainage and report changes immediately</b>; give <b>broad-spectrum antibiotics</b> as prescribed; <b>oxygen</b> by SpO₂ and respiratory status.</div>\n<div><b>Fluid balance</b> — the provider orders <b>hypertonic IV fluids and broad-spectrum antibiotics immediately</b> after diagnosis <i>(the book's wording here; lost volume is usually replaced with isotonic fluids such as normal saline or lactated Ringer's, as in chapter 47)</i>. <b>Daily weight on the same scale each morning</b> (remind assistive personnel) and careful <b>I&amp;O</b>; <b>NG tube</b> to decompress the stomach and intestine if an <b>open laparotomy</b> is expected. Fluid moves to the peritoneal cavity, and <b>NG suction and NPO</b> add losses → IV replacement, with rates changed by labs and assessment. <b>Compare irrigation fluid returned with fluid instilled</b> — retained fluid causes distention or pain. The day after a rupture or abdominal surgery, <b>fluid and electrolyte status is the priority assessment</b> (NCLEX Challenge 49.1).</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — needed to find and repair the cause, remove abscesses or foreign material, and <b>drain fluid</b> — <b>delayed</b> if the patient is too critically ill. <b>Exploratory laparotomy</b> (large incision) or <b>laparoscopy</b>: e.g., <b>appendectomy</b>, or <b>colon resection with or without colostomy</b> for a perforated diverticulum. The surgeon <b>irrigates the peritoneum with antibiotic solution</b> and may leave <b>drains</b> for irrigation after surgery. Infected open wounds may heal by <b>second or third intention</b>, needing irrigation or packing; use <b>sterile technique</b> for peritoneal irrigation.</div>\n<div><b>Right after surgery (Action Alert)</b> — monitor <b>level of consciousness, vital signs, respiratory status (rate, effort, breath sounds) and I&amp;O at least hourly</b>. Keep the patient in <b>semi-Fowler's position</b> — it lets peritoneal contents <b>drain into the lower abdomen</b> and helps the <b>lungs expand</b>. Routine post-op care (Chapter 9) applies too — <b>oxygen until fully awake</b>, <b>incentive spirometry</b>, <b>sequential compression devices</b> — and with NPO status and NG suction the patient keeps <b>IV fluids and IV antibiotics</b> (NGN Challenge 49.1).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>After a ruptured abdominal abscess or peritonitis surgery, the wound is not the first thing that kills — the <b>fluid shift</b> is. Fluid is leaving the circulation into the abdomen while NG suction and NPO add losses, so the textbook's <b>priority assessment is fluid and electrolyte status</b>. Semi-Fowler's position is the companion move: gravity keeps infected fluid low in the pelvis instead of under the diaphragm, where it would also crowd the lungs.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Going home</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>How long</b> — depends on how far the infection spread: a <b>drained localized abscess</b> that responds to antibiotics and IV fluids → home in <b>several days</b>; others need <b>mechanical ventilation or hemodialysis</b>, or a <b>transitional care unit</b> to finish antibiotics. Recovery takes longer than after other surgeries (multisystem involvement).</div>\n<div><b>Report right away</b> — <b>unusual or foul-smelling drainage</b>; <b>swelling, redness or hyperpigmentation, warmth or bleeding</b> at the incision; <b>temperature above 101°F (38.3°C)</b>; <b>abdominal pain</b>; signs of <b>wound dehiscence or ileus</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Wound and help at home</b> — large incisions left open heal by <b>secondary or tertiary intention</b> and need dressings, solution and <b>catheter-tipped syringes</b> to irrigate; a <b>home care nurse</b> can assess, irrigate or pack until the family is comfortable; a home care aide or a <b>skilled care facility</b> if ADLs need help.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs and activity</b> — review antibiotics and analgesics. With short-term opioids such as <b>oxycodone with acetaminophen</b>, a <b>stool softener (docusate)</b> may be given with a <b>laxative</b>; <b>older adults</b> constipate easily on <b>codeine-based</b> drugs; <b>no extra acetaminophen</b> (liver toxicity). <b>No lifting over 5–10 lb (2.3–4.5 kg) for at least 6 weeks</b> after open surgery; after <b>laparoscopy</b>, usual activity in <b>1–2 weeks</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — the patient <b>verbalizes relief or control of pain</b> as the infection resolves and has <b>fluid and electrolyte balance</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"49-appendicitis","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Appendicitis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What happens and how it shows</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — acute inflammation of the <b>vermiform appendix</b>, which hangs off the proximal <b>cecum</b> just below the <b>ileocecal valve</b>. It peaks at <b>ages 15–19</b> but can occur at any age.</div>\n<div><b>Why it happens</b> — the lumen is <b>obstructed</b>, usually by a <b>fecalith</b> (very hard piece of feces); the lining secretes fluid, <b>pressure rises</b> and <b>blood flow falls</b> → pain and bacterial invasion of the wall. A slow process → <b>abscess</b>; a fast one → <b>peritonitis</b>. <b>Gangrene and sepsis can occur within 24–36 hours</b>; <b>perforation within 24 hours</b>, with the risk rising rapidly <b>after 48 hours</b>. Perforation → peritonitis with <b>temperature &gt; 101°F (38.3°C)</b> and a <b>rising pulse</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The order of symptoms</b> — track the sequence: <b>abdominal pain followed by nausea and vomiting</b> suggests <b>appendicitis</b>; <b>nausea or vomiting before pain</b> suggests <b>gastroenteritis</b>. Classically, <b>cramping pain in the epigastric or periumbilical area</b>; <b>anorexia</b> is frequent.</div>\n<div><b>Where the pain moves</b> — pain can start anywhere in the abdomen or flank; as it progresses it becomes <b>more severe and shifts to the RLQ</b>, at the <b>McBurney point</b> (Fig. 49.1) — <b>midway between the anterior iliac crest and the umbilicus</b>. Pain that <b>gets worse with coughing or moving</b> and <b>eases when the right hip or the knees are flexed</b> points to <b>perforation and peritonitis</b>. So does RLQ pain that <b>suddenly eases for a while, then returns spread across the abdomen</b>, with fever, a rising pulse and falling blood pressure — the picture in <b>NGN Challenge 49.1</b> (perforated appendix → peritonitis). The provider checks for <b>rigidity and guarding</b>; pain after pressure is released is <b>rebound tenderness</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Labs and imaging</b> — labs <b>don't establish</b> the diagnosis, but the <b>WBC is often 10,000–18,000/mm³</b> with a <b>\"shift to the left\"</b> (more immature WBCs); a <b>WBC &gt; 20,000</b> may mean a <b>perforated</b> appendix. <b>Ultrasound</b> may show an <b>enlarged appendix</b>; <b>CT</b> if symptoms are recurrent or prolonged (may show a <b>fecaloma</b>).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Care (Action Alert)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Before surgery</b> — all suspected or confirmed cases are <b>hospitalized</b>. Keep the patient <b>NPO</b> — surgery is likely and food would worsen the inflammation. Give <b>IV fluids</b> for fluid and electrolyte balance; <b>semi-Fowler's</b> as tolerated so drainage stays in the <b>lower abdomen</b>. <b>Manage pain adequately before surgery</b>; once the diagnosis is confirmed and surgery scheduled, give <b>opioid analgesics and antibiotics</b> as prescribed.</div>\n<div><b>Never</b> — <b>no laxatives or enemas</b> — they can <b>perforate</b> the appendix. <b>No heat</b> to the abdomen — it increases circulation to the appendix → <b>more inflammation and perforation</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — <b>appendectomy as soon as possible</b>. Uncomplicated cases: <b>laparoscopy</b> (small incisions near the umbilicus) — few complications, usually <b>home the same day</b> with mild to moderate pain, back to usual activity in <b>1–2 weeks</b>. Unclear diagnosis but high risk → <b>exploratory laparotomy</b> (large incision). Pre-op teaching is limited by pain and urgency; post-op care is that of any general anesthesia.</div>\n<div><b>If peritonitis or abscess is found</b> — open surgery with <b>wound drains</b> and possibly an <b>NG tube</b> (decompress, prevent distention); <b>IV antibiotics and opioids</b>; get the patient <b>out of bed the evening of surgery</b> to prevent <b>atelectasis</b>; hospital stay <b>3–5 days</b>, usual activity in <b>4–6 weeks</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every \"never\" in appendicitis comes from the same <b>pressure problem</b>. The blocked appendix is a closed tube whose wall is already starved of blood by rising pressure. A <b>laxative or enema</b> drives more contents and motility against it; <b>heat</b> brings in more blood and swelling. Either one raises pressure in a wall that is about to give way — and rupture turns a local infection into <b>peritonitis</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"49-gastroenteritis","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Gastroenteritis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Types and how it spreads (Table 49.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — diarrhea and/or vomiting from <b>inflammation of the mucous membranes of the stomach and intestinal tract</b>; the <b>small bowel</b> is most often affected; <b>viral is more common</b> than bacterial. Usually <b>self-limiting, about 3 days</b>; in the <b>immunosuppressed and older adults</b>, <b>dehydration and hypovolemia</b> may need medical care or hospitalization.</div>\n<div><b>Norovirus</b> — the <b>leading foodborne cause</b> of gastroenteritis; most common <b>November to April</b> (resists low temperatures, long viral shedding <b>before and after</b> illness). Spread <b>fecal-oral</b> person to person, by contaminated <b>food, water, surfaces and objects</b>; <b>vomiting can make it airborne</b>. Incubation <b>1–2 days (about 48 hours)</b>; affects all adult ages; <b>older adults</b> can become hypovolemic with electrolyte imbalances. Outbreaks: <b>prisons, cruise ships, nursing homes, college dorms</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Epidemic viral</b> — caused by many <b>parvovirus-type</b> organisms; <b>fecal-oral</b> in food and water; incubation <b>10–51 hours</b>; communicable during the acute illness.</div>\n<div><b>Bacterial</b> — <b><i>Campylobacter</i> enteritis</b>: fecal-oral or contact with <b>infected animals or infants</b>; incubation <b>1–10 days</b>; communicable <b>2–7 weeks</b>. <b><i>E. coli</i> diarrhea</b>: fecal contamination of food, water or <b>fomites</b>. <b>Shigellosis</b>: direct and indirect fecal-oral; incubation <b>1–7 days</b>; communicable until <b>4 weeks after</b> the illness; people may be <b>carriers for months</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing and treating it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — usually self-managed at home; hospitalized if severe or very dehydrated. Ask about <b>recent travel</b> — especially tropical <b>Asia, Africa, Mexico, Central or South America</b>; <b>restaurants in the past 24–36 hours</b>; produce (outbreaks from <b>contaminated spinach and lettuce</b>); <b>raw or undercooked</b> food such as <b>oysters, sushi and rare meat</b>.</div>\n<div><b>How it shows</b> — the patient looks ill; <b>nausea and vomiting come first, then abdominal cramping and diarrhea</b>. Older or immunocompromised patients may develop <b>weakness and cardiac dysrhythmias from hypokalemia</b>. Note <b>abdominal distention</b> and listen for <b>hyperactive bowel sounds</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Dehydration (Action Alert)</b> — signs: unintentional <b>weight loss</b>, <b>poor skin turgor</b>, fever (<b>not common in older adults</b>), <b>dry mucous membranes</b>, <b>orthostatic BP changes</b> (can cause <b>falls</b>), <b>hypotension</b>, <b>oliguria</b>. In older adults dehydration can be severe and fast, and <b>acute confusion</b> (from hypoxia) may be the <b>only early sign</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fluids</b> — for any type, encourage <b>oral fluid replacement</b> — extra fluids to replace vomiting and diarrhea. <b>Oral rehydration therapy (ORT)</b> such as <b>sports drinks or Pedialyte</b> replaces fluids and electrolytes; <b>IV fluids</b> in the hospital depending on age and severity.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — <b>drugs that slow the gut may not be given</b> — they can keep the organism from being <b>eliminated</b>; if the provider decides one is needed, <b>loperamide</b> may be recommended. <b>Diphenoxylate with atropine</b> is used sparingly — it is <b>habit-forming</b> and should <b>not be used in older adults</b> (drowsiness → <b>falls</b>). <b>Antibiotics</b> for bacterial infection with <b>fever and severe diarrhea</b>: <b>ciprofloxacin or azithromycin</b> (also for <b>shigellosis</b>).</div>\n<div><b>Perianal skin</b> — stools rich in <b>electrolytes and enzymes</b> plus frequent wiping irritate the skin: <b>avoid toilet paper</b>; clean gently with <b>warm water</b> or absorbent material and dry thoroughly; a little cream, oil or gel on a damp warm washcloth lifts stuck stool; <b>protective barrier cream</b> between stools; <b>sitz baths 10 minutes, 2–3 times a day</b>. Leakage → absorbent pad held by snug underwear; incontinent at night → <b>pads instead of briefs</b> so air reaches the skin. Remind <b>assistive personnel</b> to keep the <b>perineal and buttock area clean and dry</b>, changing pads often.</div>\n<div><b>Stop the spread (Box 49.2)</b> — <b>wash hands for at least 30 seconds with soap</b>, especially after a bowel movement; keep <b>glasses, dishes, utensils and toothpaste</b> for personal use (disposables if severe); keep bathrooms clean; tell the provider if symptoms last <b>beyond 3 days</b>; <b>don't prepare or handle food</b> for others — <b>food handlers</b> consult the <b>public health department</b> before returning to work. Prevention: handwashing, <b>sanitizing surfaces</b>, hand sanitizer, proper food storage.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Why not just stop the diarrhea? In infectious gastroenteritis the diarrhea is partly how the body <b>gets rid of the organism</b>. A drug that slows the gut keeps the virus or bacteria inside longer — so the nurse <b>replaces what is lost</b> (fluid and potassium) instead of blocking the exit, and uses an antidiarrheal only when the provider decides it is needed.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Ulcerative colitis — the inflammation exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What happens</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Where and how</b> — widespread chronic inflammation of the <b>rectum and rectosigmoid colon</b> that can extend to the <b>entire colon</b>, spreading <b>continuously</b> from the rectum toward the cecum; the distribution can stay the same for years. <b>Remissions and exacerbations (flare-ups)</b>; <b>intestinal infection</b> can trigger flares. Most patients have <b>mild to moderate</b> disease. The lining becomes <b>hyperemic, edematous and red</b>; with more severe inflammation it <b>bleeds</b> and forms <b>erosions (ulcers)</b>; <b>abscesses</b> in the ulcers lead to <b>necrosis</b>; edema and thickening can <b>narrow the colon</b> and cause a partial obstruction.</div>\n<div><b>Signs</b> — stools with <b>blood and mucus</b>; <b>tenesmus</b> (unpleasant, urgent need to defecate); <b>lower abdominal colicky pain relieved by defecation</b>; <b>malaise, anorexia, anemia, dehydration, fever, weight loss</b>. Extraintestinal: <b>migratory polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, erythema nodosum</b>. <b>Older adults</b> are at high risk of <b>dehydration and hypokalemia</b> from diarrhea.</div>\n<div><b>Causes and risk</b> — cause unknown: <b>genetic</b> (runs in families and twins), <b>immunologic</b> (autoimmune; <b>IgG epithelial antibodies</b> in some patients) and <b>lifestyle</b>. <b>Proinflammatory cytokines</b> (<b>interleukins, TNF-alpha</b>) are cytotoxic to the colon lining, and long disease brings cell changes that <b>raise colon cancer risk</b>. <b>Smoking</b> and a diet <b>high in animal fat and sugar, low in fruits and vegetables</b> raise risk. About <b>3 million</b> Americans have IBD, about half UC; most diagnosed at <b>20–35</b>. UC and Crohn's occur about equally; IBD is rising in non-White populations.</div>\n<div><b>Severity (Table 49.3)</b> — <b>Mild</b>: <b>&lt; 4 stools/day</b> with or without blood; asymptomatic; labs usually normal. <b>Moderate</b>: <b>&gt; 4/day</b>; minimal symptoms, mild pain, mild intermittent nausea, possible ↑ CRP or ESR. <b>Severe</b>: <b>&gt; 6 bloody stools/day</b>; <b>fever, tachycardia, anemia</b>, pain, ↑ CRP and/or ESR. <b>Fulminant</b>: <b>&gt; 10 bloody stools/day</b>; increasing symptoms; anemia may need <b>transfusion</b>; <b>colonic distention on x-ray</b>. <b>CRP</b> rises in the <b>first 6 hours</b> of inflammation and is more sensitive than ESR.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Complications (Table 49.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>In the bowel</b> — <b>hemorrhage or perforation</b> (lower GI bleeding from erosion of the wall); <b>abscesses</b> in the ulcerated lining; <b>toxic megacolon</b> — <b>massive dilation</b> of the colon and colonic ileus that can lead to <b>gangrene and peritonitis</b>; <b>intestinal malabsorption</b> (anemia and malnutrition — <b>most common in Crohn's</b>); <b>nonmechanical obstruction</b> from toxic megacolon or cancer; <b>fistulas</b> (mainly Crohn's — bowel to bladder → <b>pyuria and fecaluria</b>).</div>\n<div><b>Colorectal cancer</b> — UC for <b>more than 10 years</b> → <b>high colorectal cancer risk</b>; cancer causes about <b>one-third of UC-related deaths</b> → <b>frequent colonoscopies</b> when UC involving the whole colon has lasted over 10 years.</div>\n<div><b>Outside the bowel</b> — <b>arthritis</b>; <b>liver and biliary disease</b> (especially <b>cholelithiasis</b>); <b>oral and skin lesions</b>; eye disorders such as <b>iritis</b> (cause unknown); <b>osteoporosis</b>, especially in Crohn's.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Assessment</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — family history of IBD; past and current therapy; dates and types of surgery; nutrition — <b>intolerance of milk products</b> and fried, spicy or hot foods; bowel pattern (<b>color, number, consistency, character</b>); pain, <b>tenesmus</b>, anorexia, fatigue; links between diarrhea and <b>meals, emotional distress and activity</b>; <b>antibiotics in the past 2–3 months</b> (rule out <b><i>Clostridium difficile</b></i>); travel to or from tropical areas; <b>recent NSAIDs</b> (can <b>trigger a flare</b>); extraintestinal symptoms (arthritis, mouth sores, vision problems, skin disorders).</div>\n<div><b>Physical</b> — vital signs usually <b>normal in mild disease</b>; severe → <b>low-grade fever (99–100°F, 37.2–37.8°C)</b>. Findings are often <b>nonspecific</b> (normal in mild cases); viral and bacterial infections can mimic UC. Note <b>distention along the colon</b>; <b>fever with tachycardia</b> may mean <b>dehydration, peritonitis or bowel perforation</b>; check <b>inflamed joints</b> and <b>mouth lesions</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — worry about <b>stool frequency and blood</b>; unpredictable diarrhea is disruptive and anxiety-producing; fear of <b>fecal incontinence</b> keeps patients home — feeling <b>\"tied to the toilet\"</b>; <b>anxiety and depression</b>; meals may bring pain and more stools; frequent visits and cancer surveillance cause anxiety. Explore: <b>life events and flares</b>, <b>stress factors</b>, <b>family and social support</b>, <b>genetic and cancer worries</b>, and <b>internet access</b> for reliable information and telehealth.</div>\n<div><b>Labs</b> — <b>↓ Hgb and Hct</b> (chronic blood loss → anemia, chronic disease); <b>↑ WBC, C-reactive protein or ESR</b> (inflammation); <b>↓ sodium, potassium and chloride</b> (diarrhea, malabsorption); <b>hypoalbuminemia</b> with extensive disease (protein lost in stool).</div>\n<div><b>Imaging and scopes</b> — <b>MR enterography (MRE)</b> is the <b>main test</b> for chronic IBD — it shows the lumen, wall, mesentery and nearby organs. Teach: <b>fast 4–6 hours</b>; the patient drinks a <b>large amount of contrast</b> (cramps, diarrhea) — <b>void before positioning</b> on the table; lies <b>prone</b> while the first of <b>two subcutaneous glucagon</b> doses slows the bowel. <b>Endoscopy or colonoscopy</b> (the prep is especially uncomfortable in IBD); <b>CT</b> for complications; <b>barium enema with air contrast</b> shows UC vs Crohn's and how deep and widespread the disease is.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. <b>Diarrhea</b> from inflammation of the bowel lining. 2. <b>Acute or persistent pain</b> from inflammation, ulceration and skin irritation. 3. <b>Potential for lower GI bleeding</b> and resulting <b>anemia</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Treating ulcerative colitis — drugs, diet and bleeding watch","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Managing diarrhea</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Goal</b> — the major concern is <b>frequent, bloody diarrhea</b> and <b>fecal incontinence from tenesmus</b>; the expected outcome is <b>less diarrhea, formed stools and control of bowel movements</b>, which lets the lining heal. Measures reduce motility, decrease inflammation and promote healing — nonsurgical and/or surgical.</div>\n<div><b>Diet, smoking and rest</b> — teach a diet <b>low in animal fats and sugars</b> with <b>more fruits and vegetables</b>; patients with <b>food insecurity</b> → <b>food banks</b> and community resources. Ask about <b>tobacco</b> (a risk factor) and refer to <b>smoking cessation</b> — check for grants if it costs money. <b>Physical and emotional rest</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Track it</b> — teach the patient to <b>record stool color, volume, frequency and consistency</b> (paper or app); monitor <b>perianal skin</b> for irritation and ulceration; <b>stool cultures</b> if diarrhea continues. <b>Weight once or twice a week</b> at home; in the hospital, <b>daily weight before breakfast on the same scale</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Drug therapy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>5-aminosalicylates (5-ASA)</b> — commonly used for <b>mild to moderate</b> UC and to <b>maintain remission</b>; thought to be anti-inflammatory by <b>inhibiting prostaglandins</b> in the intestinal lining; usually effective in <b>2–4 weeks</b>. Drug classes for UC: <b>5-ASAs, glucocorticoids, immunosuppressants, immunomodulators, antibiotics</b> — teach side effects and when to call.</div>\n<div><b>Sulfasalazine</b> — a 5-ASA that <b>intestinal bacteria</b> split into <b>5-ASA</b> (the benefit) and <b>sulfapyridine</b> (the unwanted effects). Give a <b>folic acid supplement</b> — it decreases folate absorption. <b>Drug Alert</b>: report <b>nausea, vomiting, anorexia, rash and headache</b>; high doses → <b>hemolytic anemia, hepatitis, male infertility, agranulocytosis</b>. It is related to <b>sulfonamide antibiotics</b> → <b>check for sulfa allergy first</b>; a <b>thiazide diuretic</b> may be a contraindication.</div>\n<div><b>Mesalamine</b> — <b>better tolerated</b> than sulfasalazine — <b>no sulfapyridine</b>. <b>Delayed-release</b> (terminal ileum and colon) or <b>extended-release</b> (whole colon and rectum); also as an <b>enema or suppository</b> with <b>minimal systemic absorption</b> and fewer side effects.</div>\n<div><b>Glucocorticoids</b> — <b>prednisone</b> during <b>exacerbations</b> (dose increased in flares), then <b>tapered</b> after improvement because long-term use causes <b>hyperglycemia, osteoporosis, peptic ulcer disease, infection and adrenal insufficiency</b>. Rectal inflammation → steroid <b>retention enemas or suppositories</b>; <b>budesonide</b> acts mostly in the bowel → <b>fewer systemic effects</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Immunomodulators and biologics</b> — immunomodulators alone often don't work in UC but <b>combined with steroids</b> speed response and <b>lower the steroid dose</b>. <b>Biological response modifiers</b>: <b>infliximab</b> — an <b>IgG monoclonal antibody that reduces TNF activity</b> — for <b>refractory</b> disease, <b>toxic megacolon</b> or extraintestinal disease (not first line); <b>adalimumab</b> for refractory cases; both used more in Crohn's. They <b>suppress immunity</b> → teach to <b>report any beginning infection, even a cold</b>, and <b>avoid large crowds and sick people</b>. <b>Vedolizumab</b> is a gut-specific <b>leukocyte traffic inhibitor</b> that keeps white cells from migrating into inflamed bowel.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Sulfasalazine is really <b>two molecules joined together</b>. Gut bacteria cut the bond in the colon: <b>5-ASA</b> stays there to calm the lining, while <b>sulfapyridine</b> is absorbed and causes most side effects — including the <b>sulfa allergy</b> risk. Mesalamine is the 5-ASA half alone, so it is better tolerated. The same \"where does it act?\" logic explains why rectal enemas, suppositories and budesonide cause fewer whole-body effects.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nutrition, rest and comfort</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Bowel rest</b> — hospitalized patients with severe symptoms are kept <b>NPO</b> for bowel rest; <b>TPN</b> for the severely ill and malnourished during flares; less severe → <b>elemental formulas</b>, absorbed in the small bowel with less stimulation.</div>\n<div><b>Food triggers</b> — diet is <b>not a major cause</b> of inflammation, but <b>caffeine and alcohol</b> may increase diarrhea and cramping; <b>raw vegetables and high-fiber foods</b> can cause symptoms; <b>lactose</b> may be poorly tolerated → reduce or eliminate. Common GI stimulants: <b>carbonated drinks, pepper, nuts and corn, dried fruits, smoking</b>. Tolerances differ for each patient.</div>\n<div><b>Rest and complementary therapy</b> — activity is restricted during flares — <b>rest reduces intestinal activity</b>, comforts and promotes healing; keep a <b>bedpan, commode or bathroom</b> close for urgency. Complementary options (need more study): <b>flaxseed, selenium, vitamin C</b>, biofeedback, hypnosis, yoga, acupuncture, ayurveda.</div>\n<div><b>Pain</b> — goal <b>2–3/10 or less</b>; multimodal drug and non-drug measures; surgery may relieve pain. <b>Increasing pain may mean peritonitis</b>. <b>Antidiarrheals</b> reduce discomfort but must be used <b>with caution and short term</b> — they can cause <b>toxic megacolon</b>. Perineal skin: <b>medicated wipes</b>; skin-care systems with a cleanser, healing cream and <b>petroleum-type barrier</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Lower GI bleeding (Critical Rescue)</b> — monitor stools for blood — <b>bright red (frank) or black and tarry (melena)</b>; monitor <b>Hgb, Hct, electrolytes and VS</b>; slow bleeding → <b>anemia</b>; watch for fever, tachycardia, volume depletion; <b>mental status changes</b> may be the <b>first sign</b> of dehydration or anemia in older adults. If bleeding begins, <b>notify the Rapid Response Team or provider immediately</b>; blood products for severe anemia — insert <b>two large-bore IV catheters</b>. A <b>GI bleeding scan</b> can localize bleeding <b>&gt; 0.5 mL/min</b> but takes hours and isn't for ICU patients.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Surgery for ulcerative colitis — the J-pouch and ileostomy","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">When and which surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>When</b> — when drugs fail, or for complications: <b>toxic megacolon, hemorrhage, bowel perforation, dysplasia on biopsy, colon cancer</b>. UC surgery is <b>more common and more successful</b> than surgery for Crohn's. <b>African Americans</b> have worse outcomes (higher <b>sepsis and death</b>) — linked to <b>delayed diagnosis, no insurance and poor access to an IBD specialist</b>.</div>\n<div><b>ERAS (Box 49.3)</b> — <b>Enhanced Recovery After Surgery</b> lowers sepsis and death with <b>nutrition, preventing paralytic ileus, nonopioid analgesia and goal-directed fluids</b>. <b>Before</b>: patient education, <b>frailty survey</b>, <b>reduced fasting time</b>, <b>carbohydrate loading</b>, glucose control. <b>During</b>: <b>minimally invasive surgery</b>, multimodal anesthesia, fluid administration. <b>After</b>: <b>early ambulation</b>, minimal IV, aggressive nausea and vomiting control, nutrition consult for <b>early feeding</b>, <b>multimodal pain management</b>, glucose control, <b>early removal of the urinary catheter</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Before surgery</b> — if an ileostomy is planned, give an <b>in-depth explanation</b> to the patient and family; the surgeon consults a <b>certified wound, ostomy and continence nurse (CWOCN)</b> on the <b>best stoma location</b>; a visit from an <b>ostomate</b> (a person with an ostomy) may help. An <b>ileostomy</b> brings a loop of <b>ileum</b> through the abdominal wall (stoma) to drain stool into a pouch with a <b>skin barrier (wafer)</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Approaches</b> — <b>minimally invasive</b> (laparoscopic, hand-assisted, robotic; <b>NOTES</b> through the anus or vagina) is common — several small incisions, often longer to perform; usually <b>no ICU stay</b>. <b>Obesity, previous surgery or dense adhesions</b> may rule it out → <b>open</b> surgery with general anesthesia and a short <b>ICU</b> stay.</div>\n<div><b>RPC-IPAA — the gold standard</b> — <b>restorative proctocolectomy with ileo pouch–anal anastomosis</b>, usually in <b>two stages</b> (Fig. 49.2). <b>Stage 1</b>: the <b>colon and most of the rectum</b> are removed; the <b>anus and anal sphincter stay</b>; an internal <b>pouch (J-, S- or pelvic pouch)</b> is made from the <b>last 18 inches</b> of small intestine and joined to the anus; a <b>temporary loop ileostomy</b> lets everything heal and the pouch grow. <b>Stage 2</b>, often <b>1–2 months</b> later: the ileostomy is <b>closed</b>. Continence is usually <b>excellent</b>, though some leak stool <b>during sleep</b> (antidiarrheals help); expect <b>frequent stools and urgency</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Total proctocolectomy with permanent ileostomy</b> — for patients who <b>can't have or don't want</b> the pouch: the <b>colon, rectum and anus are removed</b> and the anus is closed; the end of the ileum is brought out as a <b>permanent stoma</b> (Fig. 49.3).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The new ileostomy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Stoma check (Critical Rescue)</b> — usually in the <b>right lower quadrant below the belt line</b>; it should <b>not prolapse or retract</b>. Assess it <b>frequently</b>: <b>pinkish to cherry red</b> = good blood supply. <b>Pale, ash gray, bluish or dark → report to the surgeon immediately</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Output</b> — it starts draining within <b>24 hours</b> at <b>more than 1 L/day</b> — a <b>loose, dark green liquid</b> that may contain some blood → <b>add 500 mL or more of fluid a day</b> to prevent dehydration; after about <b>a week</b> output slows and thickens (antidiarrheals may be needed). With <b>ileostomy adaptation</b> the small bowel absorbs more <b>sodium and water</b> → stool becomes <b>pastelike, yellow-green or yellow-brown</b> with little or a sweet odor. A <b>foul odor</b> may mean <b>blockage or infection</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Skin is the priority</b> — the ileostomy drains <b>often</b>, so a pouch must <b>always</b> be worn. Small-intestine stool is full of <b>enzymes and bile salts</b> that <b>quickly excoriate skin</b>; a pouch with a <b>gelatin or pectin skin barrier</b> protects most patients.</div>\n<div><b>After surgery</b> — with ERAS and MIS most patients have <b>no NG tube</b>; after <b>open</b> surgery the patient is NPO with an <b>NG tube for 1–2 days</b>, then fluids and food are added slowly. Work with the <b>CWOCN</b> on teaching. Hospital stay <b>1–4 days</b>; MIS brings <b>less pain, fewer complications and faster return of bowel function</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After the J-pouch</b> — the pouch can become inflamed (<b>pouchitis</b>) → <b>metronidazole for 7–10 days</b>. After stage 2, <b>burning</b> with bowel movements is common because the ileum absorbs <b>gastric acid</b> poorly. Omit gas- and odor-causing foods (<b>cabbage, asparagus, brussels sprouts, beans</b>) and poorly digested ones (<b>nuts, corn</b>). Body image may change, though many feel better than before surgery.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>An ileostomy skips the colon — the organ that pulls <b>water and salt</b> back out of stool. That one fact explains the teaching: output is <b>liquid and high at first</b> (add fluid), the patient needs <b>extra salt and water</b>, especially with sweating or vomiting, the stool still carries <b>digestive enzymes and bile salts</b> that eat skin (barrier always), and a <b>laxative is never needed</b> — loose stool is normal, while <b>no output</b> may mean a food blockage.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Living with an ileostomy (Box 49.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Skin and pouch care</b> — <b>skin barrier</b> against output; <b>skin sealants and ostomy creams</b>, and a filler for uneven skin; watch the skin for <b>irritation or redness</b>. <b>Empty the pouch when one-third to one-half full</b>. <b>Change it during inactive times</b> — <b>before meals, before bed, on waking, or 2–4 hours after eating</b>. <b>Change the whole system every 3–7 days</b>. Most want a barrier that lasts <b>3–7 days</b>; <b>extended-wear</b> barriers suit high output; weigh pouch size (large = heavy when full) and <b>cost and insurance</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Food</b> — no special diet, but <b>chew food thoroughly</b> and be careful with <b>high-fiber, high-cellulose foods</b> that can cause diarrhea, constipation or <b>blockage</b>: <b>coconut, popcorn, tough-fiber meats, rice, cabbage</b>, and vegetables with skins (<b>tomatoes, corn, peas</b>); also avoid gas-forming foods (<b>nuts, raw cabbage, corn, celery, popcorn</b>). <b>Vitamin B12</b> may be needed if the <b>terminal ileum</b> is removed. Include <b>adequate salt and water</b>; be careful with <b>heavy sweating, strenuous activity, high heat, vomiting or diarrhea</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — <b>avoid enteric-coated and capsule</b> medicines; tell every prescriber and pharmacist about the ostomy. <b>No laxatives or enemas</b> — <b>loose stools are normal</b>; call the provider if <b>no stool has passed in 6–12 hours</b>.</div>\n<div><b>If it stops draining</b> — report any <b>drastic increase or decrease</b> in drainage. With <b>stomal swelling, abdominal cramping or distention</b>, or if <b>output stops</b>: <b>remove the pouch and faceplate</b>, <b>lie in a knee-chest position</b>, <b>massage the abdomen</b>, apply <b>moist towels</b> to the abdomen, <b>drink hot tea</b>. If none of these works or <b>pain is severe</b>, <b>call the provider right away</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Living with it</b> — concerns about <b>sexual and social</b> adjustment — help the patient discuss them with a partner (a <b>change in position</b> during intercourse may ease worry); body image and self-esteem; <b>clinical depression is common</b> in UC → <b>mental health referral</b>. Support: <b>United Ostomy Associations of America (UOAA)</b>, Ostomy Canada Society, <b>Crohn's and Colitis Foundation</b>, IBD support groups, ostomy clinics and ostomate visits after discharge.</div>\n<div><b>Teaching about UC</b> — teach the nature of UC — <b>flares and remissions</b> — and that relapses can be prevented with proper care. Patients on <b>immunosuppressants (steroids, biologics)</b> must <b>report signs of infection</b> and <b>avoid crowds and anyone with an infection</b>. Report <b>fever &gt; 101°F (38.3°C), tachycardia, palpitations, more diarrhea, severe abdominal pain, or nausea and vomiting</b>. Home care: respiratory, incision and ostomy care and pain management continue; a <b>home care nurse</b> helps fit ostomy care into daily life.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — the patient has <b>no or fewer episodes of diarrhea</b>, <b>verbalizes less pain</b>, has <b>no lower GI bleeding</b>, and <b>self-manages</b> the ileostomy or ileoanal pouch.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"49-crohn-s-disease","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Crohn's disease","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What makes it different</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Where</b> — chronic inflammation of the <b>small intestine (most often), the colon or both</b>; it can affect the GI tract <b>from mouth to anus</b> but most often the <b>terminal ileum</b>. Slowly progressive and unpredictable, with diseased areas separated by normal bowel (<b>skip lesions</b>). Like UC it <b>remits and flares</b>, but <b>severe complications</b> and <b>repeated hospital stays</b> are more likely. Non-White patients tend to have <b>more extensive</b> inflammation.</div>\n<div><b>What it does</b> — inflammation <b>thickens the bowel wall</b>; <b>strictures and deep ulcers (cobblestone appearance)</b> form → risk of <b>fistulas</b> (abnormal tracts between organs). Result: <b>severe diarrhea and malabsorption</b> of vital nutrients; <b>anemia</b> (iron deficiency or malabsorption) is common. <b>Severe malabsorption</b> is more common than in UC → patients can become <b>very malnourished and debilitated</b>. Hemorrhage is more typical of UC but can occur. <b>Cancer</b> of the small bowel or colon is <b>rare</b>, after <b>15–20 years</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fistulas and obstruction</b> — <b>fistulas are common in Crohn's but rare in UC</b>: between bowel segments, to the <b>skin (cutaneous)</b>, as <b>perirectal abscesses</b>, or into the <b>bladder or vagina</b> (Fig. 49.4). Obstruction comes first from <b>inflammation and edema</b>, later from <b>fibrosis and scar tissue</b> that narrow the bowel. <b>Most patients need surgery at some point</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Who and why</b> — almost <b>a million</b> Americans; usually diagnosed at <b>15–35</b>. Causes: <b>genetic, immune and lifestyle</b> factors. About <b>20%</b> have a <b>family history</b>; a <b>NOD2 gene</b> mutation affects monocytes that normally recognize and destroy bacteria; some <b>Asian Americans</b> carry a <b>TNFSF15</b> variant linked to more fistulas and strictures. <b>TNF-alpha and interleukins</b> drive inflammation (many drugs block them). Risks: <b>smoking</b>, <b>urban living</b> (pollution), <b>Ashkenazi Jewish</b> background (most common; rising in non-White groups), diet <b>high in animal fat and sugar</b>. Bacterial infection can trigger flares.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History and abdomen</b> — ask about <b>unintentional weight loss</b>, stool <b>frequency and consistency</b>, <b>blood</b>, <b>fever</b> and <b>abdominal pain</b>; assess nutrition and hydration. Inspect for <b>distention, masses or visible peristalsis</b>, and the perianal area for <b>ulcerations, fissures (cracks) or fistulas</b>. Bowel sounds may be <b>decreased or absent</b> with severe inflammation or obstruction, or <b>high-pitched and rushing</b> over narrowed loops; guarding, masses, rigidity or tenderness on palpation.</div>\n<div><b>Symptoms</b> — most have <b>diarrhea, abdominal pain and low-grade fever</b> (fever is common with <b>fistulas, abscesses</b> and severe inflammation). Ileum only → <b>5–6 soft, loose stools a day</b>; <b>steatorrhea</b> (fatty stools) is common; stools may contain bright red blood. Pain is usually <b>constant, in the RLQ</b>, sometimes <b>periumbilical before and after BMs</b>; both lower quadrants if the lower colon is involved. <b>Fatigue</b> is very common — linked to <b>disease activity and anxiety</b> → teach <b>energy conservation</b>. <b>Weight loss</b> from catabolism, anorexia, malabsorption or self-restricted eating.</div>\n<div><b>Watch for (Action Alert)</b> — be especially alert for <b>peritonitis</b>, <b>small bowel obstruction</b>, and <b>nutritional and fluid imbalances</b> — early detection of a change reduces these life-threatening complications.</div>\n<div><b>Labs and imaging</b> — <b>anemia</b> (slow bleeding, poor nutrition); <b>low folic acid and vitamin B12</b> (malabsorption); <b>↓ albumin</b> (amino acid malabsorption, protein-losing enteropathy); <b>↑ CRP and ESR</b>; <b>WBCs in the urine (pyuria)</b> from ureteral obstruction or an <b>enterovesical (bowel-to-bladder) fistula</b>; with severe diarrhea or a fistula, losses of <b>potassium and magnesium</b>. <b>X-rays</b> show narrowing, ulcers, strictures and fistulas; <b>MRE</b> shows bowel activity.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — needs a complete psychosocial assessment — the chronic course and complications affect patients and families; assess <b>coping and support systems</b>; <b>clinical depression and severe anxiety</b> are common.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Drugs</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Mild to moderate</b> — <b>5-ASA</b> drugs may help (research results are mixed for Crohn's). Most patients have <b>moderate to severe</b> disease and need stronger drugs: <b>azathioprine</b> and <b>6-mercaptopurine</b> suppress the immune system and can lead to <b>serious infections</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Biologics</b> — when other drugs fail: <b>infliximab, adalimumab and certolizumab pegol</b> <b>inhibit TNF-alpha</b> to calm inflammation, and are <b>not given</b> with a history of <b>cancer, heart disease or multiple sclerosis</b>. <b>Natalizumab</b> works differently: it <b>keeps white cells from leaving the blood for the tissues</b> (below). <b>Exam note:</b> the textbook lists natalizumab <b>in the same group</b> as these TNF-alpha inhibitors and gives the whole group the same caution (<b>not with cancer, heart disease or MS</b>) — so a question that follows the book may group them. Hispanic, African American and Asian patients are <b>less likely to receive biologics</b> (a health-equity gap).</div>\n<div><b>Biologic teaching (Drug Alert)</b> — <b>infliximab</b> is given in a <b>health care setting</b> (parenteral). Teach <b>self-injection</b> for subcutaneous forms and to report <b>injection-site redness or swelling</b>; <b>headache, abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting</b> are common side effects. <b>Avoid crowds and people with infection</b>, and <b>report any infection — even a cold or sore throat — immediately</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Natalizumab and PML</b> — given <b>IV every 4 weeks</b> under supervision for moderate–severe Crohn's when other drugs fail. It can cause <b>progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML)</b> — a <b>deadly brain infection</b>. Make sure the patient is <b>free of all infections</b> before each dose and teach them to <b>report cognitive, motor or sensory changes immediately</b>. <b>Vedolizumab</b> (IV at weeks <b>0, 2 and 6</b>, then <b>every 8 weeks</b>) has not raised PML risk, but the FDA urges PML teaching.</div>\n<div><b>Steroids and antibiotics</b> — <b>glucocorticoids</b> work, but they <b>mask infection</b> — and <b>sepsis</b> can come from abscesses or fistulas → use <b>short term with caution</b> and watch for infection; <b>don't stop them abruptly</b> (<b>adrenal insufficiency</b>). <b>Ciprofloxacin and metronidazole</b> help with <b>fistulas, anorectal abscesses</b> and infection.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>UC and Crohn's look alike at the bedside, but <b>depth</b> separates them. UC stays in the <b>lining</b> — so it <b>bleeds</b>, and it lies in one continuous stretch that surgery can remove completely. Crohn's goes <b>through every layer</b> in patches — so it <b>scars (strictures)</b>, <b>tunnels into neighbors (fistulas)</b>, and is too widespread for surgery to cure. Location matters too: Crohn's favors the <b>terminal ileum</b>, which absorbs <b>vitamin B12</b> — hence the low B12 and the monthly injections.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nutrition, fistulas and surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Nutrition</b> — long-standing deficits impair <b>fistula and wound healing</b>, waste <b>lean muscle</b>, weaken <b>immunity</b>, and raise illness and death. Severe flares → hospital for <b>bowel rest and TPN</b>; supplements such as <b>Ensure or Sustacal</b> add calories; <b>avoid GI stimulants</b> such as <b>caffeine and alcohol</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fistula types</b> — common in flares: <b>enterovesical</b> (bowel–bladder), <b>enteroenteric</b> (bowel–bowel), <b>enterocutaneous</b> (bowel–skin), <b>enterovaginal</b> (bowel–vagina). Multiple fistulas bring <b>systemic infection, skin problems</b> (abscesses, fissures) and <b>malnutrition</b>. An <b>abscess</b> needs <b>incision and drainage (I&amp;D)</b>; a fistula needs nutrition and electrolyte therapy, skin care and infection prevention.</div>\n<div><b>Fistula priorities (Action Alerts)</b> — <b>nutrition and fluid and electrolyte balance</b> come first: GI secretions are <b>high in volume and rich in electrolytes and enzymes</b> → risk of <b>malnutrition, dehydration and hypokalemia</b>. Watch <b>urine output and daily weight</b> — a drop in either suggests <b>dehydration</b> → give fluids promptly. The patient needs <b>at least 3000 calories a day</b>; <b>TEN or TPN</b> if oral intake falls short; with the dietitian: <b>high-calorie, high-protein, high-vitamin, low-fiber</b> meals, supplements, <b>calorie counts</b>. Then <b>protect the skin</b>: drainage must <b>not touch the skin</b> — intestinal enzymes are <b>caustic</b>; clean promptly to prevent breakdown or fungal infection.</div>\n<div><b>Fistula skin and drainage</b> — consult the <b>CWOCN</b>. Drainage <b>&lt; 100 mL in 24 hours</b> → <b>skin barrier or dressing</b>; heavier drainage → a <b>pouch</b> (protects skin and <b>measures output</b>); the wafer is cut <b>1/8 inch</b> around the fistula (Fig. 49.5). <b>Antifungal powder</b> helps prevent or treat <b><i>Candida</b></i>. If pouching isn't possible: <b>low continuous wall suction</b> with the catheter <b>in the wound bed, not the fistula tract</b> (<b>not for long-term</b> use), or <b>negative-pressure wound therapy</b> (wound VAC), which helps the wound heal by <b>secondary intention</b> — not for patients at <b>risk of bleeding</b> or just to contain drainage. Watch for <b>abscess and sepsis</b>: fever, abdominal pain, <b>mental status change</b>, <b>rising WBC</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — for failed medical therapy or complications — <b>perforation, massive hemorrhage, obstruction or strictures, abscesses, cancer</b>; a fistula may need <b>resection</b> of the diseased area. Crohn's surgery is <b>less successful than UC surgery</b> (the disease is so extensive). <b>Small bowel or ileocecal resection</b> can be <b>laparoscopic</b> (less pain, faster recovery) or open; <b>stricturoplasty</b> widens a stricture. Care is like any abdominal surgery.</div>\n<div><b>Going home</b> — like UC, but <b>more hospitalizations and readmissions</b> (<b>1 in 4</b> IBD patients readmitted within <b>90 days</b>; earlier GI follow-up appointments may help). Arrange <b>bathroom access and privacy</b> for fistula care. Teach the disease course, complications and drugs; <b>vitamin supplements, including monthly B12 injections</b> (the ileum can't absorb them); with the dietitian, a <b>low-residue, high-calorie diet</b> avoiding <b>milk, gluten (wheat) and caffeine</b>. <b>Rest periods</b> in flares; <b>stress management</b>, counseling, activity; <b>frequent colorectal cancer screening</b>. Support <b>complementary therapies</b> the patient finds helpful (e.g., <b>ginger</b>, acupuncture, hypnotherapy, ayurveda), though evidence is lacking. Ideally the patient is <b>independent in fistula care</b> before discharge (or a caregiver is taught); supplies via the case manager; <b>UOAA</b> support.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"49-diverticular-disease","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Diverticular disease","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What happens</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Terms</b> — <b>diverticula</b> are small pouches of <b>mucosa pushed out through the muscular wall</b> of the gut — most often the <b>colon</b>, usually the <b>sigmoid</b> (Fig. 49.6). <b>Diverticulosis</b> is the presence of many diverticula; <b>diverticulitis</b> is their <b>inflammation or infection</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Why they form</b> — the colon muscle <b>hypertrophies, thickens and becomes rigid</b>; <b>high pressure inside the lumen</b> pushes the mucosa and submucosa out through <b>weak points</b> — often where <b>blood vessels interrupt the muscle</b>. Weakness comes with <b>aging</b> or a <b>low-fiber diet</b> (less bulky stool, constipation).</div>\n<div><b>Why they get infected</b> — pouches without inflammation cause <b>few problems</b>. If <b>undigested food or bacteria are trapped</b>, the <b>blood supply falls</b>, bacteria invade → <b>diverticulitis</b> → <b>perforation</b> with a <b>local abscess</b> → <b>peritonitis</b>; <b>lower GI bleeding</b> can also occur.</div>\n<div><b>Who</b> — millions, often <b>without symptoms or awareness</b>; found in <b>two-thirds of adults over 80</b>; <b>more men</b> than women.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Diverticulosis</b> — usually <b>no signs or symptoms</b>; often found on <b>routine colonoscopy</b>. Ask about <b>intermittent left lower quadrant pain</b> and <b>constipation</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diverticulitis</b> — abdominal pain, most often in the <b>LLQ</b> — intermittent at first, then <b>steady</b> (sometimes above the pubic bone or on one side; <b>generalized if peritonitis develops</b>); <b>nausea and vomiting</b>; <b>fever</b> from low-grade to <b>101°F (38.3°C)</b>, chills, <b>tachycardia</b>; constipation from inflammation; rectal bleeding. Distention, tenderness, local muscle spasm, <b>guarding and rebound</b> with peritoneal irritation. <b>Generalized peritonitis</b> → profound guarding, widespread rebound, and <b>sepsis, hypotension or hypovolemic shock</b>. A <b>tender mass</b> may be felt on rectal exam; <b>orthostatic BP</b> changes; massive bleeding → <b>shock</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Tests</b> — diverticulosis: <b>no labs</b> needed. Diverticulitis: <b>↑ WBC</b>; <b>↓ Hgb and Hct</b> with chronic or severe bleeding; stool <b>occult blood</b> sometimes positive. <b>Abdominal x-ray</b> for <b>free air and fluid (perforation)</b>; <b>CT</b> for an <b>abscess or bowel thickening</b>; <b>ultrasound</b>. <b>Colonoscopy 4–8 weeks after</b> the acute phase to rule out a <b>tumor</b>, especially with rectal bleeding.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Treatment</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Who is admitted</b> — mild → <b>ambulatory care</b> (watch for prolonged fever, more pain, blood in the stool). Admit for moderate to severe disease, especially <b>older</b> patients or <b>complications</b>: <b>temperature &gt; 101°F (38.3°C)</b>, <b>persistent severe pain for more than 3 days</b>, and/or <b>lower GI bleeding</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — <b>broad-spectrum antibiotics</b> — <b>metronidazole with TMP/SMZ or ciprofloxacin</b>; a <b>mild analgesic</b> for pain; severe → <b>IV fluids</b> for dehydration and <b>IV drugs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Rest the bowel</b> — <b>rest</b> in the acute phase; <b>no lifting, straining, coughing or bending</b> — raised intra-abdominal pressure can <b>perforate</b> the diverticulum. <b>Low fiber or clear liquids</b> by symptoms; severe → <b>NPO</b>, an <b>NG tube</b> for severe nausea, vomiting or distention, and IV fluids. Intake rises slowly with the dietitian, and <b>fiber is reintroduced gradually</b> once inflammation resolves and bowel function is normal. <b>No laxatives or enemas</b> — they increase motility.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — watch for drug side effects — <b>confusion and orthostatic hypotension</b>; they may <b>not have typical pain or fever</b>, so a <b>sudden change in mental status</b> may be the sign of active disease; <b>frequent abdominal assessments</b> (distention, tenderness); check stools for <b>occult or frank blood</b>; <b>low-fiber diet while active, high-fiber after</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — rupture with <b>peritonitis</b>, <b>pelvic abscess</b>, <b>bowel obstruction</b>, fistula, persistent fever or pain, or <b>uncontrolled bleeding</b>. <b>Emergency surgery</b> for peritonitis, obstruction or pelvic abscess. The most common procedure is <b>colon resection with or without a colostomy</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Fiber flips between \"avoid\" and \"add\" because the problem flips. In <b>diverticulosis</b> the danger is <b>pressure</b>: small, hard, low-fiber stools make the colon squeeze harder, and that pressure pushes out new pouches — bulky high-fiber stool keeps it low. In <b>diverticulitis</b> a pouch is already <b>inflamed and may perforate</b>, so anything that works the bowel harder (fiber, laxatives, enemas, straining) loads a weak, infected wall. <b>Rest the bowel first; prevent with fiber later.</b></div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Prevention and teaching</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Fiber</b> — <b>avoid all fiber while symptoms are present</b> (it irritates), then add it gradually up to a <b>high-fiber diet</b>: <b>25–35 g a day</b>, rich in <b>cellulose and hemicellulose</b> (<b>wheat bran, whole-grain breads and cereals</b>) plus fresh fruits and vegetables for bulk. Add it <b>slowly</b> to avoid gas and cramping; a <b>bulk-forming laxative</b> (<b>psyllium</b>) increases stool size and consistency; <b>drink plenty of fluids</b> to prevent bloating.</div>\n<div><b>Foods and habits</b> — <b>avoid alcohol</b> (irritates the bowel). Foods with <b>seeds or indigestible material</b> that may block a pouch — <b>nuts, corn, popcorn, cucumbers, tomatoes, figs</b> — may need to be eliminated. <b>No laxatives other than bulk-forming types, and no enemas</b>. With a proper diet the disorder should not cause problems.</div>\n<div><b>Report</b> — signs of acute diverticulitis: <b>fever, abdominal pain, and bloody, mahogany or tarry stools</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After surgery</b> — written and oral <b>incision care</b> instructions and what to report; <b>colostomy care</b> if one was made; encourage talk about <b>body image and sexual concerns</b>; a <b>home care nurse</b> checks healing and the ostomy; offer an <b>ostomate or ostomy nurse</b> visit and the <b>UOAA</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Celiac disease and parasitic infections","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Celiac disease</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — a <b>multisystem chronic autoimmune</b> bowel disease: <b>chronic inflammation of the small-intestine lining</b> that can cause <b>bowel wall atrophy, malabsorption and diarrhea</b>. Caused by a mix of <b>genetic, immune and environmental</b> factors; risk is higher with other autoimmune diseases such as <b>rheumatoid arthritis and type 1 diabetes</b>. Main complications: <b>cancer</b> (<b>non-Hodgkin lymphoma</b> or GI cancers) and <b>nutrition deficiencies</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Signs (Box 49.5)</b> — remissions and flares, usually tied to <b>how well the diet is followed</b>. <b>Classic</b>: <b>weight loss, anorexia, diarrhea and/or constipation, steatorrhea, abdominal pain and distention, vomiting</b>. Some have <b>no symptoms</b>; others have <b>atypical</b> ones in any body system: <b>osteoporosis</b>, joint pain, <b>lactose intolerance</b>, <b>iron deficiency anemia</b>, depression, migraines, epilepsy, autoimmune disorders, stomatitis, early menopause, protein-calorie malnutrition.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis and treatment</b> — diagnosed by a <b>screening blood test and endoscopy</b>. A <b>gluten-free diet (GFD)</b> is the <b>only treatment</b> for remission — the lining usually <b>heals in about 2 years</b>. <b>Gluten</b> is the main substance in <b>wheat</b> and wheat products (bread, noodles, cookies). Teach patients to check for <b>hidden gluten</b> in <b>foods, food additives, drugs and cosmetics</b>; <b>vitamin and mineral supplements</b> replace what is missed; include a <b>registered dietitian</b> in long-term care.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>\"Gluten-free\" here is a <b>treatment, not a preference</b>. In celiac disease gluten is the <b>trigger</b> for the immune attack on the small-intestine lining. Remove the trigger and the lining heals (about 2 years); keep eating it — even hidden in a pill coating or a lipstick — and the <b>inflammation, malabsorption and cancer risk</b> continue. That is why flares track diet adherence so closely.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Parasitic infections (Table 49.5)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>How they spread</b> — parasites usually enter by the <b>mouth (oral-fecal)</b> — contaminated <b>food or water</b>, <b>oral-anal sex</b>, or contact with an infected person's <b>feces</b>. The main test is <b>stool analysis</b>; the <b>WBC can be very high</b> with dysentery.</div>\n<div><b>Giardiasis (<i>Giardia lamblia</i>)</b> — cysts and trophozoites cause <b>superficial invasion and inflammation of the small-intestine lining</b> → <b>diarrhea, malabsorption, weight loss, nutrient deficiencies</b>. The acute phase is <b>self-limiting</b>; a chronic phase can last <b>years</b>. Treatment: <b>metronidazole</b> (drug of choice) or <b>tinidazole</b>; stools checked <b>2 weeks after</b> treatment.</div>\n<div><b>Amebiasis (<i>Entamoeba histolytica</i>)</b> — most common in <b>crowded areas with poor sanitation</b>; <b>invades and ulcerates the large intestine</b>; it can cause <b>no symptoms</b> at all, or mild to severe ones. Mild to moderate: several <b>strongly foul-smelling stools a day</b>, maybe mucus but <b>no blood</b>, cramps, gas, fatigue, weight loss; remissions and recurrences. <b>Severe amebic dysentery</b>: frequent liquid foul stools with <b>mucus and blood</b>, <b>fever to 104°F (40°C)</b>, <b>tenesmus</b>, abdominal tenderness, vomiting. Colon ulcers can cause pain, bleeding and <b>obstruction</b>; rectal ulcers put <b>blood on formed stool</b>; rarely appendicitis or <b>perforation</b>. <b>Extraintestinal</b>: <b>amebic liver abscess</b> (fever, pain, enlarged liver) — it can <b>rupture</b> and kill.</div>\n<div><b>Treating amebiasis</b> — all types get <b>amebicides</b>: <b>metronidazole followed by a luminal agent such as paromomycin</b>. Severe dysentery → <b>IV fluids</b> and possibly <b>diphenoxylate with atropine</b> to slow the bowel; hospitalize for <b>extraintestinal disease or severe dehydration</b> (especially older adults); <b>asymptomatic, mild or moderate</b> disease is treated with drugs as an <b>outpatient</b>. Cure is judged by <b>at least 3 stools at 2–3-day intervals</b>, starting <b>2–4 weeks after</b> treatment — stress <b>follow-up and finishing all drugs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Cryptosporidium</b> — most common in the <b>immunosuppressed</b>; the source is often <b>contaminated swimming pools</b>; mainly <b>diarrhea</b>; <b>self-limiting</b> with normal immunity. Immunosuppressed patients may get <b>paromomycin</b> (an aminoglycoside antibiotic) — teach that it can cause <b>dizziness</b>.</div>\n<div><b>History</b> — <b>travel</b> to areas where parasites are common (GI symptoms may start <b>1–2 weeks after returning</b>); <b>immigrants</b> may arrive infected; if <b>several people in a group</b> are ill, think of a <b>shared water supply</b> (<b>Giardia or Cryptosporidium</b>); <b>pork</b> → consider <b>trichinosis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Stop the spread (Action Alert)</b> — <b>handwashing is the best way to prevent spread</b> of parasitic infections. The patient is <b>contagious until amebicides kill the parasites</b>. Teach: <b>avoid contact with stool</b>; <b>keep toilets clean</b>; <b>wash hands meticulously with antimicrobial soap after BMs</b>; <b>bathe or shower daily</b>; <b>avoid dog and beaver stool</b>. <b>No sex with rectal contact until treatment is finished</b>; <b>all household and sexual partners need stool exams</b>; test a suspected <b>water supply</b> (wells, unfiltered water) — household infections are often from shared water.</div>\n<div><b>Chagas disease</b> — caused by <b><i>Trypanosoma cruzi</b></i>, spread mostly in poor areas of <b>Latin America</b> by the <b>triatomine (\"kissing\") bug</b>. Acute infection → an asymptomatic period → <b>chronic</b> disease with <b>dysrhythmias or heart failure</b> and <b>dilation of the colon or esophagus</b> (impaired digestion and elimination). About <b>300,000</b> in the US (mostly the south); spread by <b>blood transfusion and organ transplant</b>; a CDC <b>neglected parasitic infection</b> expected to increase.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every organism in this box travels the same road: <b>feces to mouth</b>. That is why the teaching is all about <b>stool</b> (avoid contact, clean toilets, wash after BMs), <b>shared water</b> (test the well) and <b>close contacts</b> (examine the household and partners). Break the road anywhere and the cycle stops — which is why <b>handwashing is the best prevention</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"49-adpie","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Inflammation and infection of the bowel threaten the same things — <b>fluid balance, nutrition and skin</b> — and can turn into <b>peritonitis, bleeding or sepsis</b>. The nurse tracks stools, pain, vital signs and weight, protects skin from digestive enzymes, guards against spreading infection, and teaches patients to live with a chronic disease: drugs, diet, an ostomy and infection precautions.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div><b>order of symptoms</b> (pain before or after vomiting); pain character, location and spread (periumbilical → McBurney; LLQ); fever; <b>travel</b>, restaurants, raw foods, <b>group illness or shared water</b>; <b>antibiotics in the past 2–3 months</b> (<i>C. difficile</i>), <b>NSAIDs</b>; family history of IBD; <b>smoking</b>; diet (fiber, fat, sugar, lactose, gluten); stool <b>number, blood, mucus, fat</b>; weight loss and fatigue; stress and flares; extraintestinal symptoms.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div>VS — <b>fever, tachycardia, hypotension, orthostatic changes, narrowing pulse pressure</b>; the abdomen — <b>rigidity (boardlike)</b>, <b>rebound</b>, guarding, distention, bowel sounds; McBurney point; LLQ tenderness; perianal <b>fissures and fistulas</b>; hydration (turgor, mucous membranes, <b>urine output, daily weight</b>); <b>mental status</b> (older adults); joints, mouth, eyes, skin; <b>stoma color</b> and output; fistula drainage and skin.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Labs and tests</div><b>WBC</b> (~20,000 peritonitis; 10,000–18,000 with shift to the left in appendicitis; &gt; 20,000 = perforation?); <b>blood cultures</b>; <b>CRP and ESR</b>; <b>Hgb/Hct</b>; <b>Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻, Mg²⁺</b>; <b>albumin</b>, folate, <b>B12</b>; BUN, creatinine, ABGs; x-ray <b>free air</b>; ultrasound, CT, <b>MRE</b>, colonoscopy; <b>stool analysis</b> (parasites, <i>C. diff</i>, cultures); celiac <b>blood test + endoscopy</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Psychosocial</div>fear and anxiety with acute illness; with chronic IBD — fear of incontinence (<b>\"tied to the toilet\"</b>), <b>anxiety and depression</b>, body image after an ostomy, sexual concerns, social isolation, coping and support, cancer worries, cost of supplies and food, access to care.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>peritonitis: <b>acute pain</b> and <b>potential fluid volume shift</b>. UC: <b>diarrhea</b>, <b>pain</b>, <b>potential lower GI bleeding and anemia</b>. Crohn's and fistulas: <b>malnutrition, dehydration, hypokalemia, skin breakdown, sepsis</b>. Gastroenteritis: <b>dehydration</b> (older adults) and <b>spread of infection</b>. Appendicitis and diverticulitis: <b>perforation → peritonitis</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>pain <b>2–3 or less on a 0–10 scale</b> (or acceptable); <b>fluid and electrolyte balance</b> (stable weight, adequate urine output); <b>fewer diarrheal episodes and formed stools</b>; <b>no lower GI bleeding</b>; the patient <b>self-manages the ileostomy or ileoanal pouch</b>; fistulas heal with intact skin and adequate nutrition; no spread of infection to others.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Acute abdomen</div>peritonitis: <b>frequent VS for septic shock</b>, <b>broad-spectrum antibiotics</b>, <b>IV fluids</b>, NG decompression, <b>strict asepsis</b> with drains and wounds, compare irrigation in vs out; after surgery <b>hourly LOC, VS, respiratory status and I&amp;O</b>, <b>semi-Fowler</b>. Appendicitis: <b>NPO</b>, IV fluids, semi-Fowler, opioids and antibiotics once confirmed — <b>no laxatives, enemas or heat</b>. Diverticulitis: antibiotics, <b>rest</b>, <b>no lifting, straining or bending</b>, low fiber or clear liquids (NPO if severe), <b>no laxatives or enemas</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Chronic IBD</div><b>stool diary</b> and weights; drugs — <b>5-ASA</b> (sulfasalazine: <b>sulfa allergy, folic acid</b>), <b>steroids</b> (taper; never stop suddenly), <b>biologics</b> (<b>avoid crowds, report infection</b>; <b>natalizumab → PML signs</b>); bowel rest, TPN or elemental formulas; limit caffeine, alcohol and lactose; monitor for <b>bleeding</b> (Rapid Response Team, two large-bore IVs); <b>antidiarrheals short term only</b>; fistula care — <b>≥ 3000 kcal</b>, I&amp;O and daily weights, <b>keep drainage off skin</b>, CWOCN; ileostomy — <b>stoma pink to cherry red</b>, <b>+ 500 mL fluid/day</b>, barrier, <b>no laxatives</b>, knee-chest if it stops.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Infection and celiac</div>gastroenteritis: <b>oral fluids or ORT</b>, IV if severe; <b>no antimotility drugs</b> unless prescribed (no diphenoxylate in older adults); perianal skin care, <b>sitz baths</b>; <b>handwashing ≥ 30 seconds</b>, separate utensils, no food handling. Parasites: <b>metronidazole</b> (± paromomycin), stool rechecks, <b>household and partner testing</b>, hygiene and water testing. Celiac: <b>strict gluten-free diet</b>, hidden gluten, supplements, dietitian.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>pain is controlled as the infection resolves; vital signs, weight, urine output and electrolytes are stable; fever and WBC fall; diarrhea decreases and stools form; <b>no bleeding</b>; the stoma stays pink and the patient can <b>demonstrate pouch care</b> and say when to call; fistula output falls with intact skin and stable weight; the patient follows the diet (high fiber after diverticulitis, gluten-free in celiac), knows the drug precautions and keeps <b>colonoscopy</b> and follow-up appointments.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"53-overview","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">53 · Chapter 53</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Fluid &amp; electrolyte balance · glucose regulation</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Assessment of the endocrine system</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> The endocrine glands are <b>ductless</b>: they release hormones into the blood, and only <b>target tissues</b> with the matching receptor respond. Every endocrine problem is one of three things — <b>too much hormone, too little hormone, or receptors that respond poorly</b>. This chapter is the toolkit for chapters 55 (thyroid and parathyroid) and 56 (diabetes): which gland makes what, how feedback switches it on and off, what you look for on the body, and how the tests are collected so the result means something.</p><div id=\"ch53-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Glands secrete hormones into the blood; a hormone acts only where a receptor fits it (lock and key), and <b>negative feedback</b> turns secretion off once the need is met.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A gland makes too much or too little hormone, or the target tissue stops responding to it (insulin resistance in type 2 diabetes).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Changes in energy, weight, appetite, heat or cold tolerance, thirst and urination, skin pigment, hair, face and eyes, mood, menses and sexual function.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hormone assays in blood, urine or saliva; stimulation and suppression tests; 24-hour urine; glucose and A1C; imaging, thyroid scan, fine needle aspiration.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Replace a missing hormone, block or remove an excess (drugs, surgery, radiation), and teach lifelong self-management.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Fluid and electrolyte crises (sodium, potassium, calcium), glucose crises, dehydration, dysrhythmias.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Pressure on a hyperthyroid gland (<b>thyroid storm</b>); an older adult who stops drinking (low ADH reserve); new confusion with abnormal glucose, sodium or potassium.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Body condition drifts from normal<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>gland secretes its hormone<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>target tissue corrects the change<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>hormone level rises<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>secretion slows or stops (negative feedback)</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Provocative vs suppression test</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Suspect <b>too little</b>: give a stimulator — <b>no rise = hypofunction</b>. Suspect <b>too much</b>: give a suppressor — <b>no fall = hyperfunction</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Single blood level vs 24-hour urine</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Many hormones are released in <b>pulses</b>, so one sample can mislead; a 24-hour urine reflects the whole day (adrenal hormones especially).</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Norepinephrine vs epinephrine</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Adrenal medulla: about 15% norepinephrine, mainly <b>alpha</b> (vasoconstriction); 85% epinephrine, mainly <b>beta</b> (heart rate, contractility, bronchodilation).</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Connections to other chapters</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Ch 55 uses the TRH → TSH → T3/T4 axis and PTH with calcium; ch 56 uses insulin and glucagon; the RAAS (ch 13) explains aldosterone.</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Orthostatic vital signs, weight, intake and output with thirst, blood glucose, sodium, potassium and calcium, heart rate and rhythm.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Never press on or palpate a hyperthyroid gland. Report dysrhythmias, signs of dehydration, and glucose or electrolyte values far outside normal.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>What the glands keep steady</b> — working with every other body system, the endocrine glands regulate <b>temperature, metabolism, growth, nutrition, elimination, fluid and electrolyte balance and reproduction</b>. The control is tight: core temperature stays at or near <b>98.6°F (37°C)</b> whatever the weather, and <b>serum sodium stays between 136 and 145 mEq/L (mmol/L)</b> in a healthy adult with a daily intake anywhere from <b>2 g to 12 g of sodium</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Lock and key</b> — circulating hormones reach every tissue, but act only on <b>target tissues</b> whose receptors fit them. Each receptor type is specific for <b>one hormone</b>, like a lock that only one key opens. Once unlocked, the target cell changes its activity.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Simple negative feedback</b> — blood glucose rises above normal → the pancreas secretes <b>insulin</b> → cells take up glucose → glucose falls → insulin secretion slows. The hormone's action is the <b>opposite</b> of the change that started it — that is what \"negative\" means.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Complex negative feedback (an axis)</b> — low cortisol → the hypothalamus releases <b>CRH</b> → the anterior pituitary releases <b>ACTH</b> → the adrenal cortex releases <b>cortisol</b> → rising cortisol shuts off CRH and ACTH. The thyroid runs the same way: <b>TRH → TSH → T3 and T4</b>, and high thyroid hormone switches TRH and TSH off.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Hypothalamus and pituitary</b> — the hypothalamus makes releasing and inhibiting hormones and sends them straight to the anterior pituitary through a private <b>portal circulation</b>, so very little reaches the rest of the body. The posterior pituitary only <b>stores</b> what the hypothalamus makes — <b>ADH (vasopressin)</b> and <b>oxytocin</b> — and releases them when needed.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Three ways it goes wrong</b> — an <b>excess</b> of a hormone, a <b>deficiency</b> of a hormone, or <b>poor hormone-receptor interaction</b> that makes the target tissue less responsive. Each normal hormone range is well defined, so a value outside it can affect many body systems at once.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Because every hormone sits in a feedback loop, a single level means little on its own: it has to be read against what drives it. A \"normal\" cortisol can be abnormal at midnight, and some hormones have such wide normal ranges that only a <b>stimulation or suppression test</b> shows whether the gland responds. That is why so much of this chapter is about <b>timing and collection</b>, not just which test is ordered.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"The adrenal glands — aldosterone, cortisol, catecholamines","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Adrenal cortex (about 90% of the gland)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Aldosterone — the sodium and potassium hormone</b> — a <b>mineralocorticoid</b> that keeps extracellular fluid volume and electrolytes in balance: the kidney <b>reabsorbs sodium and water</b> and <b>excretes potassium</b>. Secretion is driven by the <b>renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS)</b>, by <b>serum potassium</b>, and by ACTH.</div>\n<div><b>The RAAS, step by step</b> — a drop in extracellular fluid volume (<b>blood loss, sodium loss, standing up</b>) or <b>hypoxemia</b> makes kidney arteriole cells release <b>renin</b> → renin turns angiotensinogen into <b>angiotensin I</b> → an enzyme converts it to <b>angiotensin II</b>, the active form → angiotensin II stimulates <b>aldosterone</b> → the kidney reabsorbs sodium and water until plasma volume and osmolarity are back to normal.</div>\n<div><b>Potassium switches it on fast</b> — aldosterone is secreted when serum potassium rises above normal by as little as <b>0.1 mEq/L</b>, and it makes the kidney excrete potassium until the level is back to normal.</div>\n<div><b>Cortisol — essential for life</b> — the main <b>glucocorticoid</b>. It shapes the <b>stress response</b>, carbohydrate, protein and fat metabolism, emotional stability, immune function and sodium balance, and it must be present for the <b>catecholamines to work</b> and for normal heart muscle excitability.</div>\n<div><b>What glucocorticoids do (Box 53.1)</b> — prevent <b>hypoglycemia</b> (more liver glucose production — gluconeogenesis — and less glucose use by tissues); keep cardiac muscle responsive; increase <b>lipolysis</b> (glycerol and free fatty acids) and <b>protein breakdown</b>; degrade collagen and connective tissue; release more mature <b>neutrophils</b> from the marrow; <b>antiinflammatory</b> (fewer inflammatory cells reach injuries); maintain behavior and cognition.</div>\n<div><b>Cortisol's rhythm</b> — controlled by <b>ACTH</b> (and CRH above it), which respond to the free cortisol level, the <b>sleep-wake cycle</b> and <b>stress</b>. Cortisol <b>peaks in the morning</b> and is lowest about <b>12 hours later</b>; emotional, chemical or physical stress raises it.</div>\n<div><b>Sex hormones</b> — the cortex makes small amounts of androgens and estrogens — usually minor next to the gonads, but in <b>women the adrenal gland is the main source of androgens</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Aldosterone and cortisol explain most adrenal findings before you ever meet an adrenal disease. <b>Aldosterone = sodium in, water in, potassium out</b>, so too much causes fluid retention and low potassium, and too little causes volume loss and high potassium. <b>Cortisol = sugar up, inflammation down</b>, so excess looks like high glucose, infections and poor healing.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Adrenal medulla — the catecholamines</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>A sympathetic ganglion that secretes</b> — sympathetic stimulation releases <b>epinephrine (about 85%)</b> and <b>norepinephrine (about 15%)</b> into the blood. They are <b>not essential for life</b> (other tissues make them) but drive the <b>fight-or-flight</b> stress response; small amounts are secreted all the time.</div>\n<div><b>Receptors decide the effect (Table 53.2)</b> — adrenergic receptors come in two families, each with subtypes — <b>alpha (alpha1, alpha2)</b> and <b>beta (beta1, beta2, beta3)</b> — and the receptor on the target tissue decides the response. Norepinephrine acts mainly on <b>alpha</b>, epinephrine mainly on <b>beta</b> receptors. Eyes (alpha): pupils dilate. Heart (<b>beta1</b>): faster rate, stronger contraction. Vessels: alpha <b>constricts</b>, <b>beta2 dilates</b>. Bronchioles (<b>beta2</b>): relax and dilate. GI tract: sphincters tighten (alpha), motility falls (beta). Fat (beta): lipolysis. Liver (alpha): <b>gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis</b>. Pancreas: alpha lowers, beta raises insulin and glucagon release. Skin (alpha): sweating.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The receptor table is a map of what stress looks like on the body: fast heart, wide pupils, sweating, a gut that slows down, and <b>blood glucose that rises</b> because the liver releases sugar. It is the same reason a stressed or sick person with diabetes runs high, and why the beta-blocker you meet in chapter 55 calms thyroid-driven tachycardia.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"Pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid and pancreas","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hypothalamus and pituitary (Tables 53.1 and 53.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Hypothalamus</b> — makes <b>CRH, GnRH, GHRH, TRH</b>, somatostatin (growth hormone–inhibiting), prolactin-inhibiting and melanocyte-inhibiting hormones. They either stimulate or inhibit release from the anterior pituitary.</div>\n<div><b>Anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis)</b> — some of its hormones are <b>tropic</b> — their targets are other endocrine glands — while others, such as <b>prolactin</b>, act directly on their final target tissue. <b>ACTH (corticotropin)</b> → adrenal cortex (corticosteroids and adrenocortical growth). <b>TSH (thyrotropin)</b> → thyroid (thyroid hormone). <b>FSH and LH</b> → ovary (estrogen, follicle maturation, ovulation, progesterone) and testis (sperm, testosterone). <b>Growth hormone</b> → bone and soft tissue (growth through lipolysis, protein building and insulin antagonism). <b>Prolactin</b> → breast development and milk production (no known function in men). <b>MSH</b> → pigmentation. Drugs, diet, lifestyle and disease can all change pituitary hormone release.</div>\n<div><b>Posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis)</b> — <b>vasopressin (ADH)</b> → kidney: <b>water reabsorption</b>. <b>Oxytocin</b> → uterus and breast: contractions and milk let-down. Both are made in the hypothalamus and only stored here. The gland sits in the <b>sella turcica</b> of the sphenoid bone.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Notice how many hormones on this list are <b>insulin antagonists</b>: growth hormone, cortisol, epinephrine and thyroid hormone all push glucose up. Insulin is the only hormone that lowers it — which is why so many different illnesses and drugs cause high blood sugar, and almost none cause low blood sugar.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Gonads — ovaries and testes</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What they are and make</b> — the <b>gonads</b> are the reproductive endocrine glands: the <b>ovaries</b> secrete <b>estrogen and progesterone</b>, the <b>testes</b> secrete <b>testosterone</b> (Table 53.1). They make far more estrogen and androgen than the adrenal cortex — except that in women the adrenal gland is the main androgen source.</div>\n<div><b>Switched on at puberty</b> — gonad function stays <b>dormant until puberty</b>. Then the <b>gonadotropins</b> of the anterior pituitary — <b>FSH and LH</b>, released in response to hypothalamic <b>GnRH</b> — make the glands and the <b>external genitalia mature</b>: the testes begin making <b>testosterone</b> and the ovaries <b>estrogen</b>, and these hormones produce the <b>secondary sex characteristics</b>. FSH matures ovarian follicles and drives sperm production; LH triggers ovulation and progesterone secretion and stimulates testosterone secretion (Table 53.3). Full structure and function: chapter 61.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The gonads sit at the end of the same three-step chain as the thyroid and the adrenal cortex: <b>GnRH → FSH and LH → estrogen, progesterone or testosterone</b>. So a sex-hormone problem can start in the gonad itself or higher up, in the pituitary or hypothalamus. And because these hormones build the secondary sex characteristics, their clues show up in the history (menarche, menses, menopause, libido) and on the exam (body and pubic hair, breasts, genital size).</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Thyroid gland</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Where and what</b> — in the anterior neck just <b>below the cricoid cartilage</b>: two lobes joined by an <b>isthmus</b> in front of the trachea. <b>Follicular cells</b> make <b>T4 (thyroxine)</b> and <b>T3 (triiodothyronine)</b>; <b>parafollicular cells</b> make <b>calcitonin</b>.</div>\n<div><b>T3 and T4 set the metabolic rate</b> — both raise metabolism, so every tissue uses more <b>oxygen</b> and makes more <b>heat</b>. Most circulating hormone is bound to plasma proteins; the <b>free</b> hormone enters cells, where <b>T4 is converted to T3, the most active form</b>. Conversion is impaired by <b>stress, starvation, dyes and some drugs</b>; <b>cold</b> increases it.</div>\n<div><b>Control — the thyroid axis</b> — the hypothalamus secretes <b>TRH</b> → the anterior pituitary secretes <b>TSH</b> → the thyroid makes and releases <b>T3 and T4</b>. <b>High</b> thyroid hormone levels inhibit TRH and TSH release; <b>low</b> levels increase it (negative feedback). <b>Cold and stress</b> also make the hypothalamus secrete TRH, which raises TSH.</div>\n<div><b>Making the hormone</b> — needs dietary <b>protein and iodine</b>. Iodide absorbed from the gut is trapped and concentrated by the thyroid, joined to the amino acid <b>tyrosine</b> to form T4 and T3, and stored on <b>thyroglobulin</b> in the follicles until TSH releases it. Inside cells the hormones bind DNA receptors and switch on metabolic genes that set the <b>basal metabolic rate</b>.</div>\n<div><b>What thyroid hormones do (Box 53.2)</b> — regulate the metabolic rate of all cells and <b>body heat</b>; act as <b>insulin antagonists</b>; support growth hormone, skeletal maturation and nervous system development; maintain <b>muscle tone</b>, calcium mobilization and RBC production; maintain <b>cardiac rate, force and output</b>, respiratory rate and oxygen use, and <b>GI secretion</b>; regulate protein, carbohydrate and fat metabolism and stimulate lipid turnover and cholesterol synthesis.</div>\n<div><b>Calcitonin</b> — <b>lowers serum calcium and phosphate</b> by reducing their release from bone — the opposite of PTH. <b>High</b> calcium increases calcitonin secretion; <b>low</b> calcium suppresses it.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Thyroid hormone is the body's <b>thermostat and accelerator</b> at once. Too much speeds every system — heat intolerance, fast heart, diarrhea, weight loss; too little slows every system — cold intolerance, slow heart, constipation, weight gain. Learn the function list in Box 53.2 and you can predict both diseases in chapter 55 without memorizing them separately.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Parathyroid, pancreas and pineal</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Parathyroid hormone (PTH) raises calcium</b> — four small glands on the back of the thyroid. PTH acts on three targets: <b>bone</b>, the main calcium store — releases calcium and phosphorus into the blood (resorption); <b>kidney</b> — reabsorbs calcium (and magnesium), excretes phosphorus, and <b>activates vitamin D</b>; <b>gut</b> — activated vitamin D increases calcium and phosphorus absorption. <b>Low calcium raises PTH; high calcium lowers it.</b></div>\n<div><b>Pancreas — two jobs</b> — <b>exocrine</b>: digestive enzymes through ducts into the duodenum. <b>Endocrine</b>: 1–2 million <b>islets of Langerhans</b> with <b>alpha cells (glucagon)</b>, <b>beta cells (insulin)</b> and <b>delta cells (somatostatin)</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Glucagon, insulin, somatostatin</b> — <b>glucagon raises glucose</b> — released when glucose falls (and amino acids rise) to prevent hypoglycemia. <b>Insulin lowers glucose</b> by moving it into cells and storing carbohydrate, protein and fat; it rises when glucose rises. <b>Somatostatin</b> (pancreas, gut, brain) inhibits glucagon and insulin, and gastrin, secretin and other GI peptides.</div>\n<div><b>Pineal and thymus</b> — the pineal gland, the size of a grain of rice, releases <b>melatonin in the dark</b> to set the circadian rhythm and sleep-wake cycle. The thymus works mainly in childhood and is also part of the lymphatic system.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Calcium is guarded by two opposing hormones: <b>PTH pulls calcium into the blood</b> (from bone, kidney and gut), <b>calcitonin pushes it back into bone</b>. An older woman with repeated fractures is therefore a calcium question first — and when the parathyroid glands are bruised or removed during thyroid surgery (chapter 55), calcium falls within hours.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"Aging and COVID-19 — what changes in the glands","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Age-related endocrine changes</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The general rule</b> — aging usually means <b>less glandular function and less hormone</b>. The notable exception: the <b>prostate enlarges</b> (hyperplasia). Ovarian function falls faster and more noticeably, and abruptly after a <b>hysterectomy</b> (surgical menopause). Chronic illness, diet and activity changes, poor sleep, slower metabolism and drugs make normal and abnormal hard to tell apart, so encourage regular screening.</div>\n<div><b>Less ADH</b> — urine stays <b>dilute</b> and may not concentrate when intake is low → <b>high risk for dehydration</b>. Assess for it often, and teach assistive personnel to <b>offer fluids at least every 2 hours</b> while awake unless restricted.</div>\n<div><b>Less estrogen</b> — <b>bone density falls</b>; skin becomes thinner, drier and easier to injure; perineal and vaginal tissues dry and <b>cystitis risk rises</b>. Teach <b>weight-bearing exercise</b>, adequate protein and <b>2 L of fluid</b> daily (unless contraindicated), <b>protect skin integrity</b>, perineal care at least twice a day, voiding right after intercourse, and vaginal lubricants.</div>\n<div><b>Less glucose tolerance</b> — weight above ideal with <b>elevated fasting or random glucose</b>, <b>slow wound healing</b>, <b>frequent yeast infections</b>, <b>polydipsia and polyuria</b>. Ask about family history of obesity and type 2 diabetes, teach exercise, a healthy weight and the <b>signs of diabetes to report</b>, and suggest diabetes testing for persistent vaginal candidiasis, <b>a wound not healed within 2 weeks</b> (especially leg or foot), persistent hunger and thirst, or a lasting drop in energy.</div>\n<div><b>Slower general metabolism</b> — <b>less cold tolerance</b>, less appetite, lower heart rate and blood pressure. Teach dressing in layers. Look for signs of a real endocrine disorder: lethargy, new constipation, slowed thinking or speech, temperature consistently <b>below 97°F (36°C)</b>, heart rate consistently <b>below 60</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The older adult's normal changes look like mild hypothyroidism and mild diabetes, so the job is to spot the <b>difference from their own baseline</b> and the findings that go further — a temperature that stays below 97°F, a wound that will not heal in two weeks, thirst that will not stop. Low ADH reserve is the one that turns dangerous fastest: an older patient who simply drinks less becomes dehydrated quickly.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Endocrine effects of COVID-19</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Pancreas</b> — <b>new hyperglycemia</b> appeared often in people without diabetes, probably because SARS-CoV-2 can infect and replicate in pancreatic cells. <b>Ketoacidosis</b> was often diagnosed in older patients of non-White ethnic groups with type 2 diabetes. A registry is studying new-onset diabetes after COVID-19.</div>\n<div><b>Thyroid and ovaries</b> — <b>subacute thyroiditis</b> developed during infection in several studies, but thyroid function usually <b>returned to normal within months</b>. About a third of people in one survey reported <b>menstrual changes</b> — irregular, heavy, or postmenopausal bleeding.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"Endocrine assessment — history and physical exam","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">History</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Age and sex</b> — <b>age</b> matters because diabetes, loss of ovarian function and decreased thyroid function are more common later in life; <b>sex assigned at birth</b> matters because some disorders favor one sex — <b>thyroid problems are more common in women</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Past history, menses and menopause</b> — ask about earlier <b>endocrine problems, symptoms and hospitalizations</b>. Ask women the <b>age at menarche</b> and whether <b>menopause</b> has occurred; Box 53.4 adds whether they still have <b>menstrual cycles</b>, the date of the <b>last period</b>, and any change in <b>flow, duration or frequency</b> since symptoms began. Ask everyone about a change in <b>libido</b> and concerns about how symptoms affect <b>sexuality or reproduction</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs and hormone misuse</b> — ask about past and current drugs, <b>prescribed and over-the-counter</b>, because some alter endocrine function — for example <b>cortisone, levothyroxine, oral contraceptives, antihypertensives</b>. Hormones taken when not needed for replacement (<b>androgens, thyroid hormone</b>) can seriously disrupt many glands; teach <b>all</b> patients the danger of misusing hormones or steroids.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — <b>type 2 diabetes</b> is more common in some racial and ethnic minority groups and in people of <b>lower socioeconomic status</b>. Assess every patient, advocate for early intervention, and teach everyone with or at risk for T2DM so disparities shrink.</div>\n<div><b>Nutrition clues</b> — excessive <b>thirst</b> with AVP deficiency or resistance (formerly diabetes insipidus); <b>salt craving</b> with adrenal hypofunction; persistent <b>hunger and thirst</b> with diabetes; <b>rapid weight change without diet change</b> with thyroid or hypothalamic disease. <b>Iodine</b> is needed to make thyroid hormone and the body cannot make it, so it must come from the diet: ask which sources the patient eats and how much (Box 53.3) — <b>dairy</b> (cheese, milk, yogurt), <b>eggs</b>, <b>iodized table salt</b>, <b>saltwater fish</b> (salmon, sardines, tuna), <b>seaweed</b> (kelp, nori), <b>shellfish</b> (scallops, shrimp) and <b>iodine-containing multivitamins</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Family history and current symptoms</b> — ask about <b>obesity, growth problems, diabetes, infertility and thyroid disease</b> in the family (dominant, recessive or clustered inheritance). Box 53.4: when it started and how fast; past treatment and whether it worked; the effect on <b>activities of daily living</b>; energy; feeling <b>hot or cold</b>; sleep; hair and facial or body changes; <b>eye protrusion</b>; voice; diet and <b>weight in 6 months</b>; <b>urination amount and night waking</b>; bowel habits; menses; libido and sexual concerns.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Physical assessment</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Inspection — general and head</b> — height, weight, <b>fat distribution</b> and muscle mass for age; scalp and body hair. Face: <b>prominent forehead or jaw</b>, <b>round or puffy face</b>, dull or flat expression, <b>exophthalmos</b> (protruding eyes with retracted upper lids).</div>\n<div><b>Inspection — neck</b> — the thyroid is <b>normally not visible</b>; the isthmus may show as it moves up when the patient <b>swallows</b> (stand in front and ask them to swallow). A <b>visible thyroid needs further assessment</b>. <b>Jugular vein distention</b> can mean fluid overload.</div>\n<div><b>Inspection — skin</b> — hypo- or <b>hyperpigmentation</b>; <b>fungal infections, slow healing, bruising and petechiae</b> with adrenal hyperfunction; <b>skin infections, foot ulcers and slow healing</b> with diabetes; <b>darkened skin over joints and scars</b> with some adrenal disorders (high ACTH and MSH); <b>vitiligo</b> with primary adrenal hypofunction (autoimmune loss of melanocytes). Document location, color, size and distribution of any change since the last visit or from the patient's baseline.</div>\n<div><b>Inspection — body</b> — nails malformed, thick or brittle (<b>thyroid</b>); <b>edema</b> of extremities and sacrum (fluid balance); chest size and symmetry; <b>truncal obesity and a \"buffalo hump\"</b>, and <b>reddish-purple striae</b> on breasts or abdomen (<b>adrenocortical excess</b>). Look at the <b>breasts in both sexes</b> — size, symmetry, pigment and any discharge — <b>gynecomastia</b> (low testosterone). <b>Hirsutism</b> (excess hair on a woman's face, chest or midline abdomen), excessive scalp hair loss or a change in hair texture, told apart from normal aging. Genitals: the amount and distribution of <b>pubic hair</b> change in <b>hypogonadism</b>; ask whether the <b>scrotum and penis</b> (or <b>labia and clitoris</b>) have changed in size.</div>\n<div><b>Auscultation</b> — heart <b>rate and rhythm</b> as a baseline — some endocrine problems cause <b>dysrhythmias</b>, others dehydration. Record <b>orthostatic</b> BP and pulse lying, sitting and standing. Listen over an enlarged thyroid for a <b>bruit</b>: hypertrophy increases blood flow.</div>\n<div><b>Which glands can be felt</b> — the <b>thyroid</b> and the <b>testes</b> can be examined by palpation. Depending on the setting, the <b>primary health care provider</b> or the <b>nurse</b> performs these exams; testicular examination is covered in chapters 61 and 64.</div>\n<div><b>Palpating the thyroid</b> — from <b>behind</b>, patient sitting straight with the head bent slightly forward; thumbs on the back of the neck, fingers curved to the front on either side of the trachea; feel the gland <b>rise on swallowing</b>. For the right lobe, turn the head to the right, displace the trachea gently with the left fingers and palpate with the right hand; reverse for the left. Note size, shape, symmetry, nodules, tenderness.</div>\n<div><b>Action alert</b> — <b>do not palpate or press on the thyroid</b> of a patient who has or may have <b>hyperthyroidism</b> — it can release a burst of hormone and trigger <b>thyroid storm</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — endocrine problems can change <b>behavior, personality, mood and self-image</b>: ask about handling stress, crying, patience and anger, and, with permission, ask family — patients often miss these changes. Changes in appearance, <b>infertility or impotence</b> affect sexuality and self-image; invite feelings. Many need <b>lifelong drugs and follow-up</b>: assess readiness to learn, self-management ability and <b>resources</b>, and refer.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The physical exam is a search for <b>the hormone's fingerprints</b>: cortisol leaves a round face, buffalo hump, striae and bruises; thyroid hormone changes the eyes, nails, heart rate and a visible neck; aldosterone and ADH show up as edema, JVD or orthostatic drops. And the one thing you must not do — pressing on a hyperthyroid gland — follows from the same physiology: the gland is full of stored hormone waiting to be released.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"Diagnostic tests and the nurse's job","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Laboratory tests</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Which fluid</b> — blood, urine and <b>saliva</b>. Salivary levels of <b>steroid hormones</b> (cortisol, testosterone, progesterone, estradiol) mirror blood levels; <b>protein hormones</b> (pituitary, thyroid) are <b>not</b> measured in saliva. Check the agency lab for tube type, timing and handling.</div>\n<div><b>Assays</b> — measure a specific hormone: <b>antibody-based immunoassays</b> and <b>chromatographic assays</b>, including <b>mass spectrometry</b> (identifies hormones by molecular mass and chemical makeup; very sensitive, many hormones at once).</div>\n<div><b>Provocative and suppression tests</b> — used when a level alone can't separate normal from abnormal. <b>Provocative (stimulation)</b>: for a suspected <b>underactive</b> gland, give a measured stimulating hormone — <b>failure to rise = hypofunction</b>. <b>Suppression</b>: for <b>high or high-normal</b> levels, give a substance that normally suppresses the gland — <b>failure to fall = hyperfunction</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Blood draws done right (Box 53.5)</b> — check tube type, timing and test drugs; some samples need special handling — <b>catecholamines go on ice to the lab immediately</b>. Explain restrictions. From an IV line, <b>clear it thoroughly</b> and <b>never sample from a double- or triple-lumen line</b> (contamination or dilution). Stress taking a <b>test drug on time</b> — set an alarm if it is due overnight.</div>\n<div><b>24-hour urine (Box 53.5)</b> — many hormones are released in pulses, so a <b>24-hour collection</b> reflects gland function (e.g., adrenal) better than one sample. Check the lab's handling rules first; any <b>preservative must be in the container when the collection begins</b>. <b>Start by emptying the bladder and discarding that urine</b> — timing starts then; note the time. Collect <b>all</b> urine for <b>exactly</b> 24 hours, then <b>empty the bladder at the end even without the urge</b> and add it. Keep any <b>preservative</b> in the container and avoid splashing (some are caustic); keep it cold if required in a cooler with ice, <b>not with food or drinks</b>. Shorter timed collections (<b>2, 4, 8 or 12 hours</b>) follow the same start and end rules. Check with the provider before any new drug — some interfere. That includes <b>over-the-counter</b> drugs.</div>\n<div><b>Glucose, A1C and genetic tests</b> — blood glucose and the <b>oral glucose tolerance test</b> diagnose diabetes; <b>A1C reflects average glucose over 100–120 days</b>. <b>Genetic testing</b> helps when hormone levels are too low to measure or family history is unknown.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Most endocrine test errors are made at the bedside, not in the lab: the first morning void kept instead of discarded, a dose of the test drug taken late, a sample drawn from the wrong lumen, a catecholamine tube left at room temperature. Each one changes the number the diagnosis rests on — so the teaching is the intervention.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Imaging and other tests</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Imaging</b> — skull x-rays show the <b>sella turcica</b> (erosion = a growth invading it). <b>MRI with contrast is the most sensitive for the pituitary</b> (CT also works). <b>CT</b> for the adrenals, ovaries and pancreas. <b>Ultrasound</b> for the thyroid, parathyroids, ovaries and testes.</div>\n<div><b>Thyroid scan</b> — a nuclear medicine test: the patient swallows a pill of <b>radioactive iodine</b>, which collects in the thyroid, and the gland is imaged <b>4 to 6 hours later</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fine needle aspiration</b> — <b>ultrasound-guided FNA</b> is a quick, safe outpatient procedure that shows what a <b>thyroid nodule</b> is made of and whether surgery is needed.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"53-adpie","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">An assessment chapter has one aim for the nursing process: <b>find the gland and the direction of the problem</b> — excess, deficiency or poor response — then make sure the tests that confirm it are collected so the result can be trusted, while protecting the patient from the dangers that come with the work-up.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>age and <b>sex assigned at birth</b>; onset (gradual or sudden) and past treatment; energy, sleep, <b>heat or cold intolerance</b>; weight change over 6 months; <b>thirst, urine volume, night waking</b>; bowel habits; <b>menarche, menses and menopause</b>, libido and sexual concerns; <b>prescribed and OTC drugs</b> (cortisone, levothyroxine, contraceptives, antihypertensives, misused hormones); <b>iodine intake</b>; family history of diabetes, thyroid disease, obesity, growth problems, infertility.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div>body build and <b>fat distribution</b>; face (puffy, round, prominent jaw, <b>exophthalmos</b>); <b>visible thyroid</b>, JVD; skin pigment, <b>vitiligo</b>, bruising, infections, <b>foot ulcers</b>, slow healing; nails; edema; <b>buffalo hump, striae</b>, gynecomastia, hirsutism; heart rate and rhythm, <b>orthostatic vital signs</b>, thyroid <b>bruit</b>; thyroid palpation — <b>never if hyperthyroidism is suspected</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Psychosocial and learning</div>coping, support, health beliefs; changes in <b>mood, patience, crying and anger</b> (ask family with permission); feelings about appearance, fertility and sexual function; <b>readiness to learn</b>, self-management ability and <b>money for lifelong treatment</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Endocrine onset is often <b>slow and insidious</b>, so the patient may not notice — weight creeping up over six months, a voice changing, a personality shift the family sees first. The history is built to catch slow change, which is why it asks about <b>months</b>, not days.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Fluid and electrolyte imbalance</div>potential for <b>dehydration</b> (especially older adults with low ADH reserve) or fluid overload, and for sodium, potassium and calcium imbalance, because aldosterone, ADH, PTH and calcitonin all regulate them.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Glucose dysregulation</div>potential for <b>hyperglycemia</b> with decreased glucose tolerance in aging, stress hormones, illness (including COVID-19) and steroid drugs; potential hypoglycemia when insulin acts unopposed.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Body image and knowledge</div><b>disturbed body image</b> from visible changes (face, eyes, hair, weight, striae); and a <b>need for teaching</b> about tests that only work when collected exactly right.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>the patient keeps <b>fluid balance</b> (stable weight, moist mucous membranes, no orthostatic drop); test specimens are collected <b>correctly the first time</b>; abnormal results are <b>reported promptly</b>; the patient <b>states feelings</b> about changes in appearance or function; and the patient explains how to avoid <b>misusing hormones</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Testing</div>confirm tube, timing and handling with the lab; <b>ice catecholamine samples</b> and send at once; draw from a <b>cleared single-lumen</b> line only; give or remind about <b>test drugs on time</b>; teach the <b>24-hour urine</b> (discard the first void, keep everything after, end with a final void, keep the preservative, keep cold if required). Report abnormal results and any <b>complications of testing</b> to the provider.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Safety and comfort</div><b>no palpation of a possibly hyperthyroid gland</b>; <b>orthostatic precautions</b> when blood pressure drops on standing; for older adults, <b>fluids at least every 2 hours</b> while awake unless restricted, layers for cold intolerance, skin protection and perineal care.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Teaching and support</div>teach the signs of diabetes to report and suggest testing when wounds will not heal or yeast infections persist; teach the <b>dangers of misusing hormones and steroids</b>; encourage regular screening; invite concerns about appearance and sexuality; refer for <b>cost of lifelong treatment</b>; communicate clearly what each test involves.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>weight, intake and output and orthostatic readings stay stable; the specimen arrives <b>valid</b> (right tube, timing, temperature, complete collection); abnormal values were <b>reported</b>; the patient can repeat the collection steps and name hormone-misuse dangers; the patient talks about body changes and knows where to get support.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-overview","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">55 · Chapter 55</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cellular regulation · nutrition · gas exchange</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Thyroid and parathyroid conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> Thyroid hormone sets the <b>speed of every cell</b>, and parathyroid hormone guards <b>serum calcium</b>. So the thyroid diseases are two mirror images — <b>hypothyroidism</b> slows everything (the chapter's exemplar) and <b>hyperthyroidism</b> speeds everything — and the parathyroid diseases are calcium too low or too high. The dangerous moments are the extremes: <b>myxedema coma</b>, <b>thyroid storm</b>, the airway and calcium after <b>neck surgery</b>, and <b>tetany</b>. Learn the direction of the problem and the findings follow.</p><div id=\"ch55-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">TRH → TSH → T3/T4 sets the metabolic rate; T3/T4 switch TSH off. PTH keeps calcium in a narrow range by pulling it from bone, kidney and gut.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Too little thyroid hormone (Hashimoto, surgery, radioactive iodine), too much (Graves, toxic nodules, too much replacement), or too little or too much PTH.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hypo: cold, tired, slow, constipated, weight gain, slow pulse. Hyper: hot, sweaty, wired, weight loss with hunger, fast pulse, eye changes. Calcium low: tingling, twitching, tetany; high: stones, bones, groans, confusion.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">T3, T4 and free T4 with <b>TSH</b> (high TSH + low T4 = primary hypothyroidism; low TSH + high T4 = Graves); TRAb; radioactive iodine uptake; ultrasound; calcium, phosphorus, PTH, magnesium, vitamin D.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Levothyroxine for life (hypo); methimazole, iodine, beta blockers, radioactive iodine or thyroidectomy (hyper); calcium and vitamin D (hypopara); parathyroidectomy or cinacalcet (hyperpara).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Myxedema coma, thyroid storm, hemorrhage or stridor after thyroidectomy, hypocalcemic tetany, hypercalcemic confusion and coma.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hypothyroid patient with falling consciousness, hypothermia or slow breathing; hyperthyroid patient whose temperature rises <b>even 1°F</b>; stridor, drooling or tingling lips after neck surgery.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Low thyroid hormone<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>pituitary raises TSH<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>gland enlarges (goiter) but makes no more hormone<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>metabolism slows in every organ<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>slow heart, slow breathing, cold, constipation<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>levothyroxine — started low, raised slowly</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Hypo vs hyper — one line each</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Hypo</b>: cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation, bradycardia, lethargy, dry coarse skin. <b>Hyper</b>: heat intolerance, weight loss with increased appetite, more stools, tachycardia, anxiety, warm moist skin.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Myxedema coma vs thyroid storm</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Coma: <b>low</b> temperature, sodium, glucose, pulse, BP and breathing. Storm: <b>high fever</b> and <b>tachycardia</b>, then agitation, delirium, seizures.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Hypocalcemia vs hypercalcemia</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Low: <b>tingling around the mouth, fingers and toes</b>, twitching, Chvostek and Trousseau signs, tetany, seizures. High: kidney stones, fractures, GI upset, fatigue, confusion above 12 mg/dL.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Levothyroxine raises metabolism, so too much too fast stresses the heart → start low in older and cardiac patients. Methimazole blocks new hormone but not stored hormone → relief is delayed. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Pulse and rhythm, temperature, BP, respiratory rate and SpO₂, mental status, weight, bowel pattern and sleep, serum calcium after neck surgery.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Temperature up 1°F in hyperthyroidism; stridor, dyspnea, drooling or bleeding behind the neck after thyroidectomy; tingling lips or twitching; falling consciousness in hypothyroidism.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why a goiter appears in both diseases</b> — when the thyroid makes too little hormone, the pituitary keeps raising <b>TSH</b>, and TSH makes thyroid cells <b>grow</b> — the gland enlarges without making more hormone. In <b>Graves disease</b>, antibodies stimulate the TSH receptor, so the gland grows <b>and</b> overproduces. A goiter says \"thyroid problem\", <b>not which direction</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Myxedema — what the swelling is</b> — with low metabolism, <b>glycosaminoglycans</b> (protein-sugar compounds) build up in tissues and hold mucus and water: <b>nonpitting edema</b>, especially pretibial and on top of the feet, a <b>thick enlarged tongue</b> (macroglossia), puffy face and periorbital edema.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The heart in each disease</b> — <b>hypo</b>: the heart muscle becomes <b>flabby</b> and the chambers enlarge → <b>cardiac output falls</b> → poor perfusion and gas exchange to brain and organs. <b>Hyper</b>: hormone drives rate and stroke volume up → <b>cardiac output, systolic BP and blood flow rise</b>, the diastolic pressure falls (<b>wide pulse pressure</b>), and <b>atrial fibrillation</b> appears.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Metabolism in hyperthyroidism</b> — protein breakdown exceeds buildup, <b>glucose tolerance falls</b> (hyperglycemia), fat stores fall — so the patient <b>loses weight despite a bigger appetite</b> and develops nutrition deficits.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Calcium and PTH</b> — PTH makes the kidney <b>reabsorb calcium and excrete phosphorus</b>, releases calcium from <b>bone</b> and, through vitamin D, from the <b>gut</b>. Calcium and phosphorus move in <b>opposite directions</b>: too little PTH = low calcium + high phosphorus; too much PTH = high calcium + low phosphorus.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Low calcium makes nerves and muscles <b>too excitable</b> — it normally steadies the sodium channels in nerve membranes — which is why hypocalcemia shows as tingling, twitching and tetany, and why it is an airway emergency when the larynx spasms. High calcium does the reverse: muscles weaken, the gut slows, the brain clouds.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Hypothyroidism — the cellular regulation exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is and who gets it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition</b> — reduced or absent thyroid hormone secretion → <b>whole-body slowing of metabolism</b>. Early on it is easily missed: onset is <b>gradual</b> and symptoms mimic many other diseases.</div>\n<div><b>Causes (Box 55.1)</b> — most US cases are <b>Hashimoto thyroiditis</b>, an autoimmune attack on thyroid tissue. Also common in North America: <b>thyroid surgery</b> and <b>radioactive iodine</b> treatment of hyperthyroidism. Worldwide: <b>iodine-poor soil and water</b> (endemic goiter) — the gland needs dietary <b>iodine and tyrosine</b> to make hormone. Drugs: <b>amiodarone, lithium</b>. Cancer (thyroid or metastatic), congenital problems. All of these are <b>primary</b> (the gland itself fails). <b>Secondary</b>: not enough TSH from congenital pituitary defects or pituitary or hypothalamic tumors, trauma, infection or infarction.</div>\n<div><b>Who</b> — <b>4–10% of adults</b>; women <b>5 to 8 times</b> more often than men. A congenital form is genetic, and thyroid dysfunction is linked to Down syndrome, Turner syndrome and atrial fibrillation. There is <b>no known prevention</b> — catch it early.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — secretion falls with age but so does clearance, so <b>don't rely on labs alone</b> — use <b>changes in symptoms</b> to judge replacement (symptoms are often milder at this age). Muscle loss is normal aging, so don't use strength as a sign. Age-related heart and nerve changes make them <b>more sensitive to replacement</b> — <b>start low, increase slowly</b> to avoid cardiac and neurologic toxicity, and assess for <b>chest pain, shortness of breath</b> and mental status changes (<b>confusion</b>, changes in sleep). Amiodarone can change thyroid levels.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What you find (Box 55.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Ask</b> — activity now <b>compared with a year ago</b> (changes are slow and easily missed, especially in older adults); more <b>sleep</b>; <b>fatigue</b>, weakness, muscle aches, <b>paresthesias</b>; <b>constipation</b>; <b>cold intolerance</b> (extra blankets or a sweater in warm weather); low libido, heavy prolonged periods or amenorrhea, trouble conceiving (anovulation), impotence and infertility in men; <b>lithium or amiodarone</b>; past hyperthyroid treatment and which one; <b>COVID-19</b> (can aggravate autoimmune thyroid disease).</div>\n<div><b>See</b> — <b>coarse dry scaly skin</b> (cool, pale or ash gray) with <b>poor wound healing</b>; dry, coarse, brittle hair that grows less, <b>loss of eyebrow hair</b>, thick brittle nails; <b>puffy face and periorbital edema</b>, <b>thick tongue</b>, <b>hoarseness</b>, <b>nonpitting edema of the hands and feet</b>; slow movement, <b>slow or slurred speech</b> (the thick tongue) and slow answers; <b>weight gain</b> without overeating (weigh and compare with a year ago); possibly a <b>goiter</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Measure</b> — pulse <b>below 60</b>, slow respirations (<b>hypoventilation</b>), temperature <b>below 97°F (36.1°C)</b>, <b>diastolic hypertension</b>, decreased tendon reflexes, <b>hearing loss</b>, decreased activity tolerance, dyspnea, pericardial or pleural effusion, abdominal distention or ascites.</div>\n<div><b>Mind and mood</b> — <b>apathy and depression</b> — sometimes the family brings the patient in; lethargy may keep them from noticing they have changed. <b>Confusion</b>, lethargy or somnolence, inattentiveness; impaired <b>attention and memory</b> and slowed thinking — families describe the patient as <b>withdrawn</b> → <b>social isolation</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Labs (Table 55.1)</b> — <b>T3 and T4 low</b>. <b>TSH high in primary</b> hypothyroidism (the gland fails, the pituitary shouts), <b>low or near normal in secondary</b> (the pituitary or hypothalamus fails). Older adults tend to run higher TSH.</div>\n<div><b>Normal ranges (Table 55.1)</b> — ranges vary by lab. <b>Serum T3</b>: <b>70–205 ng/dL</b> (1.2–3.4 nmol/L) at ages 20–50; <b>40–180 ng/dL</b> (0.6–2.8 nmol/L) over 50. <b>Total T4</b>: men <b>4–12 mcg/dL</b> (59–135 nmol/L); women <b>5–12 mcg/dL</b> (71–142 nmol/L); adults over 60 <b>5–11 mcg/dL</b> (64–142 nmol/L). <b>\"Direct\" free T4</b>: <b>0.8–2.8 ng/dL</b> (10–36 pmol/L). <b>TSH</b>: <b>0.3–5 μU/mL</b> (0.3–5 mU/L). T3, T4 and free T4 are <b>low in hypothyroidism</b> and <b>high in hyperthyroidism</b>. TSH is <b>high in primary hypothyroidism</b> but <b>low in secondary or tertiary</b> hypothyroidism; in hyperthyroidism it is <b>low in Graves disease</b> but <b>high in secondary or tertiary</b> hyperthyroidism.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every finding is the same idea — <b>not enough fuel burning</b>: less heat (cold intolerance, low temperature), less drive (slow pulse, slow breathing, slow gut, slow thinking), and tissues that collect water-holding compounds (puffy face, thick tongue). The danger is in the breathing: slow, shallow respiration plus any sedative can tip into respiratory failure.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Care and teaching</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. <b>Decreased gas exchange</b> from low energy, muscle weakness and fatigue. 2. <b>Reduced perfusion</b> from low cardiac output. 3. Potential for <b>myxedema coma</b>. Respiratory failure is the most common cause of death in myxedema coma.</div>\n<div><b>Gas exchange</b> — record rate and depth, <b>SpO₂</b>, oxygen for hypoxemia, listen for decreased sounds or crackles; ventilatory support if severe. <b>Avoid sedation</b>; if needed, use <b>reduced doses</b> — hypothyroidism makes patients more sensitive — and watch gas exchange closely.</div>\n<div><b>Cardiac output</b> — watch for a rising BP and <b>dysrhythmias</b> — monitor <b>BP, heart rate and rhythm</b> and act on changes before complications set in; chronic disease may bring cardiovascular disease. Teach to report <b>chest pain or discomfort immediately</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Levothyroxine — how to give it</b> — lifelong synthetic thyroid hormone. Start at a set dose and raise gradually over weeks; the <b>lowest starting dose</b> for severe symptoms, older adults, coronary disease or unknown severity — too much or too fast causes <b>dysrhythmias, heart failure and MI</b>. Final dose is set by <b>TSH</b> and how the patient feels. <b>IV</b> in myxedema coma (the gut absorbs poorly).</div>\n<div><b>Levothyroxine — drug alert</b> — take it exactly as prescribed, never change the dose alone. <b>With water, on an empty stomach, 30–60 minutes before breakfast.</b> Keep <b>iron, calcium and antacids at least 4 hours apart</b>; fiber supplements can also block absorption. Watch for <b>chest pain and dyspnea</b> when starting, and teach the signs of <b>too much</b> (hyperthyroid symptoms).</div>\n<div><b>Self-monitoring and teaching</b> — the two easiest checks are <b>sleep and bowels</b>: needing more sleep and <b>constipated</b> → dose may be too low; <b>trouble falling asleep and more bowel movements</b> → dose may be too high. Teach lifelong therapy, signs of hypo- and hyperthyroidism, <b>medical alert bracelet</b>, periodic blood tests, <b>no OTC drugs without asking</b>, a balanced diet with <b>fiber and fluids</b>, warmth until symptoms improve, rest. One person should be responsible for preparing doses so none are <b>missed or doubled</b>; one-floor living may help for a while. A patient on replacement who is <b>still tired and cold</b> (a sweater in warm weather) shows signs of too little hormone: take <b>vital signs and temperature</b>, <b>review the medication record</b> (dose, missed doses, how and when it is taken) and report — the provider may check the TSH and adjust the dose. Make sure whoever oversees daily care (family, facility staff) knows about the condition; arrange home health if needed.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — pulse <b>above 60</b> and BP normal for the patient; <b>SpO₂ above 93%</b>; improving cognition.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Replacement has to be slow because the heart has adapted to a low metabolic demand. Thyroid hormone raises <b>oxygen demand</b> in every tissue, including the myocardium — give a full dose to a 70-year-old with coronary disease and you have created the conditions for angina, dysrhythmia and infarction. That is also why the empty-stomach rule matters: absorption varies with food and minerals, and a dose that is absorbed unevenly cannot be titrated.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-myxedema-coma","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Myxedema coma","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Myxedema coma</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — a rare but life-threatening complication of <b>untreated or poorly treated hypothyroidism</b>: dangerously reduced <b>cardiopulmonary and neurologic function</b> (few patients are actually comatose). Mortality is extremely high — early recognition is everything. Low cardiac output starves the brain and other organs of blood and oxygen; <b>respiratory failure</b> is the most common cause of death.</div>\n<div><b>Triggers</b> — any hypothyroid patient who is <b>newly diagnosed</b> or has other health problems is at risk. Triggers: <b>acute illness, surgery, chemotherapy, stopping thyroid replacement, sedatives or opioids</b>.</div>\n<div><b>What you see</b> — greatly <b>reduced consciousness and cognition</b>, <b>respiratory failure</b>, <b>hypotension</b>, <b>hyponatremia</b>, <b>hypothermia</b>, <b>hypoglycemia</b>, <b>bradycardia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Action alert</b> — it can progress to <b>shock, organ damage and death</b>, so assess every hypothyroid patient at least <b>every 8 hours</b>, especially <b>mental status</b>, and report changes promptly. Treatment starts on the symptoms — <b>do not wait for lab confirmation</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Emergency care (Box 55.3)</b> — keep a <b>patent airway</b>; IV <b>normal or hypertonic saline</b>; <b>IV T3 and/or T4</b>; <b>IV glucose</b>; <b>corticosteroids</b>; temperature and <b>BP hourly</b>; <b>warm blankets</b>; watch mental status; <b>turn every 2 hours</b>; <b>aspiration precautions</b>.</div>\n<div><b>IV hormone and drug cautions</b> — thyroid hormone goes <b>IV</b> because the slowed gut absorbs pills poorly, but it carries the same risk as any start of replacement: too much too fast → <b>dysrhythmias, heart failure or MI</b> — watch rhythm, BP, chest pain and dyspnea, especially in <b>older or cardiac</b> patients. <b>Sedatives and opioids</b> both trigger and deepen the coma: avoid them; if one is essential, expect a <b>reduced dose</b> and monitor breathing and SpO₂.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Read the list of findings as \"everything that depends on metabolism has failed\": no heat (hypothermia), no drive to breathe (respiratory failure), no pump (bradycardia, hypotension), no fuel (hypoglycemia), and dilutional low sodium. Each line of the emergency box answers one of them — and warming is with <b>blankets</b>, not aggressive active rewarming, while the hormone is replaced <b>IV</b> because the slowed gut will not absorb a pill.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-hyperthyroidism-and-graves-disease","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Hyperthyroidism and Graves disease","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition</b> — <b>excess thyroid hormone</b> — the same name is used when the cause is too much <b>synthetic hormone</b> in someone with a normal gland. It <b>speeds metabolism in every organ</b> and increases sympathetic activity; it can be temporary or permanent.</div>\n<div><b>Graves disease — the most common cause</b> — <b>autoimmune</b>: often after thyroid inflammation, antibodies called <b>thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSIs)</b> bind the <b>TSH receptor</b> and drive the gland — more cells, a <b>goiter</b>, and overproduction. Only Graves adds <b>exophthalmos</b> and <b>pretibial myxedema</b> (dry, waxy swelling on the front of the lower legs). Diagnosed most often in <b>women 30–50</b>; linked to other autoimmune diseases (diabetes, vitiligo, rheumatoid arthritis), often in both identical twins — ask about family. COVID-19 can aggravate it.</div>\n<div><b>Other causes</b> — <b>toxic multinodular goiter</b> — nodules or adenomas in a goiter present for years; milder, <b>no exophthalmos or pretibial myxedema</b>; mostly women until 50, then equal. <b>Exogenous</b> hyperthyroidism — too much replacement hormone.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What you find (Box 55.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — weight and usual weight; <b>unplanned weight loss</b> with <b>increased appetite</b> and <b>more bowel movements</b>. <b>Heat intolerance is often the first symptom</b> — sweating, light clothes in cold weather. Palpitations, chest pain, dyspnea. Blurred or <b>double vision</b>, eye fatigue. Fatigue with <b>insomnia</b>; restless or anxious (family notices). Amenorrhea or lighter periods and lower fertility; decreased libido in women and men. Changes can be so gradual that the patient hasn't noticed them. Past <b>thyroid surgery or neck radiation</b> (some stay hyperthyroid after surgery or resist radiation), thyroid hormone or antithyroid drugs.</div>\n<div><b>Eyes</b> — <b>exophthalmos</b> (Graves): edema of the eye muscles and fat behind the eye push the globe forward → focusing problems, <b>optic nerve pressure</b>, and if the lids don't close, <b>dry eyes and corneal ulcers</b>; look for tearing, redness, <b>photophobia</b>. In <b>all</b> hyperthyroidism: <b>eyelid lag</b> (upper lid doesn't follow the eye down) and <b>globe lag</b> (lid pulls back faster than the eye looking up) — ask the patient to look down, then up.</div>\n<div><b>Neck — look, don't touch</b> — note size and symmetry; a goiter may be <b>4 times normal size</b>. The generalist nurse <b>does not palpate</b> the thyroid — in hyperthyroidism it can release a flood of hormone and trigger <b>thyroid storm</b>. A <b>bruit</b> may be heard from increased blood flow.</div>\n<div><b>Heart, skin, muscles, mood</b> — <b>tachycardia</b>, palpitations, <b>higher systolic and lower diastolic BP (wide pulse pressure)</b>, <b>atrial fibrillation</b>, rapid shallow breathing. <b>Warm, smooth, moist skin</b>, diaphoresis, <b>fine silky hair</b>, thinning scalp hair. <b>Tremor</b>, <b>hyperactive reflexes</b>, muscle weakness. Wide <b>mood swings</b>, short attention, anxiety, <b>hyperactivity</b>, restlessness and irritability; \"full speed ahead or completely stopped\" (poor sleep → fatigue); crying or laughing without cause. Men: erectile dysfunction, gynecomastia.</div>\n<div><b>Tests</b> — <b>T3, T4 and free T4 high</b>; <b>TSH low in Graves</b> (high in rare secondary or tertiary disease) — ranges in <b>Table 55.1</b> (free T4 <b>0.8–2.8 ng/dL</b>, TSH <b>0.3–5 μU/mL</b>; T3 and total T4 by age and sex in the hypothyroidism notes); <b>TRAb</b> (antibody to the TSH receptor) confirms Graves. <b>Radioactive iodine uptake</b> (oral ¹²³I, short half-life — <b>no radiation precautions</b>, but <b>rule out pregnancy</b>): uptake is increased and shows active nodules. <b>Ultrasound</b> (30 minutes, painless) for size and nodules. <b>ECG</b>: SVT, atrial fibrillation, PVCs.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The excess hormone acts like a <b>permanent sympathetic surge</b> plus a furnace turned up: the heart races, the skin sweats, the gut hurries, the muscles shake, and the patient is hungry yet losing weight. The low TSH is the pituitary obeying negative feedback — it has stopped asking for hormone — which is how you know the gland itself (or an antibody) is driving the excess.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-treating-hyperthyroidism-drugs-radioacti","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Treating hyperthyroidism — drugs, radioactive iodine, comfort","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nursing care while it is treated</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Monitor</b> — <b>apical pulse, BP and temperature at least every 4 hours</b>; teach to report palpitations, dyspnea, vertigo or chest pain at once. A rising temperature may be <b>thyroid storm</b> starting — <b>report an increase of even 1°F (1.8°C) immediately</b> <i>(1.8°C is the book's figure; a 1°F rise is really about 0.5°C)</i>; tell AP to report each temperature as soon as it is taken; if it is up, assess the heart right away and check the monitor for dysrhythmias.</div>\n<div><b>Reduce stimulation</b> — rest; a <b>quiet room</b> — door closed, fewer visitors, non-essential care postponed — to keep symptoms and cardiac risk down.</div>\n<div><b>Comfort</b> — <b>cool room</b>; AP keep <b>fresh ice water</b> at the bedside and <b>change damp linens</b> from sweating; cool showers or sponge baths several times a day; <b>artificial tears</b> for exophthalmos.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Drug therapy (Table 55.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Thionamides — first treatment</b> — <b>methimazole is preferred</b> (easier to use); <b>propylthiouracil (PTU)</b> is used much less because of <b>liver toxicity</b>. They block hormone production by <b>stopping iodide binding</b> in the gland. Response is <b>delayed</b> because stored hormone keeps being released. Remission is possible, especially with mild disease and a small goiter. The two are <b>not interchangeable</b> — PTU doses are much higher.</div>\n<div><b>Thionamide teaching</b> — <b>avoid crowds and sick people</b> — they lower the immune response; report <b>weight gain, slow heart rate, cold intolerance</b> (signs of hypothyroidism → dose may be too high); <b>methimazole causes birth defects</b> — report pregnancy (may be switched in the first trimester); with <b>PTU, report dark urine or yellow skin or eyes</b> (liver toxicity or failure).</div>\n<div><b>Iodine (SSKI)</b> — saturated solution of potassium iodide: give <b>orally, diluted in a glass of water or a beverage</b>; give <b>1 hour after a thionamide</b> or <b>1 week after radioactive iodine</b> to avoid worsening hyperthyroidism. Used <b>short term before surgery</b>: it reduces blood flow through the gland and hormone release; improvement within about <b>2 weeks</b>, though metabolism can take weeks longer to normalize. Can cause <b>hypothyroidism</b> — monitor and adjust the drug regimen.</div>\n<div><b>Beta blockers</b> — <b>atenolol or propranolol</b> relieve <b>sweating, anxiety, tachycardia and palpitations</b> — supportive only, they <b>do not lower hormone production</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>How it works</b> — oral <b>¹³¹I</b>, dosed to gland size and sensitivity. The thyroid takes it up and the local radiation <b>destroys some hormone-producing cells</b>. Stored hormone means relief takes <b>6–8 weeks</b>, so antithyroid drugs may still be needed early on. <b>Not in pregnancy</b> — it crosses the placenta and damages the fetal thyroid. Outpatient; one dose is often enough, but some patients need a second.</div>\n<div><b>Precautions at home (Box 55.5)</b> — the low dose usually clears the body within <b>a month</b>, but the source is unsealed: some radioactivity is in <b>urine, saliva and stool</b> for the <b>first few days</b> (saliva through the first week), so precautions cover that time. <b>Sit to urinate</b>; <b>flush 2–3 times</b> with the lid closed; condom catheter (men) or <b>flushable tissue layers</b> (women) for incontinence instead of gel pads. Stay <b>at least 6 feet from pregnant women, infants and young children</b> for the prescribed time (usually <b>at least a week</b>), including not sharing a bed. <b>Don't share toothbrushes or toothpaste</b>, <b>don't prepare food or share drinks</b>. Return to work and travel may wait only <b>a few days</b>, depending on the dose.</div>\n<div><b>Afterward</b> — many patients become <b>hypothyroid</b> and need <b>lifelong replacement</b>; hypothyroidism can appear <b>years later</b>, so follow-up thyroid tests continue. Radiation thyroiditis (mild, gone within a week) can follow in 5–10 days.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Both the drugs and radioactive iodine work slowly for the same reason: the gland is <b>full of stored hormone</b> on thyroglobulin, and nothing that stops new production stops that store from being released. That is why beta blockers are given meanwhile (they treat the effects now), and why iodine is the one drug that acts fast before surgery — it shrinks blood flow and release.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-thyroidectomy-and-thyroid-storm","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Thyroidectomy and thyroid storm","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Thyroid surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>When and how</b> — for hyperthyroidism that doesn't respond to other treatment, <b>severe disease</b>, patients who <b>can't tolerate</b> other treatment, or a large goiter <b>compressing the trachea or esophagus</b>. <b>Total</b> (then lifelong replacement) or <b>subtotal</b> thyroidectomy, often minimally invasive; surgeons avoid the <b>parathyroid glands</b> and <b>recurrent laryngeal nerves</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Before surgery</b> — thionamides first so the patient is <b>euthyroid</b>; <b>iodine</b> to shrink the gland's size and <b>vascularity</b> (less bleeding, less storm risk). <b>Hypertension, dysrhythmias and tachycardia must be controlled.</b> Teach deep breathing, to <b>support the neck with both hands behind it</b> when coughing or moving, and to expect a drain and dressing.</div>\n<div><b>After surgery — the routine</b> — monitoring for complications is <b>the most important nursing action</b>. Vital signs <b>every 15 minutes until stable</b>, then every 30. Pain relief; pillows supporting head and neck; <b>semi-Fowler</b> while awake; <b>avoid neck extension</b>; deep breathe every <b>30–60 minutes</b>; suction as needed.</div>\n<div><b>Hemorrhage</b> — most likely in the <b>first 24 hours</b>. Inspect the dressing <b>and behind the neck</b> — blood pools there. A <b>moderate amount of serosanguineous drainage</b> in a drain is normal. Bleeding can show as <b>respiratory distress</b> from tracheal compression.</div>\n<div><b>Airway</b> — swelling, tetany or <b>laryngeal nerve damage</b> can cause obstruction; <b>laryngeal stridor</b> (harsh, high-pitched) means acute obstruction. Keep <b>emergency tracheostomy equipment</b> in the room and oxygen and suction working. <b>Critical rescue</b>: stridor, dyspnea, <b>falling SpO₂, inability to swallow, drooling</b> → <b>call the Rapid Response Team</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Hypocalcemia and tetany</b> — if the <b>parathyroids</b> are removed, bruised or lose blood supply, PTH falls → <b>hypocalcemia</b>. Ask about <b>tingling around the mouth or in the fingers and toes</b>; watch for <b>muscle twitching</b>. Keep <b>IV calcium gluconate</b> available.</div>\n<div><b>Laryngeal nerve damage</b> — <b>hoarseness and a weak voice</b>. Check the voice <b>every 2 hours</b> and document; reassure that hoarseness is <b>usually temporary</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every complication after thyroidectomy is an <b>anatomy problem</b>: the gland sits on the trachea (bleeding and swelling compress it), the parathyroids are glued to its back (calcium falls), and the recurrent laryngeal nerves run beside it (the voice changes, or the cords close). Hoarseness alone is common and usually temporary; <b>stridor is not</b> — it is the airway closing.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Thyroid storm (thyroid crisis)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and why</b> — a life-threatening surge in <b>uncontrolled hyperthyroidism</b>, usually Graves: excess hormone release drives the metabolic rate dramatically up; <b>fatal if untreated</b>, and deadly even with treatment. Triggers: <b>trauma, infection, DKA, pregnancy</b>, <b>vigorous palpation of the goiter</b>, iodine exposure, <b>RAI</b>. Rare after surgery now, thanks to drugs beforehand.</div>\n<div><b>What you see</b> — key signs: <b>fever and tachycardia</b>. Also abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea; anxiety and tremors → <b>restlessness, confusion, psychosis, seizures, coma</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Critical rescue</b> — in any hyperthyroid patient — <b>even after thyroidectomy</b> — check temperature often: a rise of <b>even 1°F (1.8°C)</b> <i>(the book's Celsius figure; really about 0.5°C)</i> may mean impending crisis → <b>report immediately</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Emergency care (Box 55.6)</b> — <b>airway and ventilation</b>; oral <b>methimazole or PTU</b> (if not possible, <b>cholestyramine</b>, a bile acid sequestrant, to lower hormone levels); <b>iodide</b>, <b>non-salicylate antipyretics</b> (acetaminophen) and <b>glucocorticoids</b>; oral <b>propranolol</b> for heart rate; <b>continuous cardiac monitoring</b>; VS every <b>15–30 minutes</b>; <b>cooling blanket or ice packs</b>; <b>normal saline</b> for dehydration.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The antipyretic must be <b>non-salicylate</b> because aspirin displaces thyroid hormone from its carrier proteins and raises the <b>free</b> hormone — the active form — making the storm worse. Propranolol does double duty: it blocks the racing heart and also slows the conversion of T4 to the more active T3.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Graves eye care</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Eyes</b> — treating the hyperthyroidism usually <b>doesn't fully fix</b> the eyes; care is symptomatic: <b>elevate the head of the bed at night</b>, <b>artificial tears</b>, <b>dark glasses</b> for photophobia, <b>gently tape the lids closed at bedtime</b> if they don't close. Pressure behind the eye can cut blood supply → <b>ischemia and blindness</b>: short-term <b>high-dose prednisone, tapered</b>; selenium, <b>teprotumumab</b>; <b>orbital decompression</b> surgery if sight or the eye is threatened.</div>\n<div><b>Long-term teaching</b> — report any <b>return or increase</b> of hyperthyroid symptoms; know the signs of <b>hypothyroidism</b> and the possible need for <b>lifelong replacement</b>; keep follow-up — hypothyroidism can appear <b>years after RAI</b>, and most patients need <b>1–2 dose adjustments</b> after surgery.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"55-thyroiditis-and-thyroid-cancer","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Thyroiditis and thyroid cancer","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Thyroiditis — inflammation of the thyroid</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Infectious</b> — <b>bacterial</b>; acute: <b>sudden one-sided neck pain and tenderness</b>, malaise, fever → usually resolves with <b>IV antibiotics</b>. Chronic infection is less painful and may be on both sides.</div>\n<div><b>Subacute (granulomatous)</b> — <b>viral</b>, after a cold or upper respiratory infection: <b>neck pain</b> and a <b>hard, enlarged</b> gland (goiter). Thyroid function can stay normal. The usual course: <b>hyperthyroid → hypothyroid → back to normal</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Radiation and palpation</b> — <b>radiation thyroiditis</b> 5–10 days after radioiodine — mild, gone within a week. <b>Palpation or trauma-induced</b> thyroiditis after examination, biopsy, neck surgery or injury.</div>\n<div><b>Hashimoto (chronic autoimmune)</b> — antibodies and lymphocytes destroy thyroid tissue → <b>hypothyroidism</b>. <b>Painless enlargement</b> and <b>dysphagia</b>. Diagnosed by circulating antithyroid antibodies — <b>anti-TPO (TPO-Ab) is elevated</b>; hormone levels vary with the stage.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Thyroid cancer</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>First sign</b> — usually a <b>single, painless lump or nodule</b>; other signs depend on where it has spread.</div>\n<div><b>Four types</b> — <b>Papillary</b> — most common, women around 50, <b>slow growing</b> (often years pass before it reaches nearby <b>lymph nodes</b>), curable with partial or total thyroidectomy while confined. <b>Follicular</b> — ages 40–60, iodine-poor regions, spreads through blood to <b>bone and lung</b>; adhesion causes <b>dyspnea, dysphagia, hoarseness</b>. <b>Medullary</b> — sometimes inherited (<b>MEN2</b>, children and young adults), more often sporadic in adults 40–60. <b>Anaplastic</b> — rare, <b>rapid and aggressive</b>, invades nearby tissue, hard to treat: dyspnea, hoarseness, dysphagia. Papillary and follicular are called <b>differentiated</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — <b>African Americans</b> have higher risk of <b>anaplastic</b> cancer. Advocate for access to care, <b>telehealth and transportation</b> so it is found early.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — <b>surgery</b> for most: one lobe if small and contained, otherwise <b>total thyroidectomy</b> with neck node dissection if nodes are involved (same post-op care as above). <b>RAI</b> afterward to destroy remaining tissue (<b>not</b> for anaplastic); kinase inhibitors if that fails. <b>External beam radiation and/or chemotherapy</b> mainly for anaplastic, which has usually spread by diagnosis; radiation can also be used before surgery (to shrink the tumor) or after it (for remaining cancer). Afterward the patient is <b>hypothyroid</b> — teach replacement. Rarely fatal; refer for palliative or hospice care when needed.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"55-hypoparathyroidism-and-hyperparathyroidi","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Hypoparathyroidism and hyperparathyroidism","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Normal values (Table 55.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Parathyroid labs</b> — total <b>calcium 9.0–10.5 mg/dL</b> (2.25–2.62 mmol/L; slightly lower in older adults); <b>phosphorus 3–4.5 mg/dL</b> (0.97–1.45 mmol/L; critical below 1 mg/dL); <b>magnesium 1.3–2.1 mEq/L</b> (0.65–1.05 mmol/L; critical below 0.5 or above 3 mEq/L); <b>PTH 10–65 pg/mL</b> (10–65 ng/L); <b>vitamin D 25–80 ng/mL</b> (62.5–200 nmol/L). Hypoparathyroidism: calcium, magnesium, PTH and vitamin D <b>down</b>, phosphorus <b>up</b>. Hyperparathyroidism: calcium, magnesium and PTH <b>up</b>, phosphorus <b>down</b>, vitamin D <b>variable</b>. The table lists high calcium for <b>primary</b> hyperparathyroidism; in secondary disease (e.g., CKD) the trigger is low calcium (Box 55.7).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hypoparathyroidism — calcium too low</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and why</b> — rare: too little PTH (or PTH that doesn't work) → <b>hypocalcemia</b>. Most often <b>after surgery</b> — thyroidectomy, parathyroidectomy, or head and neck cancer surgery; also autoimmune or genetic. Box 55.7 also lists <b>radiation ablation</b> of the thyroid and <b>congenital dysgenesis</b> (glands that never developed properly).</div>\n<div><b>Ask and look</b> — head or neck <b>surgery or radiation</b>; serious neck injury (car crash, strangulation). <b>Mild–moderate</b>: <b>tingling and numbness around the mouth and in the hands and feet</b>. <b>Severe</b>: <b>muscle cramps, carpopedal spasms, seizures</b> (without loss of consciousness or incontinence); irritability to psychosis. Muscle contractions flexing the fingers, hands and elbows signal <b>impending tetany</b>. <b>Trousseau</b> and <b>Chvostek</b> signs; bands or pits on the teeth.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnose</b> — serum <b>calcium, magnesium, PTH and albumin</b>; then a <b>24-hour urine calcium</b>; vitamin D (correct any deficiency).</div>\n<div><b>Treat</b> — acute severe hypocalcemia: <b>IV calcium gluconate</b>. Milder: oral <b>calcitriol</b> (active vitamin D) and <b>calcium carbonate</b>; vitamin D for mild cases; <b>daily calcium</b> long term. Keep calcium in the <b>low-normal range</b> — enough to prevent symptoms, low enough to keep <b>urine calcium</b> down and prevent <b>kidney stones</b>. Teach calcium-rich foods <b>in moderation</b> (milk, yogurt, cheese, ice cream), that therapy is <b>lifelong</b>, and a <b>medical alert bracelet</b>; sticking to the drug and diet plan usually prevents a <b>hypocalcemic crisis</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hyperparathyroidism — calcium too high</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and why</b> — too much PTH → <b>hypercalcemia and hypophosphatemia</b>. In bone, PTH <b>slows osteoblasts and speeds osteoclasts</b> → calcium and phosphorus leave bone, <b>density falls</b>; chronic excess calcium <b>deposits in soft tissues</b>. <b>Primary</b>: one or more glands ignore calcium feedback. <b>Secondary</b>: <b>vitamin D deficiency</b> and <b>chronic kidney disease</b>. Others (Box 55.7): a <b>parathyroid tumor or cancer</b>, <b>congenital hyperplasia</b>, CKD with hypocalcemia, poor calcium absorption (celiac disease, bariatric surgery), drugs (diuretics, bisphosphonates), inherited <b>MEN1</b>, neck trauma or radiation, PTH-secreting cancers of the <b>lung, kidney or GI tract</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Ask and look</b> — fractures, weight loss, arthritis, stress, head or neck radiation. Chronic: <b>waxy pallor</b>, bone deformities. <b>Kidney stones</b> and calcium in the kidneys; <b>bone lesions, fractures, cysts, osteoporosis</b>. GI: <b>anorexia, nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain, constipation, weight loss</b>; high calcium raises <b>gastrin</b> → <b>peptic ulcer</b>. Fatigue and lethargy worsen as calcium rises; <b>above 12 mg/dL</b> (3.0 mmol/L): <b>psychosis and confusion → coma and death</b> if untreated. X-rays: stones, deposits, lesions; bone density loss.</div>\n<div><b>Without surgery</b> — for patients who aren't surgical candidates or want to delay surgery, monitor <b>calcium, kidney function and bone density</b> closely; nursing priorities are monitoring and <b>preventing injury</b>. Drug therapy: <b>cinacalcet</b> (a calcimimetic) binds calcium-sensing receptors on the parathyroids → <b>less PTH</b> → lower calcium, steadier minerals and slower bone damage; used for <b>more severe symptoms</b> or hypercalcemia from <b>parathyroid cancer</b>; monitor calcium routinely. <b>Bisphosphonates</b> are preferred for osteoporosis or fracture risk. Teach <b>regular exercise</b>, <b>good hydration</b> (prevents stones), <b>avoid lithium</b> if possible (it can raise calcium), <b>avoid prolonged bed rest</b>, a diet with <b>moderate</b> calcium and vitamin D. Bones are fragile: AP use a <b>lift sheet</b> to reposition — never pull.</div>\n<div><b>Parathyroidectomy</b> — the treatment of choice — usually <b>curative</b> (calcium returns to normal). Minimally invasive for a single adenoma, a transverse lower-neck incision when more than one gland must come out; all four glands are examined and only diseased glands are removed (all four if all are diseased). Pre- and post-op care match thyroidectomy; recurrent laryngeal nerve injury is <b>rare</b> — check for voice changes and hoarseness. The remaining glands have <b>atrophied</b> and take <b>days to weeks</b> to recover → <b>hypocalcemic crisis</b> risk: check every calcium result until stable, watch <b>tingling and twitching</b> of face and extremities, <b>Trousseau and Chvostek</b>. If it persists, hypoparathyroidism is <b>permanent</b> → lifelong calcium and vitamin D.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Hypercalcemia's old mnemonic — <b>stones, bones, abdominal groans and psychic moans</b> — is just PTH's targets turned against the patient: calcium dumped into urine forms stones, calcium pulled from bone leaves fractures, high calcium slows the gut and stimulates gastrin, and it dulls the brain. After surgery the problem flips overnight, because glands that were suppressed for years cannot restart at once.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-adpie","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Every plan in this chapter starts by naming the <b>direction</b> — too slow or too fast, calcium too low or too high — and then guards the <b>organ that fails first</b> in that direction: breathing and cardiac output when metabolism is slow, the heart and temperature when it is fast, and the airway and calcium after neck surgery.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Thyroid history</div>activity and sleep <b>compared with a year ago</b>; heat or cold intolerance; weight with appetite; bowel pattern; palpitations, chest pain, dyspnea; vision and eye discomfort; mood, memory and attention (ask family); menses, libido, fertility; <b>lithium, amiodarone</b>, thyroid hormone or antithyroid drugs; past <b>thyroid surgery, RAI or neck radiation</b>; COVID-19; family autoimmune disease.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Thyroid exam</div>pulse (rate, rhythm, <b>apical</b> when hyperthyroid), BP and <b>pulse pressure</b>, temperature, respirations and <b>SpO₂</b>; skin, hair, eyebrows, nails; face, <b>tongue</b>, voice; <b>eyes</b> (exophthalmos, lid lag, globe lag, lid closure); <b>goiter size by inspection only — no palpation</b>; <b>bruit</b>; reflexes and <b>tremor</b>; nonpitting or <b>pretibial</b> edema; mental status.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Calcium checks</div>ask about <b>tingling around the mouth, fingers and toes</b>; look for <b>twitching</b> and flexion of fingers and hands; test <b>Chvostek and Trousseau</b>; check every <b>serum calcium</b> after neck surgery; ask about stones, fractures, GI upset and confusion in hypercalcemia.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The year-ago comparison is the key question for hypothyroidism because the change is so slow that neither the patient nor a single exam notices it — the difference between then and now is the finding.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Hypothyroidism</div>1. <b>Decreased gas exchange</b> from low energy, weakness and fatigue. 2. <b>Reduced perfusion</b> from decreased cardiac output. 3. <b>Potential for myxedema coma</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Hyperthyroidism and surgery</div>potential for <b>thyroid storm</b> and <b>dysrhythmias</b>; <b>weight loss and nutrition deficit</b> from hypermetabolism; <b>eye injury</b> from exophthalmos; after thyroidectomy — <b>airway obstruction</b>, <b>hemorrhage</b>, <b>hypocalcemia/tetany</b>, laryngeal nerve injury.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Parathyroid</div>hypo: potential for <b>tetany, laryngospasm and seizures</b>. Hyper: potential for <b>fragility fractures</b>, <b>kidney stones</b>, peptic ulcer and <b>confusion</b>; after parathyroidectomy, <b>hypocalcemic crisis</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>hypothyroid: <b>pulse above 60</b>, BP normal for the patient, <b>SpO₂ above 93%</b>, improving cognition, no myxedema coma. Hyperthyroid: heart rate controlled, <b>no temperature rise</b>, weight stable, eyes protected. After neck surgery: <b>patent airway</b>, no bleeding, <b>calcium in range</b>, voice returning. Parathyroid: calcium <b>low-normal</b> without symptoms or stones; no fractures.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Hypothyroidism</div>monitor breathing and SpO₂, give oxygen, <b>avoid or reduce sedatives</b>; monitor BP, rate and rhythm; give <b>levothyroxine on an empty stomach 30–60 minutes before breakfast</b>, apart from iron, calcium and antacids by 4 hours; <b>start low, go slow</b> in older and cardiac patients; warmth; fiber and fluids; assess at least <b>every 8 hours</b> for myxedema coma and give <b>Box 55.3</b> care (airway, IV saline, IV T3/T4, IV glucose, steroids, hourly temperature and BP, warm blankets, turning, aspiration precautions).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Hyperthyroidism</div><b>apical pulse, BP, temperature at least every 4 hours</b>; report <b>any 1°F rise</b>; quiet room, rest, cool environment, ice water, dry linens; artificial tears and eye protection; <b>methimazole</b> (PTU less often), <b>SSKI</b> diluted, <b>beta blockers</b>; RAI precautions teaching; never palpate the gland; <b>Box 55.6</b> for storm (airway, antithyroid drug, iodide, acetaminophen, steroids, propranolol, cooling, saline, continuous monitoring).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">After neck surgery</div>VS every 15 minutes until stable; <b>semi-Fowler</b>, neck supported, <b>no extension</b>; check the dressing <b>and behind the neck</b>; <b>trach equipment, oxygen and suction</b> at hand; voice every 2 hours; ask about <b>tingling</b> and check calcium; <b>IV calcium gluconate</b> available; stridor, drooling, inability to swallow or falling SpO₂ → <b>Rapid Response Team</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Parathyroid</div>hypo: <b>IV calcium gluconate</b> for acute tetany; oral calcium carbonate and <b>calcitriol</b>; seizure precautions; teach lifelong therapy and a medical alert bracelet. Hyper: <b>hydration</b>, mobility, <b>lift sheet</b>, cinacalcet or bisphosphonates, avoid lithium; after parathyroidectomy check <b>every calcium</b> and watch for tetany.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>the patient keeps a <b>pulse above 60</b> and <b>SpO₂ above 93%</b> with clearer thinking (hypo); heart rate and temperature stay controlled and weight stabilizes (hyper); the airway stays open with no bleeding and <b>calcium stays in range</b> after surgery; the patient can state <b>how to take levothyroxine</b>, the signs of <b>too much and too little</b> hormone, RAI precautions, and the <b>tingling</b> that means low calcium.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-overview","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">56 · Chapter 56</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Glucose regulation · perfusion · tissue integrity · acid-base</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Diabetes mellitus</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> Diabetes is a failure of <b>glucose regulation</b>: either the beta cells make <b>no insulin</b> (type 1) or the body <b>resists</b> it and the beta cells slowly wear out (type 2). Glucose is affected first, then fat and protein metabolism. Everything in this chapter follows from two timescales — <b>minutes to days</b> (hypoglycemia, DKA, HHS, which kill if treated late or wrong) and <b>years</b> (damage to large and small vessels: heart, brain, eyes, kidneys, nerves and feet). The nurse's job is to keep glucose in range, catch the emergencies early, and teach the person who manages this disease every day: the patient.</p><div id=\"ch56-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Insulin (\"hormone of plenty\") moves glucose into cells and stores fuel; glucagon (\"hormone of starvation\") releases liver glucose; the brain needs a continuous glucose supply.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Type 1: autoimmune beta-cell destruction → absolute insulin lack. Type 2: insulin resistance → declining beta-cell secretion.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia</b>, fatigue, weight loss (type 1), blurred vision, infections and slow healing; in emergencies, Kussmaul breathing and fruity breath (DKA), confusion (HHS), shaking and sweating (hypoglycemia).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>A1C ≥ 6.5%</b>, fasting glucose <b>≥ 126</b>, 2-hour OGTT <b>≥ 200</b>, or random <b>&gt; 200</b> with symptoms; ketones, pH and bicarbonate, osmolarity; urine albumin.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Balanced eating, activity, glucose monitoring, metformin and other antidiabetic drugs, insulin; IV fluids, IV insulin and potassium in DKA/HHS; the 15-15 rule for lows.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">DKA, HHS, severe hypoglycemia; heart attack and stroke, retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, foot ulcers and amputation.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Glucose <b>&lt; 70</b> with symptoms, a patient who can't swallow, Kussmaul breathing with ketones, new confusion or seizures, potassium falling during insulin therapy.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">No insulin action<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>glucose can't enter cells<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>blood glucose rises → osmotic diuresis<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>dehydration + electrolyte loss<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>fat burned → ketones → metabolic acidosis<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>fluids first, then IV insulin, with potassium watched</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Hypoglycemia vs hyperglycemia (Table 56.8)</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Low: <b>cool, clammy, sweaty</b> skin, no dehydration, anxious, shaky, hungry, tachycardia; glucose <b>&lt; 70</b>; ketones negative. High: <b>warm, dry</b> skin, <b>dehydration</b>, Kussmaul breathing with <b>fruity breath</b> in DKA; glucose <b>&gt; 180</b>; ketones positive in DKA.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">DKA vs HHS (Table 56.9)</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">DKA: <b>sudden</b>, glucose <b>&gt; 300</b>, <b>ketones positive</b>, pH <b>&lt; 7.35</b>, HCO₃⁻ <b>&lt; 15</b>. HHS: <b>gradual</b>, glucose <b>&gt; 600</b>, osmolarity <b>&gt; 320</b>, <b>no ketones</b>, pH <b>&gt; 7.4</b>, worse <b>neurologic</b> signs; mostly older adults with type 2.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Dawn vs Somogyi</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Both = high morning glucose. <b>Dawn</b>: adrenal hormones at night → give <b>more</b> overnight insulin (NPH at 10 p.m.). <b>Somogyi</b>: a night-time <b>low</b> triggers a rebound → bedtime snack and <b>less</b> insulin. Tell them apart by checking glucose <b>during the night</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Metformin lowers liver glucose output without forcing insulin out, so it rarely causes lows alone; <b>sulfonylureas</b> squeeze insulin out of beta cells, so they <b>do</b> cause lows. SGLT2 inhibitors dump glucose in urine → dehydration, UTIs, yeast. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Glucose before meals and at bedtime (and with symptoms), A1C, intake and output and weight, potassium during insulin therapy, mental status, feet and skin, BP and urine albumin.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Glucose &lt; 70 → 15-15 rule now; can't swallow → glucagon or IV dextrose; Kussmaul breathing, ketones, vomiting or confusion; urine output &lt; 30 mL/hr before potassium; seizures or pupil changes in HHS.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Insulin — the hormone of plenty</b> — beta cells release insulin when glucose rises. It binds <b>membrane receptors</b> and opens cells to glucose (the \"key\"), especially skeletal and heart muscle. In the liver it <b>stores glycogen</b> (glycogenesis), <b>stops glycogen breakdown</b> (glycogenolysis), <b>stops gluconeogenesis</b> and <b>stops ketogenesis</b>; it builds protein and fat. Released in two patterns: a low steady <b>basal</b> amount, and a <b>prandial</b> burst within <b>10 minutes</b> of eating.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Glucagon and the balancing hormones</b> — <b>glucagon</b> (alpha cells), the \"hormone of starvation\", releases liver glucose when intake is low. <b>Epinephrine, norepinephrine, growth hormone and cortisol</b> also raise glucose. When glucose falls, insulin stops and glucagon rises; if liver glucose runs out, fat and muscle protein are broken down for fuel. <b>During a fast (no food for 8+ hours, or NPO)</b> insulin is suppressed, and the liver (and kidneys) hold glucose up by <b>glycogenolysis</b> (stored glycogen → glucose) and <b>gluconeogenesis</b> (amino acids and lactate → new glucose) — the two processes that directly prevent a low. Glycogenesis and lipogenesis store fuel instead, and ketogenesis makes acid fuel, not glucose.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Incretins</b> — gut hormones such as <b>GLP-1</b>, released when food reaches the stomach, <b>increase insulin</b>, <b>suppress glucagon</b> and <b>slow stomach emptying</b> — preventing spikes after meals. Several drugs copy or prolong them.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Without insulin (Box 56.2)</b> — less glycogen storage, <b>more glycogen breakdown, more gluconeogenesis, more lipolysis, more ketogenesis, protein breakdown</b> — the body acts as if it is starving while glucose piles up in the blood.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The three Ps</b> — <b>polyuria</b>: glucose in the urine pulls water with it (<b>osmotic diuresis</b>), taking electrolytes too → dehydration → <b>polydipsia</b> (thirst). Cells get no glucose → <b>polyphagia</b> (hunger), yet the untreated patient stays in metabolic starvation.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Ketones, acid and Kussmaul breathing</b> — burning fat yields <b>ketone bodies</b> — acids. Dehydration adds hemoconcentration, poor perfusion and <b>lactic acid</b>. The result is <b>anion-gap metabolic acidosis</b>: the brain drives <b>deep, rapid Kussmaul breathing</b> to blow off CO₂, the breath smells of acetone (<b>fruity</b>), and ABGs show low pH, low HCO₃⁻ and a compensating low PaCO₂.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Potassium can be low, normal or high</b> — urine losses drain total-body potassium (<b>low</b>), but acidosis shifts potassium <b>out of cells</b> in exchange for hydrogen ions, so the serum level may read <b>high or normal</b>. Insulin, correcting acidosis and fluids drive potassium back into cells — the serum level <b>falls fast</b> during treatment.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The \"starvation in the middle of plenty\" picture explains every acute finding: glucose is everywhere except inside the cells. The body answers starvation by burning fat (ketones, acidosis), breaking down muscle (weight loss, high BUN) and releasing more glucose (making hyperglycemia worse). Insulin is the only thing that ends the cycle — and when it does, potassium follows glucose into the cells.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-types-risk-and-screening","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Types, risk and screening","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Types of diabetes (Box 56.1, Table 56.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Type 1 (T1DM)</b> — <b>autoimmune</b> destruction of beta cells → <b>absolute insulin deficiency</b>; viral infections (mumps, coxsackievirus, COVID-19) may trigger it. Usually before <b>age 30</b>, <b>abrupt</b> onset: thirst, hunger, polyuria, <b>weight loss</b>. Ketosis-prone; <b>everyone needs insulin</b>. Same obesity rate as everyone else. Ask about family type 1, type 2 and other autoimmune disease.</div>\n<div><b>Type 2 (T2DM)</b> — <b>insulin resistance</b> first (cells respond poorly), then <b>declining beta-cell secretion</b>; strong heredity plus environment (walkability, food security, air quality, access to care). Any adult age, often <b>no symptoms</b> — or thirst, fatigue, blurred vision, or a complication is the first sign. <b>60–80% obese</b>; ketosis-resistant; <b>20–30% need insulin</b>. About <b>90–95%</b> of adults with diabetes.</div>\n<div><b>Other types</b> — <b>LADA</b> (\"type 1.5\"): slow autoimmune diabetes after 30. <b>MODY</b>: inherited single-gene loss of insulin function in younger adults — resembles type 1 (insulin, DKA risk) but <b>not autoimmune</b>. <b>Gestational (GDM)</b>: glucose intolerance first found in pregnancy — screen every pregnant woman.</div>\n<div><b>Metabolic syndrome</b> — risk factors that cluster for type 2 diabetes and heart disease: <b>waist ≥ 40 in (men) / ≥ 35 in (women)</b>; <b>fasting glucose ≥ 100</b>; <b>BP ≥ 140/90</b> <i>(the book's cutoff; many guidelines use 130/85)</i>; <b>triglycerides ≥ 150</b>; <b>HDL &lt; 40 (men) / &lt; 50 (women)</b> — or on drugs for any of these. Each speeds atherosclerosis, stroke and early death. It is diagnosed when <b>at least 3</b> of these are present (Fig. 56.5); the figure also shows darkened skin folds (neck, armpits), fatty liver, fatigue, and polycystic ovary syndrome (women) or erectile dysfunction (men).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Who gets it and who gets screened</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Numbers</b> — about <b>11.3%</b> of the US population has diabetes (37.3 million; <b>23% undiagnosed</b>); <b>96 million</b> have <b>prediabetes</b> (impaired fasting glucose, impaired glucose tolerance or <b>A1C 5.7–6.4</b>), with a <b>5–15 times</b> higher risk of type 2 within 3–5 years. Over <b>40% of hospitalized</b> patients have diabetes. Type 2 affects 12.3% of adults over 20 and <b>25.9% over 65</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — higher rates and burden in <b>American Indian/Alaska Native (14.5%)</b>, <b>non-Hispanic Black (12.1%)</b>, <b>Hispanic (11.8%)</b> and Asian (9.5%) people than non-Hispanic White people (7.4%). Be alert at every assessment and <b>advocate for early screening</b>. The CDC <b>National Diabetes Prevention Program</b> offers affordable lifestyle coaching for people with prediabetes in under-resourced communities.</div>\n<div><b>Veterans</b> — about <b>25%</b> of veterans have diabetes; <b>Agent Orange (dioxin)</b> exposure in Korea and Vietnam may add risk — assess at every visit.</div>\n<div><b>Screening (Box 56.3)</b> — test adults of <b>any age</b> with <b>BMI &gt; 25 (&gt; 23 in Asian Americans)</b> plus a risk factor: a <b>first-degree relative</b>, inactivity, high-risk ethnic group, <b>hypertension</b>, <b>HDL &lt; 35 or triglycerides &gt; 250</b>, <b>polycystic ovary syndrome</b>, cardiovascular disease. <b>Prediabetes → test yearly.</b> <b>GDM history → test every 3 years for life.</b> Everyone else starts at <b>35</b>, repeated at least every <b>3 years</b> if normal.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention and health promotion</b> — nothing prevents type 1. <b>Balanced diet, activity, weight loss</b> prevent or delay type 2. Teach: glucose in target delays complications; <b>yearly eye exam</b> by an ophthalmologist; <b>yearly urine albumin</b>; healthy weight; <b>at least 150 minutes of activity a week</b>; <b>daily foot inspection</b> and prompt reporting of sores; stop smoking; limit alcohol.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Type 1 and type 2 end in the same complications but begin differently: type 1 is an empty factory (no insulin at all, so ketones form quickly and DKA is the danger), type 2 is a factory whose product is ignored (some insulin remains, enough to prevent ketones — so the danger is the slow dehydration of HHS and years of silent damage before diagnosis). That is why so much of type 2 is found by <b>screening</b>, not symptoms.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-diagnosing-and-monitoring-glucose-a1c-cg","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Diagnosing and monitoring glucose — A1C, CGM, meters","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Diagnosis (Table 56.3, Box 56.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The numbers</b> — <b>A1C</b>: normal 4–5.7% · prediabetes <b>5.7–6.4%</b> · diabetes <b>≥ 6.5%</b>. <b>Fasting glucose</b> (no calories for 8 hours): normal 74–100 · prediabetes <b>100–125</b> · diabetes <b>≥ 126</b>. <b>2-hour OGTT</b> (75 g glucose): normal &lt; 140 · prediabetes <b>140–199</b> · diabetes <b>≥ 200</b>. <b>Random glucose &gt; 200</b> with classic symptoms or a hyperglycemic crisis. Fig. 56.1 gives <b>74–106</b> as the normal fasting range — the reference range the book's questions quote; a low is still treated at <b>&lt; 70</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Confirming it</b> — without obvious hyperglycemia, <b>two abnormal results</b> are needed (same sample or two samples) to rule out lab error. The OGTT is sensitive but used mainly for <b>gestational</b> diabetes. Screening usually uses <b>A1C plus fasting glucose</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Ongoing monitoring</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>A1C</b> — glucose binds hemoglobin (<b>glycosylation</b>): the higher glucose runs, the more is bound. Reflects about <b>120 days</b> (the red cell lifespan) and is <b>not changed by what was eaten the day before</b>. Check at least <b>twice a year</b> when stable, <b>every 3 months</b> after a therapy change or when out of target. Target: <b>&lt; 7%</b> for most nonpregnant adults without significant lows; self-management glucose targets are <b>set individually</b> (diabetes duration, age and life expectancy, other illnesses, severity of heart disease, hypoglycemia unawareness). Average glucose by A1C (Table 56.4): 6 ≈ 126 mg/dL, <b>7 ≈ 154</b>, 8 ≈ 183, 9 ≈ 212, 10 ≈ 240, <b>11 ≈ 269</b>, <b>12 ≈ 298</b> — each 1% adds about 28–29 mg/dL.</div>\n<div><b>Continuous glucose monitoring (CGM)</b> — a sensor under the skin measures <b>interstitial</b> glucose every 1–5 minutes and shows it on a receiver or phone, with <b>trend arrows</b> and alarms; sensors last <b>6–14 days</b>. <b>Time in range (70–180 mg/dL)</b> goal: <b>70%</b> of the time. Recommended for all type 1 and type 2 on insulin. Interstitial glucose <b>lags up to 30 minutes</b> behind blood when glucose changes fast → <b>confirm extremes or low symptoms with a fingerstick</b> before treating. Most sensors need <b>at least 2 fingerstick calibrations a day</b> (accuracy depends on them); with older systems, <b>give insulin only after confirming</b> the CGM reading.</div>\n<div><b>Blood glucose monitoring (BGM)</b> — a meter and capillary blood. Targets: <b>before meals 80–130</b>, <b>peak after meals &lt; 180</b>. Test more often with symptoms, <b>hypoglycemic unawareness</b>, illness, around <b>exercise</b>, gastroparesis, drug changes, steroids, pregnancy. Insulin users on multiple doses or a pump: <b>3 or more times a day</b>; fewer injections, non-insulin drugs or no drugs: a <b>daily</b> check helps judge therapy.</div>\n<div><b>Getting a true reading</b> — <b>anemia falsely raises</b> and <b>polycythemia falsely lowers</b> meter readings; meters are unreliable at very low and very high values; <b>lab glucose is more accurate</b>. Common errors: too small a drop, badly stored or <b>expired strips</b>, wrong code, expired meter. Teach with demonstration and <b>teach-back</b>, check vision, retrain over time.</div>\n<div><b>Alternate sites and infection control</b> — arm, thigh or hand readings lag when glucose changes fast — <b>the fingertip is the only safe choice then</b>, and patients with <b>hypoglycemic unawareness should not use alternate sites</b>. Wash hands, <b>never reuse lancets</b>, <b>never share</b> a meter or lancet holder (infection spreads even with a new lancet), clean the meter; staff and family <b>wear gloves</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Using the results</b> — after-meal checks show the effect of a meal; checks around exercise show how to exercise safely. If readings are <b>out of range for 3 days</b> with no change in food, drugs or activity, make the agreed adjustment.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A1C works because glucose sticks to hemoglobin and stays stuck for the life of the red cell — a 3-month memory that cannot be faked by a careful day before the appointment. A fingerstick is a snapshot and CGM is a movie; the movie arrives a few minutes late, which is why a scary CGM number gets a fingerstick before anyone treats it.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-chronic-complications-vessels-eyes-nerve","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Chronic complications — vessels, eyes, nerves, kidneys","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">How the damage happens</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The mechanism</b> — chronic hyperglycemia <b>thickens basement membranes</b>, glucose is directly <b>toxic</b> to cells, and small-vessel ischemia causes <b>tissue hypoxia</b>. Hyperglycemia is the main cause of <b>microvascular</b> damage and speeds <b>macrovascular</b> disease. Smoking, inactivity, obesity, hypertension and high lipids add to it. <b>Every 1-point drop in A1C</b> markedly reduces kidney and eye complications.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Large vessels (macrovascular)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Heart disease</b> — diabetes, prediabetes and metabolic syndrome raise cardiovascular risk; albuminuria and retinopathy predict coronary deaths. Targets (the book's cardiovascular section): <b>BP &lt; 140/90</b> (<b>120/80</b> in younger adults if reachable without excessive burden) — the kidney section gives <b>&lt; 140/80</b> (see Protecting the kidneys); <b>LDL &lt; 100</b>, or <b>&lt; 70 with cardiovascular disease</b>; lipid screen at diagnosis, then every 5 years under 40 or every 1–2 years. Omega-3s, fiber, plant sterols, weight loss, activity; stop smoking, manage stress, take prescribed lipid drugs and aspirin.</div>\n<div><b>Stroke and infection</b> — stroke risk is <b>2–4 times higher</b>, with more severe carotid atherosclerosis; hypertension, high lipids, nephropathy, peripheral vascular disease, alcohol and tobacco raise it further. Vascular changes plus high glucose <b>weaken white cells</b>, impair tissue oxygen and feed microbes → <b>more infections</b>, which turn serious fast and can lead to <b>sepsis</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Small vessels (microvascular)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Retinopathy</b> — blindness is <b>25 times more common</b>; after <b>20 years</b> nearly everyone has some retinopathy, with <b>few symptoms until vision is lost</b>. <b>Nonproliferative</b>: leaky, blocked vessels, edema, hard exudates, hemorrhages — slow, rarely blinding. <b>Proliferative</b>: hypoxic retina grows <b>new fragile vessels</b> (neovascularization) that <b>bleed</b> → vision loss. <b>Macular edema</b> is the main cause of vision loss. Also earlier <b>cataracts</b> and more <b>open-angle glaucoma</b>. High glucose blurs vision temporarily — <b>check refraction only once glucose is normal</b>. Prevent with glucose, BP and lipid control and <b>yearly eye exams</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults and vision</b> — age-related changes add to retinopathy: blurred, distorted or fluctuating vision and loss of color perception. Check whether they can <b>draw up insulin and read the meter</b>; offer <b>adaptive devices</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Peripheral neuropathy (DPN)</b> — progressive, <b>bilateral, permanent</b> nerve damage: sensory fibers → <b>pain first, then numbness</b>; motor fibers → weakness and <b>foot deformities</b>. Causes: hyperglycemia and high lipids, damaged vessels (nerve <b>hypoxia</b>), genes, smoking and alcohol; excess glucose becomes <b>sorbitol</b>, which collects in nerves. Table 56.1: <b>paresthesias</b> (burning, tingling from the toes up), <b>dysesthesias</b>, <b>loss of sensation</b>; high arch, <b>claw toes, hammertoes</b>, weight shifted to the metatarsal heads and toe tips.</div>\n<div><b>Autonomic neuropathy</b> — <b>heart and vessels (CAN)</b>: <b>orthostatic hypotension and syncope</b> (falls), exercise intolerance, <b>painless MI</b>. <b>Skin</b>: no sweating → dry, cracking skin. <b>GI</b>: reflux, <b>gastroparesis</b> (early fullness, bloating, nausea, vomiting; a cause of <b>hypoglycemia</b> because food arrives later than the insulin), bacterial overgrowth, <b>constipation — the most common GI problem</b> — alternating with diarrhea, nocturnal diarrhea, sweating while eating (Table 56.1). <b>Bladder</b>: incomplete emptying, <b>retention → UTIs</b>; frequency and urgency first, later no sense of fullness and incontinence. <b>Erectile dysfunction</b>. <b>Loss of hypoglycemia warning signs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Nephropathy</b> — diabetes is the <b>leading cause of end-stage kidney disease</b> in the US. Hyperglycemia raises pressure in kidney vessels; the <b>glomerulus leaks albumin</b>; narrowed vessels starve kidney cells → CKD. <b>Hypertension speeds it</b>. Shown by rising <b>albumin excretion</b> and falling <b>GFR</b>; delayed by glucose control.</div>\n<div><b>Sexual and cognitive function</b> — nerve and vessel damage → <b>erectile dysfunction and retrograde ejaculation</b>; in women, less lubrication, painful intercourse, lower libido. Older adults with diabetes have a <b>higher risk of all dementias</b> (neuron damage, brain atrophy).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Neuropathy is why the diabetic foot is so dangerous: sensory loss removes the warning (a stone in the shoe never hurts), motor loss deforms the foot and creates pressure points, autonomic loss dries and cracks the skin, and poor perfusion keeps the wound from healing. Each nerve type takes away one layer of protection — so foot care has to replace every layer by deliberate habit.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-antidiabetic-drugs-non-insulin","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Antidiabetic drugs (non-insulin)","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Principles (Table 56.5)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>When and how</b> — drugs are added for type 2 when <b>diet, activity and stress management</b> don't reach targets — always <b>with</b> them, never instead. Start at the <b>lowest effective dose</b> and increase; often more than one class. Choice depends on cost, side effects, ability to manage doses (once-daily or weekly helps adherence). Beta cells decline over time, so many eventually need <b>insulin</b> — indicated when 2–3 agents (including a GLP-1 agonist) fail, or <b>A1C &gt; 10% with symptoms</b>.</div>\n<div><b>For every antidiabetic drug</b> — teach the <b>signs of hypoglycemia</b> — hunger, headache, tremors, sweating, confusion — because many lower glucose even when it isn't high; and <b>check with the provider or pharmacist before any OTC drug, vitamin or supplement</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The classes</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Metformin (biguanide) — first line</b> — <b>lowers liver glucose production</b>, reduces intestinal glucose absorption and <b>improves insulin sensitivity</b>. <b>Take with food</b> (GI upset is common). <b>No alcohol</b> (lactic acidosis). Not for anyone with <b>kidney disease</b>. <b>Hold 24 hours before iodinated contrast or surgery with anesthesia</b>, and restart only when <b>kidney function is confirmed normal</b> — risk of lactic acidosis and acute kidney injury.</div>\n<div><b>GLP-1 agonists (incretin mimetics)</b> — <b>dulaglutide, exenatide, liraglutide, lixisenatide, semaglutide</b> act like the gut hormone: <b>less glucagon</b>, less liver glucose, <b>slower stomach emptying</b>. Mostly <b>subcutaneous</b> (semaglutide also oral) — teach injection. <b>Extended-release exenatide, dulaglutide and semaglutide are once WEEKLY</b> — stress the schedule to avoid overdose. Report persistent abdominal pain and nausea: <b>pancreatitis</b> risk; <b>contraindicated in gastroparesis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>SGLT2 inhibitors</b> — <b>canagliflozin, dapagliflozin, empagliflozin, ertugliflozin</b>: the kidney <b>can't reabsorb filtered glucose and sodium</b> → they leave in urine. Teach signs of <b>dehydration</b> (thirst, light-headedness, dry mouth, orthostatic hypotension), <b>hyponatremia</b> (weakness, cramps, fast heart, orthostasis), <b>UTI</b>, <b>genital yeast infection</b>, and report genital or perineal swelling, tenderness or redness — <b>Fournier gangrene</b>.</div>\n<div><b>DPP-4 inhibitors (gliptins)</b> — <b>alogliptin, linagliptin, saxagliptin, sitagliptin</b> (oral) stop the enzyme that breaks down GLP-1 and GIP, so natural incretins last longer; modest effect, <b>second line</b>. Watch for <b>rash and allergy</b>; <b>pancreatitis</b>; <b>heart failure</b> with saxagliptin and alogliptin (dyspnea, cough lying down, sudden weight gain).</div>\n<div><b>Pancreatitis warning</b> — with GLP-1 agonists and DPP-4 inhibitors, report at once: <b>jaundice</b>; sudden <b>intense abdominal pain radiating to the back, left flank or left shoulder</b>; <b>gray-blue discoloration</b> of the abdomen or around the navel.</div>\n<div><b>Insulin stimulators (secretagogues)</b> — <b>sulfonylureas</b> (<b>glipizide, glyburide, glimepiride</b>) and <b>meglitinides</b> (repaglinide, nateglinide) <b>release preformed insulin</b> from beta cells — only work if some beta-cell function remains. Sulfonylureas lower <b>fasting</b> glucose; meglitinides mainly boost <b>meal-related</b> insulin release. <b>Can cause hypoglycemia</b> (careful with insulin). Take <b>with or just before meals</b>. Sulfonylureas <b>interact with many drugs</b> — check before any OTC drug.</div>\n<div><b>TZDs (glitazones)</b> — <b>pioglitazone, rosiglitazone</b> improve <b>insulin receptor sensitivity</b> and lower liver glucose output. <b>Box warning</b>: not for <b>symptomatic heart failure</b> or certain cardiovascular disease; also more <b>heart-related deaths</b>, <b>fractures</b> and <b>macular edema</b>. With heart disease, <b>weigh daily</b> and report <b>&gt; 2 lb (1 kg) in a day or &gt; 4 lb (2 kg) in a week</b>; report vision changes at once; expect weight gain and edema.</div>\n<div><b>Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors</b> — <b>acarbose, miglitol</b> slow <b>carbohydrate digestion</b> in the intestine → no after-meal spike. Take <b>only with a meal</b>. Bloating, <b>flatulence</b>, diarrhea are common. No hypoglycemia <b>unless</b> combined with a sulfonylurea or insulin.</div>\n<div><b>Amylin analog — pramlintide</b> — copies amylin (made with insulin, <b>lacking in type 1</b>): <b>less glucagon, slower stomach emptying, satiety</b> → weight loss. Injected; for patients on insulin. Nausea and vomiting common. <b>Never mix in the same syringe as insulin</b> — incompatible pH.</div>\n<div><b>Combination products</b> — two mechanisms in one pill (e.g., dapagliflozin with metformin ER) — effective, fewer prescriptions, lower cost.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Sort the drugs by <b>where they act</b> and the warnings write themselves. Liver (metformin) → lactic acid if kidneys can't clear it. Beta cell (sulfonylureas) → lows, because insulin is forced out whether or not glucose is high. Gut and stomach (GLP-1, DPP-4, amylin, acarbose) → nausea, gas, pancreatitis. Kidney (SGLT2) → whatever leaves in the urine: water, sodium, and sugar that feeds bacteria and yeast. Receptor (TZD) → fluid retention and heart failure.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-insulin-types-timing-injection-and-stora","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Insulin — types, timing, injection and storage","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Types and regimens (Table 56.6)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Who and why injected</b> — all type 1 and <b>20–30% of type 2</b>. Insulin is a small <b>protein</b> destroyed in the GI tract, so it is <b>injected</b> (or inhaled). Older adults: poor vision, coordination or memory raise <b>dosing errors</b>. Concentrations U-100, U-200, U-300 and <b>U-500</b> (only for very large doses). <b>Insulin is a High-Alert drug.</b> <b>Analogs</b> (e.g., lispro) are human insulin with an altered structure that changes absorption and duration. The insulin type, injection technique and site all change its action, so the type is <b>changed only under the diabetes provider's supervision</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Timing by type</b> — <b>Rapid-acting</b> analogs — aspart (onset 10–20 min, peak 1–3 h, lasts 3–5 h), glulisine (10–15 min, peak 1–1.5 h, 3–5 h), <b>lispro</b> (15–30 min, peak 0.5–2.5 h, 3–6 h). <b>Short-acting regular</b>: onset <b>30–60 min</b>, peak <b>1–5 h</b>, lasts <b>6–10 h</b>. <b>Intermediate NPH</b>: onset 1–2 h, peak <b>6–14 h</b>, lasts 16–24 h. <b>Long-acting glargine</b> (onset 70 min, <b>no peak</b>, 18–24 h) and <b>detemir</b> (onset 1–2 h, no peak, 12–24 h). <b>Ultra-long degludec</b>: onset 30–90 min, <b>no peak</b>, 24+ h.</div>\n<div><b>Basal and bolus</b> — regimens copy the pancreas: <b>basal</b> insulin (NPH, glargine, detemir, degludec) about <b>50%</b> of the daily dose to cover between meals and overnight, plus <b>prandial</b> rapid-acting or regular before meals. Lower starting doses for older or very thin patients. The fasting (before-breakfast) glucose shows whether <b>basal</b> is right.</div>\n<div><b>Regimens</b> — <b>Once daily</b>: one intermediate, long or ultra-long injection (often with oral agents or a GLP-1 in type 2). <b>Two-dose mixed</b>: regular + NPH, <b>two-thirds before breakfast, one-third before the evening meal</b>; NPH's peak may require a <b>bedtime snack</b> to prevent night-time lows. <b>Multiple daily injections (intensified)</b>: basal plus a bolus before each meal plus <b>correction doses</b>, guided by up to <b>8 glucose checks a day</b> (2 hours after meals and within 10 minutes before the next).</div>\n<div><b>Skipped doses</b> — an occasional skip may follow an unusual meal or exercise day; if doses are skipped <b>often</b>, ask about barriers: <b>cost, fear of lows, fear of needles, needing help, embarrassment</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Giving it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Site and absorption</b> — <b>abdomen absorbs fastest</b> and is preferred — <b>except within 2 inches of the navel</b>; other usual areas are the upper arms, thighs and buttocks (Fig. 56.7). <b>Rotate within one area</b> (not area to area) so absorption is steady day to day; rotating lets each spot <b>heal completely</b> before it is used again. Longer-acting and larger doses absorb less predictably. <b>Heat, massage and exercising the injected limb speed absorption</b>; <b>scar tissue slows it</b> (and hurts less, which tempts reuse).</div>\n<div><b>Depth and angle</b> — <b>subcutaneous</b> — IM is faster and not used routinely. Lightly pinch and inject at <b>90°</b>; <b>45° for very thin or frail older adults</b>. <b>No aspiration.</b> Overweight patients can use <b>4–5 mm needles at 90° without pinching</b>. Insulin syringe needles come in <b>28, 29, 30 and 31 gauge</b> and <b>6, 8 and 12.7 mm</b> lengths.</div>\n<div><b>Lag time before meals</b> — <b>rapid-acting</b>: within <b>10 minutes before</b> eating when glucose is in range — or just before, or even right after the meal. <b>Regular</b>: <b>20–30 minutes before</b> eating. Glucose <b>high</b> → lengthen the lag; glucose <b>low</b> → inject right before eating (delay rapid-acting until after the meal).</div>\n<div><b>Mixing</b> — regular + NPH mixtures can give a more normal response in some patients, but mixing changes the <b>time of peak action</b>, and the response can differ from the same insulins given separately. <b>Never mix anything with glargine, detemir or premixed insulins (e.g., Humalog Mix 75/25)</b>; never mix pramlintide with insulin. <i>(Not in this chapter:)</i> when regular and NPH share a syringe, inject air into the NPH vial, then into the regular vial, and draw <b>regular (clear) first, then NPH (cloudy)</b>, so NPH never gets into the regular vial.</div>\n<div><b>Drawing up from a vial (Box 56.5)</b> — wash hands; check type and expiration; <b>gently roll</b> intermediate (NPH) insulin; clean the stopper; inject <b>air equal to the dose</b> into the vial; invert and draw the dose; remove bubbles and re-measure; use a site <b>not used in the past month</b>; clean, pinch, <b>90°</b>; push the plunger fully; release; pull straight out — <b>don't rub</b>; discard the syringe <b>uncapped in a puncture-proof container</b>. Syringes come in <b>1 mL (100 units), ½ mL (50 units) and 3/10 mL (30 units)</b>; the unit scale differs by size and maker, so always buy the <b>same type of syringe</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Pens (Box 56.5)</b> — check the label; NPH evenly mixed; wipe the tip, screw on the needle, remove both caps; <b>prime (\"air shot\") until a drop appears</b>; dial the dose; hold perpendicular, press slowly, <b>hold 6–10 seconds</b>; remove the needle into a hard container. Pens suit <b>poor vision</b> (each unit clicks). <b>ISMP alert</b>: a pen display read <b>upside down</b> (left-handed users) turns 52 into 25 and 12 into 21.</div>\n<div><b>Pumps and other devices</b> — <b>smart pens</b> (app calculates doses from insulin-to-carb ratio, correction factor and <b>insulin on board</b>). <b>Insulin pump (CSII)</b>: rapid-acting insulin every hour as basal plus meal boluses; flexible meals; <b>change the infusion set every 2–3 days</b> (skin infection); <b>DKA risk</b> from infection, blocked tubing or pump failure — <b>check ketones when glucose &gt; 300</b>; taking the pump off (shower, swim) can cause hyperglycemia, so have a backup insulin plan. <b>Automated insulin delivery</b> links a pump and CGM with an algorithm. <b>Inhaled</b> rapid insulin (onset ~12 min) only for nonsmokers without lung disease, after pulmonary testing; can cause bronchospasm.</div>\n<div><b>Dawn vs Somogyi (fasting hyperglycemia)</b> — <b>dawn phenomenon</b>: night-time adrenal hormones raise glucose around <b>5–6 a.m.</b> → more overnight insulin (e.g., evening NPH moved to <b>10 p.m.</b>). <b>Somogyi</b>: morning high <b>rebounding from a night-time low</b> → adequate <b>bedtime snack</b>, review insulin and exercise. Both are diagnosed by <b>checking glucose during the night</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Storage and inspection</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Storage</b> — <b>refrigerate insulin not in use</b>; the vial or pen <b>in use can stay at room temperature up to 28 days</b> (cold insulin stings). Never below <b>36°F (2.2°C)</b> or above <b>86°F (30°C)</b>, <b>never frozen</b>, no direct heat or light, no hard shaking. <b>Discard after 28 days</b>. Keep a <b>spare</b> of each type. Prefilled syringes: stable about <b>30 days refrigerated</b>, stored <b>needle up or flat</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Inspect before every dose</b> — look for <b>clumping, frosting, precipitate, or change in clarity or color</b> = lost potency → use another vial or pen. <b>NPH is uniformly cloudy after gentle rolling; every other insulin should be clear.</b> Needles and syringes are <b>single use</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Insulin types differ only in how fast they get from the injection site into the blood, so everything that changes blood flow at the site — heat, massage, exercise, scar tissue — changes the dose the patient actually receives. That is why rotation stays within one area, why the abdomen is preferred (steady blood flow), and why a hot shower after an injection can cause an unexpected low.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-eating-and-exercise-with-diabetes","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Eating and exercise with diabetes","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nutrition therapy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The approach</b> — <b>medical nutrition therapy</b> for everyone, planned with the <b>registered dietitian nutritionist (RDN)</b>, built around <b>culture, money, food access and lifestyle</b>. <b>Consistent timing and amounts</b> help control glucose; insulin users eat in step with their insulin's action. No ideal split of carbohydrate, protein and fat — it is individualized. Overweight: <b>losing 5–10% of body weight</b> markedly improves A1C; weight-loss drugs for <b>BMI ≥ 27</b> (≥ 25 in Asian individuals). Screen for <b>food insecurity</b> and refer. The RDN sees each patient <b>yearly</b> (more often with low health literacy); share diet teaching with <b>whoever prepares the meals</b>, and teach how to adjust food for illness, exercise, social events and delayed meals.</div>\n<div><b>Goals of nutrition therapy (Box 56.6)</b> — glucose in — or as close as possible to — the <b>target range</b>; a <b>lipid profile</b> that lowers cardiovascular risk; <b>BP</b> normal or near normal; <b>prevent or slow complications</b> by changing food and lifestyle; meet nutrition needs while respecting <b>personal and cultural preferences, food access and willingness to change</b>; cover special life stages (<b>pregnancy, breastfeeding, older age</b>); and, for patients on <b>insulin or insulin stimulators</b>, self-management training to <b>exercise safely</b>, <b>prevent and treat hypoglycemia</b> and manage diabetes during <b>acute illness</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Carbohydrates, fats, alcohol</b> — carbs from <b>vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes, dairy</b>; at least <b>25 g of fiber</b> a day; <b>avoid sugar-sweetened drinks</b> and sucrose. Fat quality over quantity: <b>Mediterranean</b> pattern (avocados, nuts, seeds, olives), <b>omega-3s</b> from fish or plants; limit trans and saturated fat as for everyone. Alcohol: up to <b>2 drinks for men, 1 for women</b>, with and in addition to the usual meal plan (<b>1 drink = 12 oz beer, 5 oz wine or 1½ oz spirits</b>), <b>with or just after a meal</b> — it blocks liver glucose output and causes <b>delayed hypoglycemia</b> hours later (worse with insulin or sulfonylureas); have someone with them who can help.</div>\n<div><b>Carb counting</b> — counts <b>total grams of carbohydrate</b> (labels, weighing, estimating when dining out). Patients on intensive insulin or a pump use an <b>insulin-to-carbohydrate ratio</b> — a usual start is <b>1 unit of rapid-acting insulin per 15 g</b> — adjusted by the 2-hour after-meal or next pre-meal glucose. Patients on <b>fixed doses</b> must keep carb amounts and meal times <b>consistent</b>.</div>\n<div><b>MyPlate</b> — for low health literacy or numeracy: <b>half the plate non-starchy vegetables</b> (a little fruit optional), <b>a quarter lean protein, a quarter whole grains</b>, tailored to the person's foods.</div>\n<div><b>Weight with insulin</b> — intensive insulin can cause <b>hyperinsulinemia and weight gain</b>: follow the meal plan, exercise, and <b>don't over-treat lows</b>; sometimes calories are restricted rather than insulin increased.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — dental problems, money, appetite and shopping or cooking limits threaten nutrition; they sense lows, highs and dehydration less (<b>HHS risk</b>). Changing 60–70 years of habits needs realism: teach <b>same meal and snack times and same amounts every day</b>; home health visits help.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Physical activity</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why and how much</b> — activity improves <b>insulin sensitivity</b>, glucose control, lipids (raises HDL), BP and heart function, and prevents or delays type 2. Aim for <b>at least 150 minutes a week of moderate aerobic activity over 5 days</b>, <b>no more than 2 days in a row without it</b>, and <b>resistance exercise twice a week</b>. <b>5–10 minute warm-up and cool-down</b>.</div>\n<div><b>What exercise does to glucose</b> — without enough insulin (uncontrolled type 1), glucagon and epinephrine pour out liver glucose while cells can't use it → exercise makes glucose <b>and ketones rise</b>. With insulin on board, muscles take up glucose and the liver holds back → <b>hypoglycemia during and up to 24 hours after</b> exercise. Hard anaerobic bursts (powerlifting, boxing) can raise glucose afterward.</div>\n<div><b>Safety rules (Box 56.7)</b> — check glucose <b>before, during and after</b>. <b>Urine ketones present → do NOT exercise.</b> Eat carbohydrate to get glucose <b>above 100</b> first; on insulin, exercise only with glucose <b>100–250</b> — <b>type 1: vigorous exercise only at 100–250 with no ketones</b>. <b>Planned</b> exercise → <b>lower the insulin dose</b>; <b>unplanned</b> → <b>extra carbohydrate</b> (about <b>10–15 g per hour</b> of moderate activity for a 70-kg adult). Snack first if more than an hour since eating or if intense. <b>Not within 1 hour of an injection or near peak action.</b> Carry <b>simple sugar</b> and <b>diabetes ID</b>; stay hydrated; avoid extreme heat or cold; <b>check feet</b> after.</div>\n<div><b>When complications are present</b> — <b>retinopathy</b>: avoid vigorous aerobic or resistance work, <b>heavy lifting, rapid head movement or jarring</b> (vitreous hemorrhage, retinal detachment). <b>Neuropathy</b>: proper footwear, daily foot checks; a foot injury or open sore → <b>non-weight-bearing</b> exercise (swimming, cycling, arm work). <b>Autonomic neuropathy</b>: poor temperature control, postural hypotension and impaired thirst → start short and gentle. Older adults: change activity <b>gradually</b> (a physical therapist evaluation may be needed); start with <b>short low-intensity sessions (under 5–10 min)</b> with warm-up, cool-down and active stretching — chair yoga, walking, water aerobics, bands — in safe places to prevent falls.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Ketones in the urine mean the cells are already starved of insulin — exercise would only add more stress hormones and push glucose and ketones higher, toward DKA. Without ketones, the same exercise works like extra insulin, which is why the danger flips to hypoglycemia for up to a day afterward. The ketone check is what tells you which way exercise will push.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Hypoglycemia — the 15-15 rule","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What happens</b> — below <b>70 mg/dL</b> the body releases balancing hormones and fires the autonomic nervous system. Normally insulin falls at about <b>83</b> and glucagon and epinephrine switch on at about <b>67</b> — before symptoms. The <b>brain can't make glucose</b> and stores only minutes' worth, so low glucose quickly becomes a brain problem.</div>\n<div><b>Symptoms (Box 56.10)</b> — <b>neurogenic (the early warning)</b> — adrenergic: <b>shaky, pounding heart, nervous</b>; cholinergic: <b>sweaty, hungry, tingling</b>. <b>Neuroglycopenic (the brain running out)</b>: weakness, fatigue, <b>difficulty thinking, confusion, behavior change</b>, emotional instability, <b>seizures, loss of consciousness</b>, brain damage, death. Skin is <b>cool and clammy</b>; no dehydration; ketones negative.</div>\n<div><b>Severity and unawareness</b> — <b>mild</b>: alert and able to self-treat. <b>Severe</b>: needs <b>another person's</b> help. Long-standing diabetes can bring <b>hypoglycemic unawareness</b> — no warning symptoms — so these patients test more and <b>never use alternate sites</b>. <b>Beta blockers mask</b> the warning signs.</div>\n<div><b>Causes</b> — too much insulin for the food and activity; insulin at the <b>wrong time</b> or the <b>wrong type</b>; <b>missed or delayed meals</b>; <b>gastroparesis</b>; <b>alcohol</b> (liver stops releasing glucose); <b>kidney failure</b> (insulin cleared slowly); <b>exercise</b>. At risk: everyone on <b>insulin or sulfonylureas</b> (alone or with metformin), especially older adults and those with liver or kidney impairment.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — slower kidney clearance of sulfonylureas and insulin, <b>weaker epinephrine and glucagon responses</b>, frequent unawareness. Family checks glucose for unsteadiness, light-headedness, poor concentration, trembling or sweating; <b>bedtime snack</b> if appetite is poor.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Treating it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>In the hospital</b> — check glucose <b>no more than 30 minutes before</b> a meal and give rapid-acting insulin <b>just before</b> the meal; coordinate trays and insulin. Monitor before antidiabetic drugs, before meals, at bedtime and with symptoms. Inpatient lows come from the <b>wrong insulin type</b>, insulin <b>not matched to the timing of food</b>, or a <b>changed eating plan without a dose change</b>; protocols give oral carbohydrate if the patient is alert and can swallow, otherwise <b>IV dextrose or glucagon</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The 15-15 rule</b> — glucose <b>&lt; 70</b> or symptoms → <b>15 g of fast carbohydrate</b> (<b>30 g if &lt; 50</b>), liquid if they can swallow — <b>avoid high-potassium choices like orange juice</b> in the hospital. <b>Recheck in 15 minutes</b>; still low → <b>repeat</b>. Once above the low range → a <b>complex carbohydrate with protein</b> (crackers with nut butter) to prevent a repeat. <b>Chocolate and fatty sweets absorb slowly</b> — use glucose.</div>\n<div><b>15 g examples (Box 56.11)</b> — glucose tablets or gel · <b>½ cup (120 mL) juice or regular soda</b> · <b>5 hard candies</b> · <b>4 sugar cubes or 4 teaspoons sugar</b> · <b>1 tablespoon honey or syrup</b>. Symptoms can <b>persist after glucose is back to normal</b> — go by the 15-minute recheck, then a small complex-carbohydrate and protein snack.</div>\n<div><b>Moderate and severe at home</b> — <b>moderate</b> (cold clammy pale skin, rapid pulse, rapid shallow breathing, mood change, drowsiness; usually &lt; 50): <b>30 g</b> fast carbohydrate, glucagon may be needed, recheck in 15 minutes, repeat if &lt; 60, then a snack. <b>Severe</b> (can't swallow, confused, combative, unconscious, seizing): family gives <b>glucagon IM, subcut or nasal</b>; <b>second dose in 10 minutes</b> if still unconscious; then <b>call 911</b> for IV dextrose; call the provider; a small meal once awake and not nauseated.</div>\n<div><b>Can't swallow — glucagon and dextrose</b> — <b>glucagon</b> (the main balancing hormone) is first-line for severe lows. It can cause <b>vomiting → turn the patient on their side</b>. <b>Concentrated IV dextrose</b> (e.g., D50) is <b>hyperosmolar</b> — give carefully to avoid <b>extravasation</b> and tissue damage. Both wear off: check glucose <b>every 15 minutes until resolved</b>, then monitor for hours; target <b>70–110</b>. <b>Critical rescue</b>: glucagon → repeat in <b>10 minutes</b> if still unconscious → notify the provider.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention and teaching</b> — personal hypoglycemia plan; <b>teach the family the 15-15 rule and how to give glucagon</b>; <b>medical alert bracelet</b>; <b>always carry glucagon, a carb source and a meter</b>; regular meal timing (<b>a meal delayed more than 30 minutes</b> raises risk on some regimens); test <b>before driving</b>; exercise when insulin isn't peaking; alcohol only with food; call the provider if lows occur <b>more than twice a week</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Fast sugar works because glucose needs no digestion — it goes straight from the gut into the blood. Fat in chocolate slows stomach emptying and delays it, and complex carbohydrates have to be broken down first; that is exactly why they are used <b>after</b> the low is fixed, to keep glucose up while the fast sugar runs out.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-dka-and-hhs-the-hyperglycemic-emergencie","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"DKA and HHS — the hyperglycemic emergencies","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — <b>uncontrolled hyperglycemia + metabolic acidosis + ketones</b>, from <b>insulin deficiency</b> plus burning fat and protein for fuel and a surge of stress hormones. <b>Most common trigger: infection or illness.</b> Onset is <b>sudden</b>.</div>\n<div><b>What you see</b> — <b>polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia</b>, <b>fruity breath</b>, <b>vomiting, abdominal pain</b>, weakness; dehydration — weight loss, dry skin, <b>sunken soft eyeballs</b>, poor turgor, oliguria with high specific gravity, weak rapid pulse, <b>flat neck veins</b>, postural hypotension. <b>Kussmaul respirations</b>; mental status from alert to coma. <b>Confusion or Kussmaul breathing → shock and coma can come quickly.</b></div>\n<div><b>Labs (Table 56.9)</b> — glucose <b>&gt; 300</b>; serum ketones <b>positive</b>; <b>pH &lt; 7.35</b>; <b>HCO₃⁻ &lt; 15</b>; sodium low, normal or high; <b>BUN &gt; 30 and creatinine &gt; 1.5</b> from dehydration; urine ketones positive; potassium may start <b>high</b> (shift) despite total-body loss.</div>\n<div><b>Monitoring</b> — first: <b>airway, LOC, hydration, electrolytes, glucose</b>. <b>BP, pulse and respirations every 15 minutes until stable</b>; <b>urine output, temperature and mental status hourly</b>; CVP every 30 minutes if present; then vital signs every 4 hours once stable. Glucose results decide when to switch from saline to dextrose.</div>\n<div><b>Fluids</b> — <b>first goal: restore blood volume</b> — about <b>15–20 mL/kg in the first hour</b>. Then <b>hypotonic</b> fluid at 4–14 mL/kg/hr. <b>When glucose reaches 250 → D5 in 0.45% saline</b>, to prevent <b>hypoglycemia</b> and <b>cerebral edema</b> from osmolarity falling too fast. Up to <b>6–10 L in 24 hours</b>; watch for <b>fluid overload</b> (heart failure, pulmonary edema) via BP, I&amp;O and daily weight.</div>\n<div><b>Insulin</b> — <b>regular insulin by continuous IV infusion</b> after an IV bolus (unless DKA is mild) — short half-life and slow subcutaneous onset make IV necessary. Aim to lower glucose about <b>50–75 mg/dL per hour</b>. Switch to <b>subcutaneous</b> when the patient takes oral fluids and <b>ketosis has stopped</b>. <b>Resolved</b> when glucose <b>&lt; 200</b>, bicarbonate <b>&gt; 18</b>, venous pH <b>&gt; 7.30</b>, anion gap <b>&lt; 12</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Potassium and bicarbonate</b> — insulin, fluids and correcting acidosis <b>drive potassium down</b>: start replacement once K⁺ <b>falls below 5.0</b>. <b>Action alert: urine output at least 30 mL/hr before giving IV potassium.</b> Watch for <b>hypokalemia</b> — fatigue, confusion, muscle weakness, <b>shallow breathing</b>, abdominal distention or ileus, hypotension, weak pulse, <b>ECG changes</b>. <b>Bicarbonate only for severe acidosis</b> (pH <b>≤ 7.0</b> or HCO₃⁻ <b>&lt; 5</b>), by <b>slow IV infusion over hours</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hyperglycemic-hyperosmolar state (HHS)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — extreme hyperglycemia and <b>dehydration</b> without significant ketosis: glucose <b>&gt; 600</b>, osmolarity <b>&gt; 320</b>; <b>ketones negative</b>; pH <b>&gt; 7.4</b>; HCO₃⁻ <b>&gt; 20</b>. Enough insulin remains to <b>prevent ketosis</b> but not hyperglycemia, so glucose climbs higher than in DKA and the osmotic diuresis is extreme. Onset is <b>gradual</b>; mostly <b>older adults with type 2</b>, many undiagnosed; mortality up to <b>16%</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Why older adults</b> — blunted <b>thirst</b>, poor <b>urine concentration</b> and <b>diuretics</b> → assess <b>all</b> older adults for dehydration, diabetic or not. Triggers: <b>infection</b>, poor fluid intake, <b>MI, sepsis, pancreatitis, stroke</b>, and drugs — <b>glucocorticoids, diuretics, phenytoin, beta blockers, calcium channel blockers</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The brain</b> — confusion to coma, <b>seizures</b>, reversible paralysis; <b>coma once osmolarity &gt; 350</b> (normal <b>270–300 mOsm/L</b>). Brain recovery lags hours behind normal glucose.</div>\n<div><b>Fluids first</b> — goal: rehydrate and normalize glucose over <b>36–72 hours</b>. <b>Normal saline in shock or severe hypotension; otherwise half-normal saline</b>, at <b>1 L/hr</b> until CVP rises or BP and urine output are adequate, then <b>100–200 mL/hr</b>; <b>half the deficit in the first 12 hours</b>, the rest over the next 36. Check for <b>cerebral edema hourly</b> (abrupt mental change, abnormal neuro signs, coma). No improvement = too little fluid or osmolarity not falling; <b>regression after improvement = osmolarity falling too fast</b>. <b>Slow, steady neurologic improvement</b> = fluids are right.</div>\n<div><b>Critical rescue and insulin</b> — changes in <b>LOC, pupils</b> (size, shape, reaction) or <b>seizures → notify the provider immediately</b>. <b>IV insulin only after adequate fluids</b>: bolus, then infusion until glucose reaches <b>250</b>, lowering <b>50–70 mg/dL per hour</b>. Potassium drops quickly → <b>electrolytes every 1–2 hours</b> and <b>continuous cardiac monitoring</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Sick days and prevention (Box 56.12)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Sick-day rules</b> — <b>tell the provider you are ill</b>; check glucose <b>every 2–4 hours</b>; <b>test for ketones even if glucose is in range</b>, especially when vomiting; <b>keep taking insulin or antidiabetic drugs</b> unless told otherwise — <b>never omit insulin</b>; <b>8–12 oz (240–360 mL) of sugar-free, caffeine-free fluid every hour awake</b> (sugar-containing fluids if glucose is below target); keep regular meal times; if solids won't stay down, replace the meal's carbohydrate with easier foods or liquids (<b>at least 150 g of carbohydrate a day</b> prevents starvation ketosis); rest.</div>\n<div><b>When to call</b> — <b>persistent nausea and vomiting</b>; can't keep food or fluids down; <b>moderate or large ketones</b>; glucose <b>&gt; 250 not responding</b> or still high after <b>two supplemental insulin doses</b>; persistent hypoglycemia; temperature <b>101.5°F (38.6°C)</b> or rising, or fever <b>&gt; 24 hours</b>; illness <b>longer than 1–2 days</b>.</div>\n<div><b>DKA teaching</b> — check glucose <b>every 2 hours</b> while anorexia, nausea or vomiting continue or glucose stays <b>&gt; 250</b>; check <b>ketones when glucose &gt; 250</b>; drink at least <b>2 L a day</b>, more with infection; with nausea, sip liquids with <b>glucose and electrolytes</b> (regular soda, diluted juice, sports drinks); take extra rapid or regular insulin as the provider directs.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Both emergencies are dehydration emergencies first, which is why <b>fluids come before insulin</b>: insulin pulls glucose — and water with it — into the cells, and given to a patient with an empty circulation it can collapse the blood pressure. The two differ in one thing: in DKA there is no insulin at all, so fat is burned and ketoacids form; in HHS a little insulin stops the ketones but not the climbing glucose, so the danger shifts to the <b>brain</b> as osmolarity soars.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-foot-care-kidneys-and-preventing-injury","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Foot care, kidneys and preventing injury","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The diabetic foot</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why feet are lost</b> — most amputations follow a <b>foot ulcer</b>, and 5-year mortality after amputation is high. <b>Neuropathy is the main cause of ulcers; poor perfusion is the main cause of poor healing.</b> Motor neuropathy → <b>claw toes</b>, thinned fat pad under the <b>metatarsal heads</b>, pressure points → calluses → ulcers. <b>Charcot foot</b>: warm, swollen, painful; the arch collapses into a <b>\"rocker bottom\"</b>. Autonomic → dry, cracked skin. Sensory → numbness, injuries unnoticed. <b>PAD</b> slows healing.</div>\n<div><b>How injuries happen</b> — <b>walking barefoot</b>, <b>ill-fitting shoes</b>, <b>heat</b> (hot water bottles, heating pads, hot baths) and <b>chemical burns</b> from OTC corn removers. Pressure ulcers: <b>sole and ball</b> from walking, <b>top and sides</b> from shoes; typically at the <b>great toe, under the metatarsal heads, and on tops of claw toes</b>. Infection is common, hard to treat, and raises glucose further.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — neuropathy, <b>male sex</b>, poor glucose control, cardiovascular, eye or kidney complications; poor gait, calluses, deformities, limited joint motion, severe nail problems, PAD, and <b>any prior ulcer or amputation</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Assessment (Box 56.8)</b> — history of ulcer or amputation; <b>dry, cracked skin</b>, ulcers, thick or ingrown nails, tinea pedis, fungal nails; circulation — <b>claudication</b>, <b>dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses</b>, prolonged capillary refill (the box prints \"greater than 25 seconds\", which looks like a misprint — normal refill is under about 3 seconds <i>(not in this chapter)</i>), <b>hair loss on the top of the foot</b>; deformity — calluses, corns, prominent metatarsal heads, <b>claw or hammertoes</b>, bunions, Charcot foot; strength — ankle and great-toe motion; sensation — numbness, burning, tingling and <b>Semmes-Weinstein monofilament at 10 points on each foot</b>. Professional foot exam <b>at least yearly</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Monofilament and risk categories (Table 56.7)</b> — a <b>10-g</b> nylon monofilament: <b>can't feel it anywhere = high ulcer risk</b>. Category 0 (sensation intact, no PAD or deformity): <b>yearly exam</b>, footwear teaching. Category 1 (no protective sensation ± deformity): <b>every 3–6 months</b>, consider a specialist. Categories 2–3 (no sensation <b>plus PAD</b>, or <b>prior ulcer/amputation</b>): <b>every 1–3 months</b>, specialist, <b>prescription footwear</b>, vascular consult.</div>\n<div><b>Footwear</b> — fitted by an experienced fitter (podiatrist): <b>½–⅝ inch longer than the longest toe</b>, <b>heels under 2 inches</b>; <b>change shoes at midday and again in the evening</b>. Soft, seamless, padded, moisture-absorbing socks — <b>white</b>, so blood or drainage shows. No tight stockings or constricting bands. Toe deformities → <b>custom shoes with high, wide toe boxes</b>; break new shoes in slowly with frequent checks.</div>\n<div><b>Teaching (Box 56.9)</b> — <b>inspect feet daily</b>, including <b>between the toes</b> (mirror on the floor for the soles); wash daily in <b>lukewarm</b> water and <b>dry thoroughly</b>; moisturize — <b>but not between the toes</b>; clean cotton socks daily; <b>alternate shoes</b> day to day, breathable leather or cloth; <b>check shoes for pebbles</b> and torn linings; buy shoes <b>later in the day</b>; <b>socks for warmth</b>; <b>cut nails straight across</b> and file; <b>check bath water with the wrist or a thermometer</b>; never hot-water bottles, heating pads or heaters; <b>don't self-treat corns, calluses, blisters or ingrown nails</b>; <b>never barefoot</b>; no open-toed sandals or thong straps; <b>don't cross legs</b>; see the provider <b>at once</b> for blisters, sores or infection (dry sterile dressing, <b>no tape on skin</b>). First check that the patient can see and reach every part of the foot; if not, <b>teach a family member</b> to inspect and care for it.</div>\n<div><b>Wound care</b> — standards: a <b>moist wound environment</b>, <b>débridement</b> of dead tissue, and <b>offloading</b> — <b>no shoe on the affected foot</b>; total-contact casts, half shoes, cast walkers, wheelchairs, crutches. Patients without sensation keep walking on painless ulcers, enlarging them. <b>Ulcers recur unless weight is permanently redistributed.</b></div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Protecting the kidneys</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Screening and targets</b> — <b>yearly urine albumin</b>: type 1 after <b>5 years</b>, type 2 <b>from diagnosis</b>. <b>Persistent albuminuria 30–299 mg/24 h</b> = earliest nephropathy (type 1) and a marker in type 2. Keep <b>BP below 140/80</b> (the kidney section's target; the cardiovascular section gives &lt; 140/90); control glucose and cholesterol; <b>stop smoking</b>. Once albuminuria appears, the focus is BP, glucose and <b>avoiding nephrotoxic drugs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>ACE inhibitors and ARBs</b> — for <b>persistent albuminuria</b> or advanced nephropathy they <b>slow progression and lower albuminuria</b> — but are <b>not</b> given just to \"protect\" the kidney when BP and albumin are normal. <b>Monitor potassium (hyperkalemia).</b></div>\n<div><b>Nephrotoxins</b> — the most common: <b>antifungals</b> and <b>aminoglycoside antibiotics</b>; also <b>NSAIDs</b> (ibuprofen, naproxen). Check with the provider or pharmacist before OTC drugs or herbs. <b>IV contrast</b> → monitor <b>IV hydration before and after</b> to prevent contrast nephropathy.</div>\n<div><b>Dialysis</b> — the same as for anyone, but <b>reduce insulin</b> when dialysis starts — <b>hypoglycemia</b> risk rises.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Kidney damage and insulin interact in a way that surprises students: the kidney also <b>clears insulin</b>, so as kidney function falls, each dose lasts longer and hypoglycemia becomes more common — the \"improving\" glucose in a patient with worsening kidneys is often a warning, not a success.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-diabetes-in-the-hospital-and-around-surg","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Diabetes in the hospital and around surgery","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Glucose targets in the hospital</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why glucose rises</b> — illness, less activity, <b>held antidiabetic drugs</b>, <b>corticosteroids or antipsychotics</b>, changed nutrition. Inpatient hyperglycemia means <b>more infections, longer stays, more ICU care and higher mortality</b>; admission glucose <b>&gt; 198</b> predicts worse outcomes. <b>Hypoglycemia</b> also raises mortality.</div>\n<div><b>Targets</b> — <b>critically ill: 140–180</b> (continuous <b>IV insulin drip</b> works best in ICU). <b>Non-critical: before meals &lt; 140, random &lt; 180</b>, with scheduled <b>basal + mealtime + correction</b> subcutaneous insulin. <b>Review the regimen below 100; change it below 70.</b></div>\n<div><b>NPO patients</b> — give <b>basal</b> insulin (often at a <b>reduced</b> dose) — it controls baseline glucose. <b>Hold rapid- and short-acting insulin, amylin analogs and incretin mimetics</b> (they cause lows without food). <b>Don't give premixed insulins</b> (they contain rapid or short insulin).</div>\n<div><b>Unexpected rise = look for infection</b> — balancing hormones often raise glucose <b>before a fever appears</b>. When a patient's controlled glucose <b>rises unexpectedly</b>, check for <b>infection</b> — UTI, pneumonia, cellulitis, wound infection.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why it is riskier</b> — anesthesia and surgery release <b>counterregulatory hormones</b> → hyperglycemia and <b>ketoacidosis</b> risk; <b>HHS</b> after surgery carries high mortality; diuresis → dehydration and <b>acute kidney injury</b>. Diabetes adds hypertension, ischemic heart disease, stroke, <b>MI</b> and cardiomyopathy risk; <b>urinary retention</b>; <b>gastroparesis and reflux (aspiration with anesthesia)</b>; <b>paralytic ileus</b> from autonomic neuropathy.</div>\n<div><b>Before surgery</b> — <b>sulfonylureas stopped 1 day before</b>; <b>metformin stopped at least 24 hours before</b> and restarted only when <b>kidney function is normal</b>; <b>other oral drugs stopped the day of surgery</b>; long-acting insulin may be switched to <b>intermediate-acting 1–2 days before</b>. Pre-op glucose <b>&lt; 200</b> (higher = more infection and poor healing).</div>\n<div><b>During surgery</b> — IV <b>insulin, glucose and potassium</b> with <b>hourly</b> capillary glucose; target <b>140–180</b>; about <b>5 g glucose per hour</b> to prevent hypoglycemia, ketosis and protein breakdown; stress may raise insulin needs.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">After surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Insulin and analgesia</b> — continue glucose and insulin infusions until stable and eating; short-term insulin may be needed even for patients on oral agents. <b>Opioids slow the gut and change glucose</b>; in older adults they risk confusion, ileus, hypoventilation, hypotension and retention — <b>PCA</b> reduces respiratory problems and confusion.</div>\n<div><b>Monitoring</b> — autonomic neuropathy or vascular disease → watch for <b>hypotension and respiratory arrest</b>. <b>Beta blockers mask hypoglycemia</b> → monitor closely. <b>Hyperkalemia</b> in kidney failure; <b>hypokalemia</b> worsened by intraoperative insulin and glucose → <b>cardiac rhythm and K⁺</b>. <b>Continuous ECG</b> for older patients, long-standing type 1 and heart disease — ECG or K⁺ changes may be a <b>silent MI</b>. Fluid balance for <b>acute kidney injury</b> (hydrate well with nephrotoxic antibiotics); raised blood protein or nitrogen makes fluid management harder — check central venous or pulmonary artery pressure as needed.</div>\n<div><b>Post-op nutrition</b> — clear or full liquids: about <b>200 g of carbohydrate a day</b> in equal portions — <b>not sugar free</b>. <b>25–35 kcal/kg/day</b>. Advance to solids as fast as tolerated; once eating, at least <b>150–200 g of carbohydrate daily</b> to prevent lows. <b>TPN</b> can cause severe hyperglycemia → glucose at least <b>every 6 hours</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Pancreas transplant</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Who and what</b> — the most common surgical treatment: success means <b>no insulin, no glucose monitoring and few diet limits</b>, but long-term complications only partly reverse. <b>Lifelong antirejection drugs</b> (some raise glucose; infection, cancer and atherosclerosis risk) limit it to serious progressive complications or uncontrollable acute ones; often <b>with a kidney transplant</b> (pancreas alone, after kidney, or simultaneous) — the pancreas graft survives better when placed <b>at the same time as the kidney</b>. 1-year survival <b>&gt; 95%</b>; <b>HLA tissue matching</b> affects results.</div>\n<div><b>The operation</b> — a cadaver pancreas is placed in the <b>pelvis</b>; the native pancreas stays. Its <b>800–1000 mL a day</b> of fluid drains to the <b>bladder or bowel</b> — bladder drainage irritates and loses <b>bicarbonate (acidosis)</b>. Antiviral, antibacterial and antifungal prophylaxis first; adjust insulin if steroids are used.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Surgery is a controlled injury, and injury triggers the same stress hormones as illness — cortisol, epinephrine, glucagon — all of which raise glucose and blunt insulin. That is why insulin needs go <b>up</b> around surgery even while the patient eats <b>nothing</b>, and why the oral drugs that either force insulin out (sulfonylureas, low risk without food) or depend on healthy kidneys (metformin) are stopped first.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-teaching-self-management","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Teaching self-management","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Teaching self-management</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Assess before teaching (Box 56.13)</b> — history, diet, activity, drugs and supplements; <b>literacy, numeracy</b>, motivation and health beliefs; current self-management and past training; <b>age, mobility, vision, hearing, dexterity, attention</b>; psychosocial support and <b>mental health</b>; substance use; work, money, culture, religion; <b>access to care</b>; language (use a <b>medical interpreter</b>). If not ready to learn, ask permission to teach a <b>family member</b>, and leave written materials and a number to call.</div>\n<div><b>Physical limits</b> — check that they can <b>read insulin labels and syringe markings</b> (vision worsens with fluctuating glucose); materials in the <b>preferred language</b>, pictographs if helpful. Hand injury, <b>tremor or arthritis</b> → dosing errors with syringes → consider <b>pens</b> or other preparations; meters with <b>prefilled strip drums</b>.</div>\n<div><b>How to teach</b> — written or picture handouts, step-by-step discussion, <b>hands-on</b> practice, and <b>teach-back</b>. In acute illness teach only <b>survival skills</b>; save detail for follow-up. Break skills into <b>small achievable tasks</b> (first just drawing up an accurate dose) to build <b>self-efficacy</b> — mastering injection reduces fear.</div>\n<div><b>Survival skills</b> — simple pathophysiology; <b>how to take insulin or the drug</b>; <b>recognize, treat and prevent hypo- and hyperglycemia</b>; basic diet; <b>glucose and ketone monitoring</b>; <b>sick-day rules</b>; where to buy and <b>how to store</b> supplies; <b>when to call</b> the provider.</div>\n<div><b>In-depth education</b> — explain insulin action, how <b>diet, drugs and activity</b> move glucose, and why normal glucose prevents complications; <b>demonstrate injection</b>; state the insulin's <b>onset, peak and duration</b>, when, where and how it is stored; insulin-to-carb ratios and correction factors if supported; <b>dangers of skipping doses</b> and resources if unaffordable; drug interactions (especially older adults). Consistent carbs or carb counting; include whoever <b>buys and cooks</b> the food. SMBG and how to act on it. Sick-day rules <b>from diagnosis</b> — hyperglycemia often appears <b>before</b> infection symptoms.</div>\n<div><b>Living with it</b> — diagnosis can feel like a <b>loss of control and flexibility</b>; technology helps, but routines remain. Adherence follows strategies that <b>make sense and seem to work</b>; SMBG shows the patient their own cause and effect.</div>\n<div><b>The team (Interprofessional Collaboration)</b> — diabetes is diagnosed and managed mostly in the <b>clinic and community</b>, so teaching happens at every encounter. The team: primary health care provider, <b>endocrinologist</b>, diabetes-specialist advanced practice nurse, physician associate, <b>certified diabetes care and education specialist (CDCES)</b>, <b>ophthalmologist</b>, registered nurse, <b>pharmacist</b>, <b>registered dietitian nutritionist (RDN)</b>, <b>podiatrist</b>, physical therapist and <b>wound care specialist</b>; social workers and psychologists also teach.</div>\n<div><b>Home care (Box 56.14) and resources</b> — mental status; vital signs and weight (<b>fever = infection?</b>); vision; mouth, gums and teeth; <b>injection sites and rotation</b>; skin and old wounds; <b>foot care and feet</b> (pulses, sensation, skin, nails); bowels; the glucose diary; meter technique; insulin storage, preparation, injection and sharps disposal; drug knowledge. Older adults living alone need <b>daily phone contact</b>, help with shopping, meals, transportation and drawing up insulin; refer to home care or public health.</div>\n<div><b>Ready for home (Box 56.15)</b> — the patient (or significant other) can tell <b>why</b> the drug is prescribed, <b>name, dose and frequency</b>, how meals relate to its action, the diet plan; <b>prepare and inject insulin accurately</b>; test glucose and <b>urine ketones</b> (and when); <b>store insulin</b>; list <b>hypoglycemia symptoms</b> and the <b>carbs that treat them</b>; list <b>hyperglycemia symptoms</b>; diet changes when <b>ill</b>; <b>when to call</b> (frequent lows, signs of highs); and <b>foot care</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Diabetes is managed by the patient roughly 99% of the time — every meal, every dose, every walk is a decision made at home. Teaching is therefore not a discharge task but the main treatment, and the most important thing to check is not what the patient heard but what they can <b>do</b>: draw up the dose, read the meter, treat a low.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-adpie","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Diabetes care runs on two clocks at once. The fast clock is the emergencies — <b>hypoglycemia, DKA, HHS</b> — where minutes and the right first step matter. The slow clock is the years of vessel damage — <b>eyes, kidneys, nerves, feet, heart</b> — where daily habits matter. Every plan protects against both, and puts the patient in charge of the slow one.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>risk factors, <b>family history</b>, weight change, <b>3 Ps</b>, fatigue; infections — <b>vaginal yeast</b>, skin injuries that <b>heal slowly</b>; vision and touch changes; women: <b>large babies or gestational diabetes</b>; drugs (steroids, antipsychotics), alcohol, smoking; current regimen, meal pattern, activity, monitoring habits, past lows and highs; <b>cost</b> and other barriers.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div>vital signs with <b>orthostatics</b> (autonomic neuropathy); weight and BMI, <b>waist</b>; skin and <b>injection sites</b>; <b>full foot exam</b> (pulses, monofilament, skin, nails, deformity); vision; mouth and gums; hydration (turgor, mucous membranes); breathing pattern and <b>breath odor</b> (Kussmaul, fruity); mental status; bowel sounds.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Labs</div><b>glucose</b> (fingerstick, lab, CGM trend), <b>A1C</b>, <b>ketones</b> (urine or blood), electrolytes with <b>potassium</b>, <b>pH and bicarbonate</b>, anion gap, <b>osmolarity</b>, BUN and creatinine, <b>urine albumin</b>, lipids; ECG when potassium shifts.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Ask about the <b>slow-healing wound</b> and the <b>yeast infection</b> on purpose: in type 2 they are often the first and only signs, years before thirst or weight loss — and they are things patients rarely connect to their blood sugar.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>1. <b>Potential for injury from hyperglycemia</b>. 2. <b>Potential for surgical complications</b>. 3. <b>Potential for injury from peripheral neuropathy</b>. 4. <b>Potential for kidney disease</b> from reduced kidney perfusion. 5. <b>Potential for acute complications</b> — hypoglycemia, DKA, HHS.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Prioritizing in the moment</div>a <b>conscious patient with glucose &lt; 70</b> comes before almost anything — treat first, document after. In DKA and HHS: <b>airway → fluids → insulin → potassium</b>. Unexpected glucose rise in a controlled patient → think <b>infection</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>glucose stays <b>in the target range</b> (A1C <b>&lt; 7%</b>, pre-meal 80–130, peak post-meal &lt; 180; in hospital &lt; 140 before meals and &lt; 180 random; ICU 140–180) <b>without significant lows</b>; recovery from surgery without complications; <b>intact skin on the feet</b>; optimal kidney function and urine output; no DKA, HHS or severe hypoglycemia; the patient meets the <b>Box 56.15</b> teaching criteria.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Glucose control</div>monitoring before meals and bedtime and with symptoms; give <b>rapid-acting insulin just before meals</b> after a check <b>within 30 minutes</b>; correct technique, sites and storage; <b>basal insulin when NPO</b>, hold mealtime insulin; reinforce the RDN's plan, carb counting or MyPlate; <b>activity rules</b> (ketones → no exercise; 100–250 to start); teach <b>sick-day rules</b> and <b>alcohol with food</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Emergencies</div><b>hypoglycemia</b>: 15-15 rule (30 g if &lt; 50), <b>glucagon or IV dextrose</b> if unable to swallow, side-lying, recheck every 15 minutes, then a complex carb with protein. <b>DKA</b>: VS every 15 minutes, hourly UO and mental status, <b>15–20 mL/kg first hour</b>, <b>IV regular insulin</b>, <b>D5½NS at 250</b>, <b>K⁺ once &lt; 5.0 with UO ≥ 30 mL/hr</b>. <b>HHS</b>: <b>fluids first</b> (NS if shocked, otherwise ½NS), hourly neuro and cerebral edema checks, insulin after fluids, electrolytes every 1–2 hours, continuous ECG.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Surgery</div><b>sulfonylureas off 1 day before, metformin ≥ 24 hours before</b> (restart after kidney function confirmed), other orals off the day of surgery; pre-op glucose <b>&lt; 200</b>; intra-op IV insulin, glucose and potassium with hourly checks (140–180); post-op <b>continuous ECG</b> when at risk, potassium, I&amp;O for AKI, <b>non-sugar-free liquids (~200 g carb/day)</b>, PCA for pain.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Feet, kidneys and teaching</div>yearly <b>monofilament</b> exam and risk category; <b>daily foot inspection</b> and Box 56.9 habits; proper shoes and white socks; <b>offloading</b> and moist wound care for ulcers. Yearly urine albumin; <b>BP &lt; 140/80</b>; ACEI/ARB for albuminuria with <b>potassium checks</b>; avoid <b>NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, antifungals</b>; <b>hydrate around contrast</b>. Teach with <b>teach-back</b>, starting with survival skills.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>glucose and A1C reach target <b>without frequent lows</b>; DKA resolution criteria met (glucose &lt; 200, HCO₃⁻ &gt; 18, pH &gt; 7.30, anion gap &lt; 12); HHS — <b>steady neurologic improvement</b>; surgery recovery without infection or AKI; <b>feet intact</b>; urine albumin stable; and the patient can <b>demonstrate</b> injection, glucose and ketone testing, treatment of a low, sick-day rules and foot care.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"}];
